window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["imhans-2021"] = {"id": "imhans-2021", "title": "IMHANS Kozhikode 2021", "fullTitle": "IMHANS Kozhikode 2021, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, booklet 21020 A)", "sub": "75 real questions · official LBS key, checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Genogram was originated by:", "options": {"a": "Franz Alexander", "b": "Murray Bowen", "c": "Master and Johnson", "d": "James Mann"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the genogram grew out of the family systems work of Murray Bowen. A genogram is a family diagram that maps at least three generations: squares for males, circles for females, horizontal lines for marriages, lines down to children, and special lines for closeness, conflict and emotional cutoff. Murray Bowen (1978), an American psychiatrist, built family systems theory in the 1950s after admitting whole families of patients with schizophrenia to a research ward. He argued that anxiety and ways of relating pass down the generations (multigenerational transmission), so the therapist must look at the wider family, not only the person with the symptom. His main ideas include differentiation of self (thinking clearly while staying emotionally connected), triangles (a tense pair pulls in a third person, often a child) and emotional cutoff. The family diagram was his tool for tracking these patterns. Example: Riya's therapist draws her genogram and notices that in three generations the eldest daughter took on the caretaking role and then cut off from her mother; the pattern, not Riya alone, becomes the focus. Option b is right because the genogram is the signature assessment tool of Bowen's transgenerational school. Exam trap: genograms are now used everywhere (case histories, social work, structural and strategic family therapy), so examiners offer other famous names from therapy; only Bowen is credited with its origin. Key note: the standard symbols and format were set out later by Monica McGoldrick and Randy Gerson in Genograms in Family Assessment (1985), so some books link the genogram with them; its origin is credited to Bowen, and b stands.", "topic": "Genogram and Bowen", "fig": "Murray Bowen"}, {"q": "The Vygosky's stage which is similar to Piaget's concentration and conservation:", "options": {"a": "Mature concept stage", "b": "Vague syncretic stage", "c": "Potential concept stage", "d": "Complex stage"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the potential concept stage is the stage of Lev Vygotsky (1934) usually set beside the centration and conservation of Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980). In Thinking and Speech (1934), Vygotsky studied concept formation with a block-sorting task refined by his colleague Leonid Sakharov. Blocks of different colours, shapes, heights and sizes carry nonsense words on their undersides, and the child must work out which blocks belong together. Vygotsky described a sequence. First come vague syncretic images, or heaps: objects lumped together by chance impression or position. Next is thinking in complexes: groups built on concrete, shifting links (a red block, then a red triangle, then any triangle), ending in the pseudo-concept, which looks like a true concept but rests on visible likeness. Then come potential concepts: the child abstracts one attribute (all the tall ones) and groups on it, but can hold only one attribute at a time. Last, in adolescence, mature (true) concepts combine abstracted attributes into a rule-based system tied to a word. Piaget's preoperational child shows centration, fixing on one striking dimension (the height of the water) and so failing conservation. Example: Arjun sorts the blocks by height alone and ignores width even when width also matters. Option c is right because grouping on a single attribute at a time is the Vygotskian parallel to centring on a single dimension. Exam trap: the complex stage contains the pseudo-concept, which looks advanced and tempts. Key note: the booklet's 'concentration' is read as Piaget's centration; the potential concept stage, where only one attribute is held at a time, is the stage usually set beside centration and the conservation tasks that expose it, though some guides pair the mature concept stage with decentration; c stands.", "topic": "Vygotsky's concept formation stages", "fig": "Lev Vygotsky"}, {"q": "The first attempt for standardization of mental tests in India was made by:", "options": {"a": "Dr. S. M. Mohsin", "b": "V. V. Kamat", "c": "Dr. C.H. Rice", "d": "Dr. Sohen Lal"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the first attempt to standardise a mental test in India was made by C. Herbert Rice (1922). Standardisation means giving a test to a large, representative sample under uniform conditions, so that norms can be built and any one score can be read against them. Intelligence testing began with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) in Paris, and Binet-type scales soon spread across the world. In India, C. Herbert Rice, working in Lahore, began adapting the Binet scale for Indian children in 1922, work that produced the Hindustani Binet Performance Point Scale. As its name says, the scale put Binet-type items into Hindustani, added performance tasks and scored the whole by points, and it was tried out on Indian school children to give local norms. That makes it the first Indian adaptation and standardisation of a Binet-type test. Later Indian work built on it. V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test), and S. M. Mohsin and Sohan Lal developed Hindi group tests of general mental ability. Example: if Meera's teacher wants to know whether Meera's score is high, she needs norms from Indian children like Meera, and Rice's scale was the first to offer them. Option c is right because Rice's work, begun in 1922, came before the work of Kamat, Mohsin and Sohan Lal. Exam trap: the Binet-Kamat test is the best known Indian Binet, so it tempts as 'the first'; it was a later revision, not the first attempt.", "topic": "First Indian test standardisation", "fig": "C. Herbert Rice"}, {"q": "According to psychodynamic theory, individuals are sometimes motivated to respond to the world through the perspectives they have learnt from important other people in their developmental past. This is called:", "options": {"a": "Object permanence theory", "b": "Distorted perspective theory", "c": "Object-relations theory", "d": "Ego defence theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: this is object relations theory. In psychodynamic language an 'object' is not a thing but a person, or part of a person, towards whom feelings and wishes are directed, usually the mother or another early caregiver. Object relations theory holds that the infant takes in (internalises) mental pictures of the self and of important others from early relationships, along with the feelings that link them. These internal objects then act as templates: later in life the person sees and responds to partners, bosses and therapists through the perspectives learnt from those early figures. The school grew in Britain after Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). Melanie Klein (1882 to 1960) described splitting of the world into good and bad objects and the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions; W. R. D. Fairbairn (1952) argued that the infant is object-seeking rather than pleasure-seeking; Donald Winnicott (1953) described the transitional object and the good-enough mother; Otto Kernberg (1975) linked split internal object relations to borderline personality organisation. Example: Kabir grew up with a harsh, critical father, and as an adult he hears criticism in every neutral remark from his manager, reacting to the internal father image rather than to the manager. Option c is right because the stem describes exactly this internalised, relationship-based template learnt from important others. Exam trap: 'object permanence' shares the word 'object' but belongs to Jean Piaget's (1896 to 1980) sensorimotor stage, where the infant learns that things go on existing when out of sight.", "topic": "Object relations theory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following test will you use to find out whether children's motivation to succeed at school is related to the size, ethnicity, and socio-economic status of their families?", "options": {"a": "Chi-square", "b": "Kruskal-Wallis test", "c": "Multiple regression", "d": "Two-way ANOVA"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: multiple regression is the test to use. Multiple regression predicts one continuous outcome (the criterion) from two or more predictors at once. It produces an equation of the form Y = a + b1X1 + b2X2 + b3X3, where each b (a regression coefficient) shows how much the outcome changes with that predictor while the others are held constant. R squared shows how much of the variance in the outcome all the predictors explain together, and a test on each coefficient shows which predictors matter. Predictors can be continuous (family size, income) or categorical once dummy-coded (ethnic groups entered as 0 and 1 codes). Here the outcome is children's motivation to succeed at school, a scale score, and there are three family predictors: size (a count), ethnicity (categorical) and socio-economic status (often a continuous index). The question asks whether motivation is related to all three, which is exactly what one regression model tests. Example: Riya collects motivation scores from 300 pupils in Kochi and enters family size, ethnicity codes and SES together; she finds that SES predicts motivation even with family size held constant. Option c is right because it is the only listed technique that relates one continuous outcome to several predictors of mixed type at once. Exam trap: the words 'related to' tempt chi-square, but chi-square works on frequencies in categories, not on a motivation score. Key note: two-way ANOVA, the option ticked on the copy, handles exactly two categorical factors; with three predictors, some of them continuous, multiple regression is the standard answer, and c stands.", "topic": "Choosing multiple regression", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The ------ can be defined as the sequence of identical occurrence of the element (numbers or symbols), preceded or followed by different occurrence of the elements or by no elements at all.", "options": {"a": "Kruskal-Wallis H", "b": "Run", "c": "Mann-Whitney U", "d": "Students t"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the definition describes a run. A run is an unbroken sequence of identical symbols, preceded and followed by a different symbol or by nothing at all (the start or end of the series). In the coin series H H T T T H there are three runs: HH, TTT and H. Counting runs is the basis of the runs test, a non-parametric test of randomness. If a sequence has too few runs, like symbols cluster together (H H H H T T T T); if it has too many, they alternate too regularly (H T H T H T); either way the order does not look random. The one-sample runs test checks whether a series of events (right and wrong answers, men and women arriving at a clinic) occurs in random order. Abraham Wald and Jacob Wolfowitz (1940) turned runs into a two-sample test: pool the scores of two groups, put them in order, label each score by its group and count the runs; few runs mean the two groups differ. Example: Kabir notes whether each patient entering the OPD is a man (M) or a woman (W): M M W W W M W W M M. Each change of letter starts a new run, so he counts 5 runs and compares that number with the table for chance. Option b is right because the stem gives the standard definition of a run, the unit the runs test counts. Exam trap: the other three options are named test statistics (H, U and t), so a student who half-remembers 'non-parametric test' may grab one; a run is a pattern in a sequence, not a test statistic.", "topic": "Runs and the runs test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An analogue observation enables:", "options": {"a": "Surreptitious observation of the client", "b": "Observations of non-clinical population", "c": "Observation of more than one client at a time", "d": "Observation that require three or more clinicians"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: analogue observation enables surreptitious observation of the client. In behavioural assessment the clinician watches behaviour directly rather than relying only on what the client reports. Naturalistic observation happens in the real setting (home, classroom, ward). Analogue observation happens in a contrived, controlled setting built to resemble the real one: a clinic playroom where a parent and child carry out a set task, or a role-played social situation in which a client must refuse a request or start a conversation. Because the setting is arranged in advance, the clinician can watch from behind a one-way mirror or through a video link, unseen and unobtrusive, which is what 'surreptitious' means. This reduces reactivity (people behaving differently because they know they are being watched) and lets behaviour be coded in a standard way. The cost is generalisation: behaviour in a clinic room may not match behaviour at home. Example: Meera's parents say she refuses every instruction, so the psychologist asks her mother to have Meera tidy toys in a playroom and records Meera's compliance through a one-way mirror. Option a is right because the controlled analogue setting is what makes hidden, unobtrusive observation of the client possible. Exam trap: the word 'analogue' also appears in analogue research, where non-clinical people stand in for patients; that is a research strategy, not an observation method. Key note: the booklet prints 'Superstitious', plainly a slip for 'surreptitious' (unseen, unobtrusive), and the option is shown corrected; b describes analogue research, a different use of the word, so a stands.", "topic": "Analogue observation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An IQ test that does not provide which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Good validity", "b": "High test-retest reliability", "c": "High internal consistency", "d": "Good predictor of behavior"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: an IQ test does not provide a good prediction of everyday behaviour. Modern intelligence tests such as the Wechsler scales (David Wechsler, 1939 onwards) and the Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916) are among the best built tools in psychology. Their full-scale IQs show high test-retest reliability, usually .90 or more, so scores stay stable over weeks and years. They show high internal consistency, because their items and subtests cohere in measuring general ability. They also have good validity for what they were built to predict: school grades and educational attainment, and to a fair degree job performance. What they do poorly is predict how a person will actually behave in daily life: social conduct, emotional adjustment, everyday adaptive behaviour, or how someone copes in a crisis. Behaviour depends on motivation, personality, emotional and social skills, culture and the situation, none of which an IQ score captures. That is why a diagnosis of intellectual disability also needs a separate measure of adaptive behaviour, such as the Vineland scales. Example: Arjun scores 125 on the WAIS, yet he struggles to keep friends and cannot manage his money; his IQ did not forecast these everyday difficulties. Option d is right because reliability, internal consistency and validity are strengths of IQ tests, while broad prediction of behaviour is their known limit. Exam trap: 'good validity' tempts because IQ tests are often criticised, but the criticism is about cultural bias and narrowness, not poor validity for academic outcomes.", "topic": "Limits of IQ tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If two variables, X and Y, have a very strong linear relationship, then:", "options": {"a": "there is evidence that X causes a change in Y", "b": "there is evidence that Y causes a change in X", "c": "there might not be any causal relationship between X and Y", "d": "None of these alternatives is correct"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: even a very strong linear relationship between X and Y might involve no causal relationship at all. Correlation measures how closely two variables move together. Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment r runs from minus 1 to plus 1, and a value near either end means the points lie close to a straight line. But r says nothing about why the variables move together. Two problems block a causal reading. The directionality problem: X may cause Y, or Y may cause X, and the correlation is the same either way. The third-variable problem: an unmeasured factor Z may drive both, so X and Y rise together with no direct link. Ice cream sales and drowning deaths correlate strongly because hot weather raises both. Some strong correlations are pure coincidence, especially when many variables are searched. Only a true experiment, in which the researcher manipulates X, controls other variables and assigns people at random, licenses a cause-and-effect conclusion. Example: Riya finds r = .85 between daily screen time and anxiety scores in college students; anxiety might drive screen use, screen use might raise anxiety, or poor sleep might cause both. Option c is right because 'there might not be any causal relationship' is the honest conclusion from correlational data. Exam trap: options a and b each pick one direction, and a strong r makes them sound convincing; the strength of an association is not evidence of causation in either direction.", "topic": "Correlation versus causation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "ANCOVA is:", "options": {"a": "Used to correct for unmeasured variables that could affect the outcome variable", "b": "A method of portioning the results of ANOVAs to investigate where difference between independent variables lie", "c": "A robust version of ANOVA", "d": "An extension of ANOVA that partials out the effect of other measured variables"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: ANCOVA is an extension of ANOVA that partials out the effect of other measured variables. Analysis of covariance, developed by Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962), combines analysis of variance with regression. Alongside the grouping variable (the IV), the researcher measures one or more continuous covariates known to affect the outcome, such as pre-test score, age or IQ. ANCOVA uses regression to remove the part of the outcome that the covariate predicts, then compares the groups on adjusted means, as if every group had the same covariate score. This does two things. It reduces error variance, so the test gains power, and it statistically adjusts for groups that started out unequal on the covariate. It cannot replace random assignment, and it adjusts only for variables that were actually measured and entered. Example: Kabir compares three teaching methods on final maths scores, but the three classes began with different maths ability; with the pre-test score as the covariate, ANCOVA compares the methods on adjusted final scores. Option d is right because partialling out the effect of a measured covariate is the defining feature of ANCOVA. Exam trap: option a sounds almost the same but says 'unmeasured'; ANCOVA can remove only the influence of variables entered as covariates, and unmeasured confounds are exactly what it cannot fix.", "topic": "ANCOVA defined", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The assumption which does ANCOVA have and ANOVA does not have:", "options": {"a": "Homogeneity of variance", "b": "Homoscedasticity", "c": "Homogeneity of sample", "d": "Homogeneity of regression slopes"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: homogeneity of regression slopes is the assumption ANCOVA has and ANOVA does not. ANCOVA, developed by Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962), adjusts group means for a covariate by fitting a regression of the outcome on the covariate and using one pooled slope for all groups. Pooling only makes sense if the covariate relates to the outcome in the same way in every group, so the groups' regression lines must be parallel. If the slopes differ, there is a group by covariate interaction: the effect of the treatment depends on the level of the covariate, and a single adjustment cannot fit every group. Researchers check this by adding the group x covariate interaction term to the model; a non-significant interaction supports parallel slopes. ANCOVA also keeps all of ANOVA's assumptions (normality, independence, homogeneity of variance) and adds a linear covariate-outcome relationship and a reliably measured covariate, ideally recorded before treatment. Example: Meera uses ANCOVA, with baseline anxiety as the covariate, to compare yoga, CBT and a waitlist. If baseline anxiety predicted final anxiety strongly in the CBT group but only weakly in the yoga group, the slopes would be unequal and the adjusted means misleading. Option d is right because only ANCOVA involves regression slopes at all, so this assumption cannot belong to plain ANOVA. Exam trap: homogeneity of variance is the best known ANOVA assumption and tempts, but ANCOVA shares it rather than adding it.", "topic": "ANCOVA assumptions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A Latin Square in which the treatments in the first row and in the first column are arranged in alphabetical order or numerical order.", "options": {"a": "Simple Latin square", "b": "A standard Latin square", "c": "Partial Latin square", "d": "Double Latin square"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a Latin square whose first row and first column are in natural (alphabetical or numerical) order is a standard Latin square. A Latin square is an n x n arrangement of n treatments in which each treatment appears exactly once in every row and exactly once in every column. Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962) brought it into agricultural field trials, where rows and columns controlled two directions of soil variation. In psychology the rows are often participants or groups and the columns the order of testing, so the square controls two nuisance variables at once and balances order effects. Many different squares of a given size exist. To list and choose among them, statisticians use the standard form (also called reduced or normalised), in which both the first row and the first column read A B C D, or 1 2 3 4. Any Latin square can be turned into a standard one by reordering its rows and columns, and in practice the chosen square is then randomised by shuffling rows, columns and treatment labels. A 4 x 4 standard square: row 1 A B C D, row 2 B C D A, row 3 C D A B, row 4 D A B C; the first row and the first column are both in order. Example: Arjun tests four memory strategies on four participants across four sessions and starts from this standard square before randomising it. Option b is right because 'standard' is the technical name for exactly this ordered form. Exam trap: 'simple' sounds like a plain, basic square, but it is not the technical term for the ordered form.", "topic": "Standard Latin square", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In LSD the number of treatment, rows and columns are:", "options": {"a": "Seldom equal", "b": "Usually, equal", "c": "Equal", "d": "Unequal"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: in a Latin square design (LSD) the number of treatments, rows and columns are equal. A Latin square is an n x n grid: n rows, n columns and n treatments, with each treatment appearing exactly once in each row and once in each column. This balance is what lets the design control two nuisance variables at the same time. Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962) developed it for crop trials in which the rows and columns of a field differed in fertility. In psychology the rows usually stand for participants or groups and the columns for the order of testing; every condition then appears once in each position, which counterbalances practice and fatigue with only n orders instead of every possible order (4 conditions need 4 orders, not 24). If the numbers were unequal, some treatment would have to appear twice in a row or be missing from a column, and the design would no longer be a Latin square. Example: Kabir compares three relaxation methods (A, B, C) with three participants over three sessions; the square A B C, B C A, C A B has 3 treatments, 3 rows and 3 columns. Option c is right because equal numbers of treatments, rows and columns are part of the definition, not a habit. Exam trap: 'usually equal' sounds careful and scholarly, but there is no 'usually' about it; a layout with unequal numbers is a different design, such as a Youden square or an incomplete block design.", "topic": "Latin square structure", "fig": ""}, {"q": "------ research is based on naturalism.", "options": {"a": "Field research", "b": "Descriptive research", "c": "Basic research", "d": "Applied research"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: field research is based on naturalism. Naturalism is the principle that behaviour should be studied in its natural setting, as it ordinarily happens, with as little interference from the researcher as possible. Field research takes the researcher out of the laboratory into homes, schools, factories, wards and streets. Its forms range from naturalistic observation (watching children in a playground) and ethnography (living with a community to understand it from the inside) to field studies and field experiments. Research methods texts separate field studies, which manipulate nothing and study relationships as they exist in real social settings, from field experiments, which manipulate a variable in a real setting. The strength of this naturalism is ecological and external validity: the findings reflect real life. The cost is weaker control over extraneous variables, so causal conclusions are harder to draw. Example: Meera wants to understand how patients spend their day in a rehabilitation centre in Kozhikode; instead of handing out a questionnaire in her office, she spends weeks on the ward, observing and taking notes without changing any routine. Option a is right because studying people in their natural environment, without artificial manipulation, is what defines field research. Exam trap: descriptive research also describes behaviour as it is, so it tempts; but 'descriptive' is defined by its aim (describing what exists) in any setting, while naturalism is the setting principle of field research.", "topic": "Field research and naturalism", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following statements are true? 1. Classical conditioning regulates reflexive, involuntary responses exclusively. 2. Classical conditioning can increase sexual arousal. 3. Operant conditioning regulates voluntary responses exclusively. 4. The distinction between the two types of conditioning is not absolute.", "options": {"a": "1, 2 and 3 only", "b": "3 and 4 only", "c": "2 and 4 only", "d": "1, 3 and 4 only"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: only statements 2 and 4 are true. Classical conditioning, studied by Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936), pairs a neutral stimulus with one that already triggers a response, until the neutral stimulus triggers it too. Operant conditioning, studied by Edward Thorndike (1898) and B. F. Skinner (1938), changes behaviour through its consequences. Standard accounts say classical conditioning mainly governs reflexive, involuntary responses (salivation, fear, heart rate) and operant conditioning mainly governs voluntary ones (pressing a lever, studying). Statements 1 and 3 fail on the word 'exclusively'. In autoshaping, a pigeon's key-peck, a skeletal response, is produced by classical pairing of a key light with food. In biofeedback, autonomic responses such as heart rate and skin temperature are changed by operant reinforcement, following Neal Miller's (1969) claim that visceral responses can be operantly conditioned. So statement 4 is true: the distinction is not absolute, and the two processes interact in everyday learning. Statement 2 is true as well: sexual arousal can be classically conditioned. Stanley Rachman (1966) conditioned mild sexual arousal in men to a picture of boots by pairing it with erotic slides, and Michael Domjan's work in the 1980s showed conditioned sexual responses in male quail to a cue that signalled a female. Example: Arjun learns to feel his heart race (classical) when he sees his strict teacher, and then starts avoiding her corridor (operant) because avoiding her brings relief. Option c is right because it keeps the two true statements and drops the two 'exclusively' claims. Exam trap: 'exclusively' turns a broadly true generalisation into a false statement, so read absolute words carefully. Key note: LBS's first key for this paper (29 Nov 2021) marked c, but its revised key (6 Dec 2021) changed it to a, statements 1, 2 and 3. The revised key is wrong: statements 1 and 3 say 'exclusively', which autoshaping and biofeedback disprove, and a leaves out statement 4, the textbook line that the distinction is not absolute, so c stays.", "topic": "Classical versus operant conditioning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The region of the brain which is not implicated in OCD?", "options": {"a": "Caudate nuclei", "b": "Orbitofrontal cortex", "c": "Basal ganglia", "d": "Pons"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the pons is not implicated in OCD. Obsessive-compulsive disorder is linked to the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) loop. In this circuit the orbitofrontal cortex and the anterior cingulate cortex raise a sense that something is wrong or unfinished; the signal passes to the striatum, chiefly the caudate nucleus, which is part of the basal ganglia and normally filters such signals; the thalamus then sends it back to the cortex. In OCD the loop seems to run too hot, so worry signals keep cycling and the person feels driven to check or wash. Evidence came from PET studies in the late 1980s by Lewis Baxter and colleagues, which showed overactivity in the orbitofrontal cortex and caudate of OCD patients, and later work by the same group found that caudate activity fell after successful treatment with either medication or behaviour therapy. Surgery for severe, treatment-resistant cases (cingulotomy, capsulotomy, deep brain stimulation) targets parts of the same circuit. The pons, by contrast, sits in the brainstem between the midbrain and the medulla. It helps control breathing, triggers REM sleep and relays signals from the cortex to the cerebellum; it is not part of the OCD loop. Example: Kabir checks the gas knob twenty times before leaving home; a scan of a patient like him would show the frontal and caudate parts of the loop working overtime, not the pons. Option d is right because the pons is the only listed structure outside the CSTC circuit. Exam trap: 'caudate nuclei' and 'basal ganglia' look like two separate answers, but the caudate is part of the basal ganglia, so both are implicated.", "topic": "OCD brain circuit", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In a classroom, students are self-motivated to do homework where the teacher stopped nagging students about late homework, is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Positive reinforcement", "b": "Extrinsic motivation", "c": "Negative reinforcement", "d": "Blocking"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: this is an example of negative reinforcement. In the operant conditioning of B. F. Skinner (1938), reinforcement always increases a behaviour and punishment always decreases it. 'Positive' means something is added and 'negative' means something is taken away. So negative reinforcement is the removal of an unpleasant (aversive) stimulus when a behaviour occurs, which makes that behaviour more likely in future. In the stem the teacher's nagging is the aversive stimulus. When students hand in homework on time, the nagging stops; because doing homework removes the nagging, homework becomes more frequent. This is escape and avoidance learning, the same mechanism that makes a driver fasten the seat belt to stop the car's beeping. Example: Kabir's mother scolds him every evening until he tidies his room; once he tidies it the scolding ends, so he starts tidying sooner each day. Option c is right because the behaviour increases and the consequence is the removal of an aversive event. Exam trap: students often read 'negative' as 'bad' and treat negative reinforcement as punishment, but punishment weakens behaviour while negative reinforcement strengthens it. Key note: 'self-motivated' in the stem may tempt intrinsic or extrinsic motivation, but the operant mechanism described (behaviour strengthened because the nagging stops) is negative reinforcement, which is not punishment; b is a broad label and every reinforcement contingency is extrinsic, so c stands as the specific answer.", "topic": "Negative reinforcement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When the client is repeatedly tapping their foot on the ground, the therapist would get them to exaggerate the movement and talk about the feelings that come up. This therapeutic technique is most likely related to:", "options": {"a": "Gestalt therapy", "b": "Client centered therapy", "c": "Mindfulness", "d": "DBT"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: exaggerating a movement and talking about the feelings it brings up is a Gestalt therapy technique. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970), Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; its founding book, Gestalt Therapy (1951), was written by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman. It holds that people get stuck when they lose awareness of parts of their own experience, and that growth comes from full awareness of what one is feeling and doing in the here and now. Body language is treated as a message: a tapping foot, a clenched fist or a soft voice may carry a feeling the client is not admitting in words. In the exaggeration exercise the therapist asks the client to repeat and enlarge the gesture until its meaning becomes clear, then to put the feeling into words ('I am impatient, I want to leave'). Other Gestalt methods include the empty chair (speaking to an absent person or a part of oneself), the dialogue between 'top dog' and 'underdog', and 'I take responsibility' statements. Example: Riya keeps tapping her foot while describing her mother; asked to tap harder and harder, she suddenly says, 'I'm furious and I want to run away.' Option a is right because amplifying a bodily cue to bring a hidden feeling into present awareness is a signature Gestalt exercise. Exam trap: mindfulness also stresses present-moment awareness, but it observes sensations without changing them, whereas Gestalt deliberately amplifies them to provoke an emotional discovery.", "topic": "Gestalt exaggeration technique", "fig": "Fritz Perls"}, {"q": "Amiya is having stage fear in schools, her therapist teaches her to minimize emotional disturbances and self-defeating behaviors by acquiring a more realistic, workable, and compassionate philosophy of life. On this journey her therapist asks her to ask silly questions to the teacher in classroom. What kind of therapy the therapist would have most likely used here?", "options": {"a": "Client centered", "b": "Existential therapy", "c": "Rational emotive therapy", "d": "CBT"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the therapist is most likely using rational emotive therapy. Albert Ellis (1913 to 2007) founded rational therapy in 1955; it later became rational emotive therapy, and in 1993 rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). Ellis held that emotional disturbance comes not from events but from irrational beliefs about them, summed up in the ABC model: an Activating event, the Belief about it, and the emotional and behavioural Consequence. The most damaging beliefs are rigid 'musts' ('I must never look foolish'), awfulising and low frustration tolerance. Therapy disputes these beliefs and replaces them with flexible preferences, and Ellis stated its goal in almost the words of the stem: to help people minimise emotional disturbance and self-defeating behaviour by acquiring a more realistic, workable philosophy of life. REBT also sets behavioural homework, and its best known task is the shame-attacking exercise: the client deliberately does something mildly 'shameful' in public (asking a silly question, calling out the stops on a bus) and discovers that the feared disaster does not happen and that others' disapproval is bearable. Amiya's stage fear rests on a belief such as 'I must not be laughed at', so asking silly questions in class attacks that belief directly. Option c is right because the 'philosophy of life' aim and the shame-attacking assignment are both Ellis's signatures. Exam trap: REBT is one of the cognitive-behavioural therapies, so CBT tempts, but when a stem gives a technique specific to one school, choose that school.", "topic": "REBT and shame attacking", "fig": "Albert Ellis"}, {"q": "Three mountain task was used by Piaget to study ------ in children.", "options": {"a": "Centration", "b": "Egocentrism", "c": "Conservation", "d": "Reversibility"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the three mountain task was used to study egocentrism. Jean Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1948 in French; The Child's Conception of Space, 1956 in English) seated a child before a model of three mountains of different sizes, one capped with snow, one with a house and one with a red cross. A doll was placed at different points around the model, and the child had to choose, from a set of pictures, the view the doll would see. Children of about four usually picked their own view, and even six-year-olds often failed; only at about seven to eight did children reliably choose the doll's view. Piaget took this as evidence of egocentrism in the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years): not selfishness, but an inability to take another person's point of view, here literally a visual perspective. Later work by Martin Hughes (1975), in which a child hid a boy doll from policeman dolls, showed that four-year-olds could take another's view when the task made sense to them, so Piaget probably underestimated young children. Example: Kabir, aged four, covers his own eyes during hide-and-seek and says, 'You can't see me now', assuming that what he sees is what everyone sees. Option b is right because the three mountain task was built to test perspective taking, the core of egocentrism. Exam trap: centration, conservation and reversibility are also preoperational topics, but they are studied with conservation tasks (pouring water, rolling clay), not with the mountain model.", "topic": "Three mountain task", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "Gilligan found that women base their moral decision on culture of caring other human beings. This would place them at the stage ------ of Kohlberg's moral development theory", "options": {"a": "One", "b": "Two", "c": "Three", "d": "Four"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the scoring system of Lawrence Kohlberg (1958 onwards) tended to place a morality of caring for others at Stage 3. Kohlberg studied moral reasoning with dilemmas, the best known being Heinz, who must decide whether to steal a drug to save his dying wife. He scored the reasons, not the choice, into three levels of two stages each. The preconventional level has Stage 1 (punishment and obedience) and Stage 2 (instrumental exchange, 'you scratch my back, I'll scratch yours'). The conventional level has Stage 3 (interpersonal concordance, the 'good boy, good girl' orientation, where being good means caring about others, being kind and keeping their approval and trust) and Stage 4 (law and order, keeping the social system going). The postconventional level has Stage 5 (social contract) and Stage 6 (universal ethical principles). Carol Gilligan (1982), who had worked with Kohlberg at Harvard, argued in In a Different Voice that women's reasoning, built on relationships, responsibility and not hurting anyone, was often scored at Stage 3, which made women look less mature than men. She held that Kohlberg's scale reflects a male ethic of justice (rights, rules, fairness) and misses an equally mature ethic of care. Example: Meera says Heinz should not steal but should keep talking to the druggist so that nobody is hurt and the family stays together; a Kohlberg scorer would rate this caring reasoning at Stage 3. Option c is right because care for others and their approval define Stage 3. Exam trap: Stage 4 is also conventional and tempts, but it centres on duty to laws and society, which Gilligan linked to the justice orientation, not to care.", "topic": "Gilligan and Kohlberg's stages", "fig": "Carol Gilligan"}, {"q": "Arrange the phase of CBT in correct sequence: 1. Assessment or psychological assessment 2. Skill acquisition 3. Reconceptualization 4. Skill consolidation and application training 5. Maintenance post treatment assessment follow-up", "options": {"a": "1-3-2-4-5", "b": "1-2-3-4-5", "c": "1-2-4-3-5", "d": "1-4-2-3-5"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the correct sequence is 1-3-2-4-5, that is assessment, reconceptualisation, skills acquisition, skills consolidation and application training, then maintenance with post-treatment assessment and follow-up. This phase model of cognitive-behavioural treatment was set out by Dennis Turk, Donald Meichenbaum and Myles Genest in Pain and Behavioral Medicine (1983), and it closely follows Meichenbaum's stress inoculation training. Assessment comes first: therapist and client gather information on the problem, its triggers, thoughts and behaviours, using interviews, diaries and questionnaires. Reconceptualisation comes next: the therapist helps the client form a new, more hopeful understanding of the problem (for pain, that thoughts, feelings and behaviour change how pain is felt), which gives the rationale for treatment and builds motivation. Only then does skills acquisition begin, teaching coping skills such as relaxation, attention control and cognitive restructuring. Skills consolidation and application training follows, with rehearsal, role play, imagery and homework in real situations. The last phase covers maintenance and generalisation, with relapse prevention, post-treatment assessment and follow-up. Example: Arjun, who has chronic back pain, first keeps a pain diary (assessment), then learns that worry and inactivity make his pain worse (reconceptualisation), learns relaxation and pacing (acquisition), practises them at work (application) and returns for follow-up sessions (maintenance). Option a is right because reconceptualisation must come before skills training: a client first needs a reason for learning the skills. Exam trap: option b looks tidy because it follows the printed 1 to 5 order, but the list deliberately puts reconceptualisation third.", "topic": "Phases of CBT", "fig": "Donald Meichenbaum"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true of test-retest reliability?", "options": {"a": "The test is measuring what it claims to be measuring", "b": "The test will produce consistent results", "c": "The test will improve performance second time round", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: test-retest reliability means the test will produce consistent results over time. Reliability is the consistency of measurement. In classical test theory every observed score is a true score plus random error, and a reliability coefficient estimates how much of the score variance is true variance. Test-retest reliability, also called the coefficient of stability, is found by giving the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the two sets of scores with Karl Pearson's (1896) r. A high coefficient (say .85 or more) means people keep roughly the same rank order across occasions, so the test is stable. The interval matters: too short and people remember their answers, which inflates the estimate; too long and real change (growth, therapy, learning) lowers it. The method suits stable traits such as intelligence better than changeable states such as mood. Example: Kabir takes a trait anxiety inventory in June and again in July; a correlation of .88 between the two sets of scores shows high test-retest reliability. Option b is right because consistency of results across occasions is exactly what test-retest reliability measures. Exam trap: option a defines validity (measuring what the test claims to measure); a test can be perfectly consistent and still measure the wrong thing, so reliability and validity must not be confused.", "topic": "Test-retest reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Choose the statement which refers to concurrent validity:", "options": {"a": "That two tests are done at the same time.", "b": "Two or more clinicians agree on the outcome", "c": "The items on the test consistently relate to each other", "d": "The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: concurrent validity is the notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. Criterion-related validity checks a test against an outside standard, the criterion, and has two forms that differ only in timing. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later (an entrance test against first-year marks). In concurrent validity the criterion is measured at about the same time, and often it is an established test of the same attribute, a clinician's diagnosis or membership of a known group. The evidence is a validity coefficient: the correlation between the new test and the criterion. Concurrent validation is common when a short or cheap test is meant to replace a longer one. Example: Riya builds a 10-item Malayalam depression screen and gives it, in the same week, with Aaron Beck's (1961) Beck Depression Inventory to 150 clinic patients; a correlation of .80 supports concurrent validity. Option d is right because it describes a high correlation with an existing measure of the same attribute, which is how concurrent validity is shown. Exam trap: option a reads 'concurrent' literally (two tests at the same time) and leaves out the correlation with a criterion, which is the whole point. Key note: strictly, d blends concurrent validity with convergent validity, the construct validity idea of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959); a is a half-truth about timing with nothing about correlation, so d is the best of the four and stands. The same item reappears as Q64 with the options reordered.", "topic": "Concurrent validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "------ is assessed by Cronbach's alpha.", "options": {"a": "Concurrent validity", "b": "Inter-rater reliability", "c": "Test re-test reliability", "d": "Internal consistency"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: coefficient alpha, devised by Lee Cronbach (1951), assesses internal consistency. Cronbach introduced it in Psychometrika as a general index of how well the items of a test hang together. Internal consistency asks whether all the items measure the same thing: if they do, a person who scores high on some items should tend to score high on the others. Alpha is calculated from the number of items and the ratio of the summed item variances to the variance of total scores, so in effect it reflects the average inter-item correlation and the length of the test. It has a maximum of 1, and values of about .70 or more are usually treated as acceptable for research, with higher values needed for clinical decisions about individuals. It needs only one administration of one test, unlike test-retest or parallel forms. Alpha generalises Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), which works only for items scored right or wrong, to items with several response points, such as Likert items, and Cronbach showed it equals the mean of all possible split-half estimates. Example: Meera writes a 20-item self-esteem scale rated on five points; an alpha of .86 tells her the items measure a common attribute fairly consistently. Option d is right because alpha is computed from the interrelations of items within a single administration, which is what internal consistency means. Exam trap: inter-rater reliability also rests on agreement, but agreement between scorers (Jacob Cohen's kappa, 1960), not between items.", "topic": "Cronbach's alpha", "fig": "Lee Cronbach"}, {"q": "The term 'mental test' was invented by:", "options": {"a": "Galton", "b": "Cattel", "c": "Goodenough", "d": "Pearson"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: James McKeen Cattell coined the term \"mental test\". Cattell (1860 to 1944) was an American who took his doctorate under Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig (1886) and then worked with Francis Galton in England. He used the phrase in his paper \"Mental Tests and Measurements\", published in the journal Mind in 1890. There he proposed a standard battery of simple sensory and motor tasks, such as grip strength, rate of movement, two-point touch sensitivity, reaction time to sound and speed of naming colours, to be given to students. The idea, taken from Galton, was that a quick, keen nervous system marks an able mind. It failed: Clark Wissler (1901) found that the scores of Columbia students barely correlated with their college grades. The breakthrough came instead from Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), who tested judgement and reasoning directly. So the term is Cattell's even though his kind of test did not last. Option b is right because the phrase \"mental test\" first appears in Cattell's 1890 paper. Exam trap: Galton is called the father of mental testing and of psychometrics, and he ran the Anthropometric Laboratory in London from 1884, so his name pulls students; but fathering the movement is not the same as coining the term. A second trap is the name itself: James McKeen Cattell (mental test, reaction time, individual differences) is unrelated to Raymond Cattell, who built the 16PF and proposed fluid and crystallised intelligence.", "topic": "Origin of the term mental test", "fig": "James McKeen Cattell"}, {"q": "The major purpose of including negative and positive items in the test is to:", "options": {"a": "maintain balance", "b": "promote practicality in the test", "c": "check acquiescence response set", "d": "enhance reliability of the item"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: positively and negatively worded items are mixed in a test mainly to check, and offset, the acquiescence response set. A response set is a habitual way of answering that shifts scores regardless of what the items say; Lee Cronbach (1946) drew attention to it. Acquiescence, or yea-saying, is the tendency to agree, or to answer \"true\" or \"yes\", whatever the content; its mirror image is nay-saying. If every item on an anxiety scale is keyed so that \"agree\" means anxious, a yea-sayer earns a high anxiety score without being anxious at all. Balanced keying solves this. About half the items are worded so that agreeing shows the trait (\"I often feel tense\") and half so that disagreeing shows it (\"I usually feel calm\"), and the second set is reverse-scored. Example: Kabir, a habitual yea-sayer, agrees with both of those items. On a balanced scale his agreements cancel out, and a run of agreements with contradictory statements flags his answer sheet for a closer look. Option c is right because the reversed items exist precisely to expose and neutralise a content-free tendency to agree. Other response sets have their own controls: forced-choice pairs matched for desirability guard against social desirability (Allen Edwards, 1957), and validity scales such as the MMPI's L, F and K catch faking. Exam trap: \"maintain balance\" sounds right because the method is literally called balanced keying, but balance is the means, not the purpose; the purpose is controlling acquiescence. Do not confuse acquiescence with social desirability, which depends on item content (choosing what looks good).", "topic": "Balanced keying and acquiescence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An item of the test is best defined as a single task that:", "options": {"a": "Can be partially broken down into smaller units", "b": "Can be wholly broken down into smaller units", "c": "Cannot be broken down into any smaller units", "d": "Can be stretched and broken down into two smaller units"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: a test item is best defined as a single task or question that cannot be broken down into any smaller units. The item is the basic building block of a test, its smallest scorable unit: one question or task with its own scoring rule, such as one multiple-choice question, one true-false statement or one block design puzzle. Total scores are built by adding item scores, and item analysis (difficulty, discrimination, distractor analysis) works item by item. That only makes sense if each item is one indivisible unit. If a \"question\" can be split into parts that are scored separately, each part is really an item, and the original is a cluster of items (a section or testlet), not a single item. This is also why item-writing rules warn against double-barrelled items such as \"I enjoy parties and meeting strangers\": a person who likes one but not the other cannot answer honestly, because the item hides two tasks. Example: Meera writes a maths question for Class 4: \"What is 12 + 7, and is the answer odd or even?\" It contains two tasks, so a child who adds correctly but confuses odd and even gets a muddled score; she splits it into two items. Good items also measure only one thing, are unambiguous, give no clues to the answer, suit the difficulty level and discriminate between high and low scorers. Option c is right because indivisibility is what makes an item the unit of scoring and analysis. Exam trap: options a, b and d all describe something that can be broken down, which is the mark of a subtest, section or compound question, not an item; words like \"partially\", \"wholly\" and \"stretched\" are filler that makes the wrong options sound technical.", "topic": "Definition of a test item", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The steepness or slope of the item characteristic curve gives information about:", "options": {"a": "Difficulty value of the item", "b": "Discriminating power of the item", "c": "Effectiveness of distractors of item", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the steepness, or slope, of the item characteristic curve tells you the item's discriminating power. Item response theory (IRT), also called latent trait theory, models each item separately. It assumes an unobservable trait, theta, and plots the probability of a correct (or keyed) response against the person's level on that trait. The graph is the item characteristic curve (ICC), an S-shaped ogive, and three of its features carry three parameters. Its location on the trait axis, the point halfway up the curve (a probability of 0.50 when there is no guessing), is the difficulty, the b parameter: a curve shifted to the right belongs to a harder item. Its slope at that point is the discrimination, the a parameter: a steep curve means the chance of success jumps sharply as ability rises past b, so the item separates people just below that level from people just above it. A flat curve means high and low scorers pass at nearly the same rate, so the item tells you little. Its lower asymptote is the pseudo-guessing parameter, c, the chance that a very low-ability examinee still gets it right. The one-parameter model of Georg Rasch (1960) fixes the slope and estimates only difficulty; the work of Frederic Lord from 1952 led to the two- and three-parameter models. Example: Arjun's algebra item has a steep curve centred on average ability, so it cleanly divides average students from below-average ones. Option b is right because the slope is the IRT counterpart of the classical discrimination index. Exam trap: difficulty is read from where the curve sits (its left-right position), not from how steep it is; examiners count on students linking a \"steep\" curve with a \"hard\" item.", "topic": "Item characteristic curve slope", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Psychologist) with List-II (Concept): a. Adler, b. Bandura, c. Jung, d. Horney. 1. Basic anxiety, 2. Triadic Reciprocal causation, 3. Fictional Finalism, 4. Principle of Equivalence", "options": {"a": "a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1", "b": "a-1, b-3, c-2, d-4", "c": "a-3, b-2, c-4, d-1", "d": "a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Adler goes with fictional finalism (a-3), Bandura with triadic reciprocal causation (b-2), Jung with the principle of equivalence (c-4) and Horney with basic anxiety (d-1). Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), founder of individual psychology, borrowed from Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of \"As If\" (1911) the idea that people live by useful fictions. Fictional finalism is the imagined final goal, never fully real, that pulls a person's behaviour forward, such as \"I must be the best\". Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021) described reciprocal determinism (1978), later called triadic reciprocal causation (1986): person factors (beliefs, expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another in both directions. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) treated libido as general psychic energy governed by principles borrowed from physics. By the principle of equivalence, energy withdrawn from one interest reappears in another of equal value; by the principle of entropy, energy flows toward a balance that is never reached. Karen Horney (The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, 1937) traced neurosis to basic anxiety, the child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, which drives the strategies of moving toward, against or away from people. Example: when Kabir retires from cricket and pours the same passion into coaching, Jung would call it the principle of equivalence. Option c is right because it is the only set in which all four pairs are correct. Exam trap: option d gets Adler and Horney right but swaps Bandura and Jung; \"reciprocal\" and \"equivalence\" both sound like a balance of forces, so students trade them. Bandura is the social-cognitive theorist of three interacting factors; Jung is the energy theorist.", "topic": "Personality theorists and concepts", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): The usual educational evaluation is aimed at maximizing examiner variance. Reason (R): Examiner variance is reduced with higher inter-scorer reliability.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false as printed, while the Reason is true. Start with what \"variance\" means here. The spread of marks in an examination comes partly from true differences among examinees and partly from error. One error source is the examiner, or scorer: if a mark depends on who corrects the paper, part of the spread is examiner (scorer) variance. Assertion: educational evaluation aims to maximise examiner variance. False. A fair examination wants marks to reflect what students know, so it tries to maximise true differences among examinees and to minimise examiner variance. That is why boards use model answers, marking schemes, objective items and moderation of markers. Reason: examiner variance falls as inter-scorer reliability rises. True. Inter-scorer (scorer) reliability, discussed by Anne Anastasi in Psychological Testing (first edition 1954), is the agreement between two or more scorers who mark the same responses independently. High agreement means the score hardly depends on who marks it, so the share of variance due to scorers is small. Example: Riya's history essay gets 14 from one teacher and 6 from another; that gap is examiner variance. A clear marking scheme that brings both teachers to 11 raises inter-scorer reliability and shrinks it. Option d is right because a false Assertion paired with a true Reason is exactly what d states. Exam trap: the Reason is a correct textbook statement, which tempts students to call both true; judge each statement on its own before asking whether one explains the other. Key note: read as printed, A is false and d stands. If \"examiner\" was a slip for \"examinee\", A would be true (tests aim to spread examinees out), but R would still not explain it, which would give b; as printed, d stands.", "topic": "Examiner variance and scorer reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following variable(s) can be labelled as organismic variable(s)? 1. Task complexity 2. Intelligence 3. Errors made while learning task 4. Heart rate", "options": {"a": "4 only", "b": "2 and 4 only", "c": "1, 2 and 4 only", "d": "2, 3 and 4 only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: intelligence and heart rate are organismic variables, so 2 and 4 only. Robert Woodworth (1869 to 1962), whose Dynamic Psychology (1918) put the organism and its drives at the centre, expanded the behaviourist stimulus-response (S-R) formula into S-O-R, placing the organism between stimulus and response, and experimental psychology still sorts variables this way. Stimulus or task variables are features of the situation or task the experimenter presents: task complexity, list length, noise, instructions. Organismic (O, or subject) variables are characteristics or states that the participant brings to the study: age, sex, intelligence, personality, motivation, fatigue, and physiological states such as heart rate or blood sugar. Response variables are the behaviour measured as the outcome, the dependent variable: errors, reaction time, trials to criterion. Organismic variables usually cannot be manipulated, so they are measured, selected or controlled (by matching or random assignment); left uncontrolled, they act as extraneous variables. Example: Arjun learns a finger maze. The number of turns in the maze is a task variable, his IQ and resting heart rate are organismic variables, and the errors he makes are the response variable. Option b is right because only intelligence (a trait) and heart rate (a physiological state) belong to the organism. Exam trap: errors made while learning come from the person, so they feel organismic, but they are the response being measured (the DV); task complexity is a property of the task. Key note: heart rate is recorded as a response measure in some experiments, for example in psychophysiology, but as a state of the organism it is an O variable here; b stands.", "topic": "Organismic variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): A large body of research indicates that violence and aggression is learned through observation. Reason (R): Learning through observation takes place due to exposure to similar situation and experienced reinforcement.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "Both (A) and (R) are false"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false. Assertion: a large body of research shows that aggression is learned through observation. True. In the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch, kick and hammer an inflatable doll later copied the model's exact acts and words far more than children who saw a calm model or none, without any reward. Their 1963 follow-up found filmed and cartoon models worked too, and decades of media-violence research point the same way. Reason: observational learning happens because of exposure to the same situation and experienced reinforcement. False. Observational learning needs neither. The observer learns by watching someone else, often in a situation they have never been in, and need not be reinforced. Bandura's four processes are attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation; the first two govern learning (acquisition) and the last two govern performance. In Bandura's 1965 study, children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet when offered a reward for showing what the model had done, the differences between the groups all but disappeared: they had learned without reinforcement. Seeing a model rewarded or punished (vicarious reinforcement) changes whether an act is performed, not whether it is learned. Example: Kabir has never been in a fight, but after watching a film hero slap a rival he tries the same move on a classmate. Option c is right because A is well supported while R describes direct, operant learning rather than observational learning. Exam trap: R uses the language of reinforcement, which fits the earlier imitation theory of John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941), who held that imitation must itself be reinforced; Bandura showed it need not be.", "topic": "Observational learning of aggression", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Recent research has shown positive effect of cognitive remediation training on the neuro-cognitive functions as well as the overall functioning of patients with schizophrenia. Reason (R): Pharmacotherapy alone does not help patients with schizophrenia to function at the highest possible level of their ability.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but the Reason does not explain the Assertion. Cognitive remediation is a training approach for the cognitive deficits of schizophrenia, such as poor attention, memory, processing speed and planning. The patient does graded, repeated exercises on a computer or with paper and pencil, learns strategies (chunking, self-instruction, breaking a task into steps) and practises carrying them into daily tasks. Assertion: research shows it improves neurocognition and overall functioning. True. A 2011 meta-analysis by Til Wykes and colleagues found durable, moderate gains in cognition and gains in everyday functioning, and the functional gains were larger when cognitive remediation was combined with psychiatric rehabilitation such as supported employment. Reason: medication alone does not help patients function at their highest level. Also true. Antipsychotics mainly reduce positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations) but do little for the cognitive deficits and negative symptoms (flat affect, avolition) that best predict work and social outcome. Does R explain A? R explains why add-on psychosocial treatments are needed; it does not explain why cognitive remediation works. Remediation works because practice and strategy training improve cognitive skills that then carry over to daily life. Example: Arjun is stable on medication but cannot hold a job; after three sessions a week of attention and memory training plus vocational support, his work performance improves. Option b is right because each statement is true on its own, but R states a need, not the mechanism behind A's finding. Exam trap: when R reads like a sensible justification, students jump to a; always ask whether R tells you why A is true. Key note: some answer sheets read R as the rationale and mark a; strictly R gives the need, not the reason A is true; b stands.", "topic": "Cognitive remediation in schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Concept) with List-II (Explanation): a. Primack Principle, b. Maintenance Rehearsal, c. Pygmalion Effect, d. Activation spreading. 1. Exceptional performance by a student as a result of high teacher expectations. 2. Retrieval of pieces of information based on their relatedness to one another. 3. Keeping information in working memory by repeating it to you. 4. More preferred activity conserve as a reinforcer for a less preferred activity.", "options": {"a": "a-4, b-3, c-1, d-2", "b": "a-1, b-3, c-4, d-2", "c": "a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4", "d": "a-1, b-3, c-2, d-4"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Premack principle 4, maintenance rehearsal 3, Pygmalion effect 1 and spreading activation 2. The Premack principle (David Premack, 1959) says a more probable, preferred activity can reinforce a less probable one: \"finish your homework, then you can play cricket\". (The stem's \"conserve\" should read \"serve\".) Maintenance rehearsal, a term tied to the levels of processing framework of Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972), is simple rote repetition that keeps information active in working (short-term) memory, as when you repeat a phone number until you dial it; it contrasts with elaborative rehearsal, which links the item to meaning and lasts longer. The Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, Pygmalion in the Classroom, 1968) is a self-fulfilling prophecy: teachers told that certain randomly chosen pupils were about to bloom expected more of them, and those pupils showed larger IQ gains. Spreading activation (Allan Collins and Elizabeth Loftus, 1975) treats concepts in semantic memory as linked nodes; activating one spreads activation to related ones, so retrieval follows relatedness (\"doctor\" makes \"nurse\" easier to retrieve). Example: Meera's mother lets her watch television only after she has practised the veena, a Premack arrangement. Option a is right because every pair matches its definition. Exam trap: options b and d hand the Pygmalion definition to the Premack principle, because both describe performance rising after something else happens; remember that Premack is about one activity reinforcing another, and Pygmalion is about other people's expectations.", "topic": "Learning and memory concepts", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Surgery is one of the methods to understand brain behaviour relations in humans. Reason (R): Lesion method destroys part of the brain.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this item, in both the 2019 and the 2021 paper, marks (A) false and (R) true. Reason: the lesion method destroys part of the brain. True. A lesion is damage to or removal of tissue, and the lesion (ablation) method infers a region's function from what is lost, as in the ablation studies of Pierre Flourens (1820s) and the cortical lesions Karl Lashley made in rats (Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence, 1929). Assertion: surgery is one of the methods to understand brain-behaviour relations in humans. On the key's reading, false: as a research method, surgery means experimental lesioning, deliberately destroying or removing tissue to see what changes, and that is done only in animals. No one may operate on a human brain to find out what it does. In humans, brain-behaviour relations are studied through naturally occurring damage (strokes, tumours, head injuries), neuropsychological testing, electrical recording (EEG, event-related potentials), neuroimaging (CT, MRI, fMRI, PET) and non-invasive stimulation (transcranial magnetic stimulation). Patients who need surgery for epilepsy or a tumour can be tested before and after, and some of the best known findings came that way: Henry Molaison (H.M.), whose medial temporal lobes William Scoville removed in 1953 and whom Brenda Milner tested, and the split-brain patients of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1960s). But there the surgery is treatment, and the research method is the clinical study of a lesion. Example: a researcher who wants to know what the hippocampus does can lesion it in rats, but in people has to find patients whose hippocampus was damaged by illness or by a necessary operation. Option d is right on the key's reading because R correctly defines the lesion method, while A wrongly lists surgery as a method that can be used on humans. Exam trap: H.M. and the split-brain cases make A look obviously true, so most students pick a. Key note: this mock used to mark a, because therapeutic surgery such as H.M.'s has taught a great deal about the human brain and is a lesion in all but name. LBS's official key marks d for this question in both 2019 and 2021, on the reading that surgery is never a research method in humans. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "topic": "Surgery and the lesion method", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Read the following statements carefully and give an appropriate response: 1. The association between IQ and high-status professional jobs shows that such jobs require more intelligence. 2. Women tend to excel on test items that require verbal ability, vocabulary and rote learning. 3. Only about 6 percent of the population score above 140 on IQ tests. 4. An IQ score below 90 indicates mental retardation.", "options": {"a": "1, 3 and 4 are false, but 2 is true", "b": "1, 2 and 3 are false, but 4 is true", "c": "3, 2 and 4 are false, but 1 is true", "d": "3 and 4 are false, but 1 and 2 are true"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: statements 1, 3 and 4 are false and statement 2 is true. Take them one at a time. Statement 1 claims that because IQ is associated with high-status jobs, those jobs require more intelligence. False: a correlation cannot show that. People with higher IQs get more years of education and the degrees such jobs demand, and family background affects both IQ and access to these careers, so selection, credentials and class can all produce the link. Statement 2 says women tend to do better on verbal, vocabulary and rote-learning items. True in standard texts: the reviews of Diane Halpern (Sex Differences in Cognitive Abilities, first edition 1986) report small female advantages in verbal fluency, spelling, reading and rote verbal memory, while overall IQ shows no sex difference. Statement 3 says about 6 percent score above 140. False: with mean 100 and standard deviation 15, 140 lies about 2.67 SD above the mean, so only about 0.4 percent score higher; even above 130 (2 SD) is only about 2 percent. Statement 4 says an IQ below 90 indicates mental retardation. False: the cut-off for intellectual disability is about 70 (two SD below the mean), together with deficits in adaptive functioning and onset in childhood; 80 to 89 is \"low average\" and 70 to 79 \"borderline\". Example: Riya scores 86; she is in the low average range, not intellectually disabled. Option a is right because it is the only pattern with statement 2 alone true. Exam trap: the 6 percent figure is chosen to look plausible; work it from the normal curve, where only about 2 percent lie above 130. Key note: statement 2 is a broad generalisation (vocabulary differences are tiny), but with 1, 3 and 4 clearly false, a is the only option that fits; a stands.", "topic": "IQ facts and misconceptions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following does not assess the internal consistency reliability?", "options": {"a": "Test retest reliability", "b": "Split-half reliability", "c": "K-R formula", "d": "Cronbach's alpha"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: test-retest reliability does not assess internal consistency. Reliability is the consistency of scores, and each method catches a different source of error. Test-retest reliability gives the same test to the same people twice, usually a fortnight to a few weeks apart, and correlates the two sets of scores. It yields a coefficient of stability and catches error from changes over time, such as mood, health and testing conditions. Internal consistency methods need only one administration and ask whether the parts of the test agree with each other. Split-half reliability scores two halves (often odd against even items), correlates them, and steps the half-test correlation up to full length with the Spearman-Brown formula, published independently by Charles Spearman and William Brown in 1910. The Kuder-Richardson formulas, KR-20 and KR-21 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), estimate consistency across all items for items scored right or wrong. Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) generalises KR-20 to items with several answer points, such as rating scales, and equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients. Example: Kabir gives his new exam-anxiety scale once to 200 students and computes alpha to check that the items hang together; a month later he retests them to check stability, which is test-retest reliability. Option a is right because test-retest needs two occasions and measures stability over time, not agreement among items. Exam trap: all four options are reliability methods, so the item turns on which one is about time; parallel (alternate) forms reliability is not internal consistency either, since it measures the equivalence of two forms.", "topic": "Internal consistency reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When the reliability coefficient of a test is 1.00, the standard error of measurement will be:", "options": {"a": "1.00", "b": "0", "c": "0.50", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: when the reliability coefficient is 1.00, the standard error of measurement (SEM) is 0. A reliability coefficient describes how consistent a group's scores are; the SEM turns that into the likely error in one person's score. It is the standard deviation of the scores a person would obtain over many imaginary retestings, spread around their true score. The formula is SEM = SD x square root of (1 minus r), where SD is the standard deviation of the test scores and r is the reliability coefficient. With r = 1, 1 minus r is 0, its square root is 0, and the SEM is 0: every observed score equals the true score, with no measurement error at all. At the other extreme, with r = 0, the SEM equals the SD, because the score is pure error. In between, the SEM shrinks as reliability rises. Example: on an IQ test with SD 15 and reliability 0.91, SEM = 15 x 0.30 = 4.5, so if Meera scores 110, her true score lies between 105.5 and 114.5 about 68 percent of the time. Option b is right because perfect reliability leaves no error to estimate. Exam trap: students see 1.00 in the stem and pick 1.00, or assume SEM and reliability move together; they move in opposite directions.", "topic": "Standard error of measurement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If a psychological test yields the same results consistently but does not measure what it intends to measure, it is:", "options": {"a": "Valid but not reliable", "b": "Reliable but not valid", "c": "Neither reliable nor valid", "d": "Both reliable and valid"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: a test that gives the same results consistently but does not measure what it is meant to measure is reliable but not valid. Reliability is consistency: the same person gets about the same score on retesting, on another form or from another scorer. Validity is accuracy of meaning: the test measures the construct it claims to measure and supports the decisions made from it. The two are linked in one direction only. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. An unreliable test cannot be valid, because random error swamps any true signal, but a reliable test can still be consistently measuring the wrong thing. In numbers, a test's validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of its reliability coefficient. Example: Arjun's bathroom scale always reads 2 kg too high. Weigh him five times and it shows 72 kg every time, so it is perfectly reliable, but it is not valid, because his true weight is 70 kg. In testing, a \"creativity\" test that really measures vocabulary can give stable scores for the wrong construct. Option b is right because consistency without accuracy is exactly what \"reliable but not valid\" means. Exam trap: option a reverses the pair, and a test cannot be valid without being reliable; tie \"consistent\" to reliability and \"measures what it intends\" to validity.", "topic": "Reliability versus validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The largest percentage of people taking an IQ test will score in range from:", "options": {"a": "100 to 140", "b": "80 to 100", "c": "90 to 109", "d": "60 to 90"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the largest percentage of people taking an IQ test score in the 90 to 109 range. Modern intelligence tests use the deviation IQ: within each age group the mean is 100 and the standard deviation 15, and scores fall roughly on a normal curve, so most people cluster near the middle and fewer appear the further out you go. David Wechsler's classification, used since the Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939), labels bands of this curve. On the Wechsler scales, 90 to 109 is \"Average\" and holds about 50 percent of people, the largest single band. 80 to 89 (low average) and 110 to 119 (high average) hold about 16 percent each; 70 to 79 (borderline) and 120 to 129 (superior) about 7 percent each; 130 and above, and 69 and below, about 2 percent each. Example: if 100 children in Kabir's school are tested, about half will score between 90 and 109, so Kabir's 104 is perfectly ordinary. Option c is right because the average band sits at the peak of the curve and is the classification band with the biggest share. Exam trap: option a is a wider range and looks generous, but it covers only the half of the curve above 100, and even 100 to 140 holds slightly less than half of all people; option b (80 to 100) holds roughly 40 percent and option d (60 to 90) about a quarter. Key note: on pure normal-curve arithmetic, the 40-point span 100 to 140 also holds just under half (about 49.6 percent), but it is not a classification band; the question tests the classic fact that the average band 90 to 109 holds the most people, so c stands.", "topic": "IQ distribution and classification", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Cattel's 16 PF inventory is based upon:", "options": {"a": "Factor analysis strategy", "b": "Logical content strategy", "c": "Criterion group strategy", "d": "Mixed strategy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: Raymond Cattell's 16PF is built on the factor-analytic strategy. Personality inventories are built in four main ways. The logical-content (rational) strategy writes items whose content plainly reflects the trait, as in Robert Woodworth's Personal Data Sheet (1917), the first personality inventory. The theoretical strategy writes items for the constructs of a theory, as in the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule, based on the needs of Henry Murray (Explorations in Personality, 1938). The criterion-group (empirical keying) strategy keeps whichever items separate a diagnosed group from controls, whatever they say, as in the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943). The factor-analytic strategy gives many items to many people, correlates them, and lets factor analysis find clusters of items that go together; each cluster becomes a scale. Raymond Cattell (1905 to 1998) started from the lexical hypothesis, that important traits are coded in everyday words. He reduced the list of trait words compiled by Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) to clusters, factor-analysed ratings and questionnaire data, and arrived at 16 source traits such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability and dominance. The Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire appeared in 1949. Example: Meera's 16PF profile shows high warmth and low tension, each a factor-derived scale. Option a is right because the 16 scales are the factors that emerged from the analysis, not categories decided in advance. Exam trap: do not confuse Raymond Cattell (16PF, fluid and crystallised intelligence) with James McKeen Cattell, who coined \"mental test\"; and remember that the MMPI, not the 16PF, is the criterion-keyed inventory.", "topic": "Construction strategy of the 16PF", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "------ is not measured by General Aptitude Test Battery (GATB).", "options": {"a": "Numeric aptitude", "b": "Clerical perception", "c": "Finger dexterity", "d": "Abstract reasoning"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: abstract reasoning is not measured by the General Aptitude Test Battery (GATB). The GATB, released by the United States Employment Service in 1947 to match job seekers to occupations, is a multiple aptitude battery: a set of separately scored tests that gives a profile of aptitudes instead of a single IQ. It measures nine aptitudes: G, general learning ability; V, verbal aptitude; N, numerical aptitude; S, spatial aptitude; P, form perception; Q, clerical perception; K, motor coordination; F, finger dexterity; and M, manual dexterity. The dexterity aptitudes are measured with apparatus such as pegboards rather than paper and pencil. Abstract reasoning belongs to a different battery, the Differential Aptitude Tests (DAT) of George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman (1947), built for school guidance. Its eight subtests are verbal reasoning, numerical ability, abstract reasoning, clerical speed and accuracy, mechanical reasoning, space relations, spelling and language usage. Example: Arjun, applying for work assembling watch parts, takes the GATB; his high finger and manual dexterity scores suit precision work, but the battery gives him no abstract reasoning score. Option d is right because abstract reasoning is a DAT subtest and is not one of the GATB's nine aptitudes. Exam trap: both batteries date from the 1940s and both include numerical and clerical measures, so students merge their lists; the GATB's signature is its motor and dexterity aptitudes, the DAT's is abstract and mechanical reasoning.", "topic": "General Aptitude Test Battery", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If 10 years old has a mental age of 14 years, his IQ would be:", "options": {"a": "140", "b": "71.4", "c": "100", "d": "120"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: a 10-year-old with a mental age of 14 has a ratio IQ of 140. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon built the first intelligence scale in 1905, and their 1908 revision expressed ability as mental age (MA), the age level of the items a child passes. William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age (CA) to get the intelligence quotient, and Lewis Terman (Stanford-Binet, 1916) multiplied it by 100 to remove the decimal. So ratio IQ = MA / CA x 100. Here MA is 14 and CA is 10: 14 / 10 = 1.4, and 1.4 x 100 = 140. A child whose mental age equals chronological age scores exactly 100; a mental age above chronological age gives an IQ above 100, and one below gives an IQ below 100. The ratio IQ breaks down for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15 within each age group). Option a is right because 14 divided by 10, times 100, is 140. Exam trap: option b (71.4) comes from inverting the ratio, CA / MA x 100 = 10 / 14 x 100; a quick check is that a child who is ahead of their age must score above 100.", "topic": "Ratio IQ calculation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Researches have shown that antisocial and schizoid personality often score high on ------ of EPQ.", "options": {"a": "P scale and E scale", "b": "P scale and N scale", "c": "N scale only", "d": "P scale only"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: research shows that antisocial and schizoid personalities often score high on the P scale only. The Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ) of Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1975) measures three superfactors plus a Lie scale: E (extraversion versus introversion), N (neuroticism versus emotional stability) and P (psychoticism versus impulse control), with L detecting faking good. High P scorers are described as cold, tough-minded, egocentric, impersonal, impulsive, aggressive and lacking in empathy, and sometimes creative; despite its name, P is closer to psychopathy than to psychosis. Both personality types share P features. The antisocial person is callous, impulsive and aggressive; the schizoid person is cold, detached and emotionally flat. On E, however, they part ways: schizoid people are typically introverted (low E), while antisocial people tend to be extraverted and sensation seeking (high E). N, the anxious and moody dimension, is not the shared core of these two cold, callous types. So the one scale on which both score high is P. Example: Kabir, a detached loner with no close friends, and Arjun, a thrill-seeking youth with a record of fights, would both score high on P, but Kabir low and Arjun high on E. Option d is right because P is the only dimension the two profiles share. Exam trap: option a looks right if you remember that antisocial people are extraverted, but schizoid people are not, so \"P and E\" fails for the pair; option b adds N, which describes anxious emotional reactivity, not cold detachment.", "topic": "EPQ psychoticism scale", "fig": "Hans Eysenck"}, {"q": "Match List-I (Phenomenon) with List-II (Psychologist): a. Two factor theory, b. Multifactor Theory of Intelligence, c. Structure of Intellect Model, d. Level Theory of Intelligence. 1. Thurston, 2. Jensen, 3. Spearman, 4. Guilford", "options": {"a": "a-2, b-3, c-1, d-4", "b": "a-1, b-2, c-4, d-3", "c": "a-4, b-3, c-2, d-1", "d": "a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: two-factor theory goes with Spearman (a-3), multifactor theory with Thurstone (b-1), the Structure of Intellect model with Guilford (c-4) and level theory with Jensen (d-2). Charles Spearman (1904) noticed that all mental tests correlate positively and proposed two factors: g, a general ability shared by every test, and s, a specific ability unique to each test. Louis Thurstone (Primary Mental Abilities, 1938) factor-analysed a large set of tests given to students and found about seven primary mental abilities (verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning): several independent abilities rather than one dominant g, hence a multiple-factor theory. J. P. Guilford (1967) proposed the Structure of Intellect cube, operations x contents x products, giving 120 abilities at first and later 150 and 180. Arthur Jensen (1969) proposed a two-level theory: Level I, associative ability (rote memory, digit span), and Level II, conceptual ability (reasoning and abstraction, close to g). Example: Meera recalls a long string of phone numbers easily (Level I) but struggles with matrix puzzles (Level II). Option d is right because it is the only set in which all four pairs match. Exam trap: the word \"two\" misleads, since Spearman has two factors and Jensen two levels, so option a hands two-factor theory to Jensen; tie \"two-factor\" to g and s, which are Spearman's alone. Key note: Indian texts often call Edward Thorndike's theory (1920s) \"multifactor\" and Thurstone's \"group factor\"; with Thorndike absent, Thurstone is the only fit for b; d stands.", "topic": "Theories of intelligence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Route) with List-II (Processing): a. Central route to persuasion, b. Peripheral route to persuasion, c. Distinct routes to persuasion, d. Attitude behaviour discrepancies. 1. Heuristic processing, 2. Elaboration likelihood processing, 3. Cognitive Dissonance, 4. Systematic processing", "options": {"a": "a-4, b-1, c-2, d-3", "b": "a-3, b-2, c-4, d-1", "c": "a-1, b-2, c-4, d-3", "d": "a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: central route 4 (systematic processing), peripheral route 1 (heuristic processing), distinct routes 2 (elaboration likelihood) and attitude-behaviour discrepancy 3 (cognitive dissonance). The elaboration likelihood model (ELM) of Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1981, 1986) says there are two distinct routes to persuasion. Elaboration means thinking carefully about a message's arguments, and its likelihood depends on motivation (personal relevance, need for cognition) and ability (time, knowledge, freedom from distraction). With both, people take the central route: they scrutinise the quality of the arguments, and the resulting attitude change lasts, resists attack and predicts behaviour. Without them, people take the peripheral route, relying on cues such as an attractive or expert source, the number of arguments or a good mood, and the change is shallow and short-lived. The heuristic-systematic model of Shelly Chaiken (1980) makes a parallel split, and its terms map onto the ELM: systematic processing is the central route and heuristic processing (\"experts are usually right\") the peripheral route. When people act against their attitudes, Leon Festinger's cognitive dissonance theory (1957) applies: the discrepancy creates discomfort, which people often reduce by changing the attitude, as in the one-dollar study of Festinger and James Carlsmith (1959). Example: Riya compares battery life and camera specifications before buying a phone (central, systematic), while her brother buys the one a cricket star advertises (peripheral, heuristic). Option a is right because every pair fits. Exam trap: option c gives the central route heuristic processing and option d calls the peripheral route systematic; \"central\" should pull you to careful, systematic thought and \"peripheral\" to mental shortcuts.", "topic": "Elaboration likelihood model", "fig": "Richard Petty and John Cacioppo"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Encoding specificity states that a retrieval cue is successful only if it is present at the time of encoding. Reason (R): When participants are given category names as retrieval it enables the participant of the experiment to remember which were previously presented to them to remember.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but the Reason does not explain the Assertion. Assertion: encoding specificity states that a retrieval cue succeeds only if it was present at encoding. True. Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson (1973) showed that a cue helps recall only to the extent that it was encoded together with the item. In their experiments, a target word studied beside a weakly related word was recalled better when that same weak partner was the cue than when a strong associate of the target, never seen at study, was the cue. Context-dependent memory fits the same principle: Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley (1975) found that divers recalled words best in the setting, underwater or on land, where they had learned them. Reason: category names given as retrieval cues help people recall the words presented to them. Also true. Endel Tulving and Zena Pearlstone (1966) had people learn word lists grouped by category; at recall, those given the category names (\"animals\", \"fruits\") recalled far more than those without cues, showing that many words were available in memory but not accessible without a cue. Does R explain A? No. R demonstrates that cues aid retrieval; it does not say why a cue must match what was encoded, which is the heart of encoding specificity. Example: Kabir studies in a quiet library and later recalls more in a quiet hall than in a noisy canteen. Option b is right because R illustrates cued recall rather than stating the reason for the principle. Exam trap: A and R share the word \"cue\", which tempts a. Key note: in that study the category names were present at study, so R is consistent with A and some sheets mark a; an illustration is not the explanation of a principle, so b stands.", "topic": "Encoding specificity and cued recall", "fig": "Endel Tulving"}, {"q": "Anitha is a 23-year-old girl who is unstable in relationships, self-destructive, and having recurrent thoughts of suicide and impulsive. Which is the most suitable therapeutic method for this condition?", "options": {"a": "Client centered therapy", "b": "Expressive psychotherapy", "c": "Dialectic behavioral therapy", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) is the most suitable method for Anitha. Her picture, unstable relationships, self-destructive acts, recurrent suicidal thoughts and impulsivity, matches borderline personality disorder (BPD): a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotion, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood. Marsha Linehan (Cognitive-Behavioral Treatment of Borderline Personality Disorder, 1993) developed DBT for chronically suicidal women with BPD. Standard CBT's constant push for change felt invalidating to them, while acceptance alone left the self-harm untouched, so DBT joins acceptance (drawn from Zen practice) with change (drawn from behaviour therapy). Her biosocial theory sees BPD as emotion dysregulation arising when an emotionally sensitive child meets an invalidating environment. Standard DBT combines weekly individual therapy, a skills group, telephone coaching in crises and a therapist consultation team. Its four skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, and treatment targets life-threatening behaviour first. Randomised trials, beginning with Linehan's own in 1991, show fewer suicide attempts, less self-harm and fewer days in hospital than usual treatment. Option c is right because DBT was built for exactly this cluster and has the strongest trial evidence for reducing suicidal and self-harming behaviour. Exam trap: \"most suitable\" asks for the evidence-based first-line treatment, not for any therapy that might be tried. Key note: expressive psychotherapy, which Otto Kernberg described for borderline patients (Borderline Conditions and Pathological Narcissism, 1975) and later developed into transference-focused psychotherapy, is also used for BPD with some evidence, but DBT is the first-line answer for recurrent suicidality and impulsivity; c stands.", "topic": "DBT for borderline personality", "fig": "Marsha Linehan"}, {"q": "18-year-old Navaneeth believes that he is unique and life difficulties may not affect him, and he involves in rash driving. This phenomenon is known as", "options": {"a": "Imaginary audience", "b": "Personal fable", "c": "Self concept", "d": "Self esteem"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Navaneeth's belief that he is unique and that life's difficulties will not touch him is the personal fable. David Elkind (1967) argued that when adolescents reach the formal operational stage of Jean Piaget (described with Barbel Inhelder in 1955; English translation 1958) they can think about thinking, including other people's thoughts, but they fail to separate what others are thinking about from their own preoccupations. He called this adolescent egocentrism and described two mental constructions. The personal fable is the belief that one's experiences and feelings are unique (\"no one has ever felt what I feel\"), together with a sense of invulnerability (\"it will not happen to me\"). It helps explain why teenagers take risks such as rash driving, unprotected sex or drug use while knowing the dangers. The imaginary audience is the belief that everyone is watching and judging, which makes teenagers intensely self-conscious about a pimple or a bad haircut. Both are strongest in early adolescence and fade in the late teens. Navaneeth shows both strands of the fable: uniqueness (\"I am different\") and invulnerability, acted out in rash driving. Option b is right because uniqueness plus invulnerability leading to risk-taking is the textbook description of the personal fable. Exam trap: the imaginary audience is its twin, also from Elkind; tell them apart by focus: the audience is about being watched (self-consciousness), the fable is about being special and safe (risk-taking).", "topic": "Personal fable in adolescence", "fig": "David Elkind"}, {"q": "Infants understand much longer sentence from their parents and others, but they may be able to produce one or two words only. This phenomenon is known as", "options": {"a": "Child directed speech", "b": "Receptive productive lag", "c": "Telegraphic speech", "d": "Language acquisition device"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the gap between understanding long sentences and producing only one or two words is the receptive-productive lag. Receptive (comprehension) language is what a child understands; productive (expressive) language is what the child can say. In every normally developing child, comprehension runs ahead of production. Around the first birthday an infant may say only a word or two yet respond correctly to dozens of words and to whole requests such as 'bring me your shoes'; children understand about 50 words several months before they can say 50. The lag exists because understanding a word only needs the child to match a heard sound pattern to a meaning, while saying it needs the child to retrieve the word and to plan and control the muscles of speech. The gap never fully closes: adults, too, understand far more words than they use. Example: one-year-old Riya cannot yet say 'ball', but when her grandmother asks 'where is the ball?' she crawls across the room and points to it. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly this mismatch: long sentences understood, one or two words produced. Exam trap: the other three options are real terms from the same chapter on language development, so they feel familiar. Telegraphic speech describes the form of what the child says (two-word utterances), child-directed speech describes what adults say to the child, and the language acquisition device is a theory of why language develops at all; none names the gap between understanding and saying.", "topic": "Receptive-productive lag", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Analysis) with List-II (Explanation): a) Structural analysis, b) TA, c) Script analysis, d) Game analysis. 1) describing what between two or more people, 2) understanding transaction that lead to bad feeling, 3) understanding what is happening within individual, 4) understanding individual's life plan", "options": {"a": "a-3, b-2, c-1, d-4", "b": "a-1, b-3, c-4, d-2", "c": "a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1", "d": "a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: structural analysis studies what is happening within the individual (3), transactional analysis proper studies what passes between two or more people (1), script analysis studies the person's life plan (4) and game analysis studies transactions that end in bad feeling (2). All four are levels of Eric Berne's transactional analysis (TA), set out in Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy (1961) and Games People Play (1964). Structural analysis maps the three ego states inside one person: Parent (attitudes copied from parents), Adult (objective processing of present reality) and Child (feelings and responses kept from childhood). Transactional analysis proper then studies exchanges between people, each a stimulus from one ego state and a response from another: complementary transactions keep talk going, crossed ones stop it and ulterior ones carry a hidden message. Game analysis studies repeated series of ulterior transactions with a predictable payoff of bad feelings, such as 'Why Don't You, Yes But', in which every suggestion is rejected. Script analysis uncovers the life script, an unconscious life plan decided in childhood under parental injunctions ('Don't be close') and resting on a life position such as 'I'm not OK, you're OK'. Example: Kabir keeps asking friends for advice, rejects every idea and then feels misunderstood (a game); his therapist traces it to a childhood decision that nobody can really help him (his script). Option d is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: the label TA names both the whole theory and its second level, so students give 'within the individual' to TA; that belongs to structural analysis, because ego states live inside one person.", "topic": "Berne's four levels of TA", "fig": "Eric Berne"}, {"q": "When the person answers your question in a disorganized or rambled way and doesn't reach an answer it is called:", "options": {"a": "Tangentiality", "b": "Word salad", "c": "Circumtiality", "d": "Flight of ideas"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: a reply that wanders away from the question and never reaches the answer is tangentiality. Tangentiality and its look-alikes are disorders of the form of thought, judged from how speech is organised rather than from what the person believes. In tangentiality the person starts near the question, drifts off along a loosely related path and never returns to the goal. Nancy Andreasen's (1979) Scale for the Assessment of Thought, Language and Communication defines it as replying to a question in an oblique or even irrelevant way, which matches this stem's 'answers your question'. It is seen in schizophrenia and mania. In circumstantiality the person also takes needless detours and piles up detail, but finally does reach the point; it is typical of epilepsy, obsessional traits and organic states. Example: asked 'Where do you live, Arjun?', a tangential reply runs 'Near the temple; the temple bells are loud; loud music hurts my ears; my ears have hurt since school...', and the place is never named. A circumstantial reply wanders through the bus route and the neighbours, then ends 'so, behind Palayam market'. Option a is right because the stem's deciding phrase is 'doesn't reach an answer'. Exam trap: tangentiality and circumstantiality both sound rambling, so examiners pair them; the test is whether the goal is reached. Goal never reached: tangentiality. Goal reached late, after detours: circumstantiality.", "topic": "Tangentiality versus circumstantiality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following cannot be included in the categories of classification of responses of Rorschach test?", "options": {"a": "Location", "b": "Context", "c": "Content", "d": "Determinant"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'context' is not one of the scoring categories of the Rorschach test. Hermann Rorschach (1921), in Psychodiagnostik, presented ten symmetrical inkblots and asked 'What might this be?'. Every response is then coded on fixed categories. Location records which part of the blot was used: W the whole blot, D a common detail, Dd an unusual detail and S the white space. Determinants record what feature of the blot shaped the percept: form (F), movement (human M, animal FM, inanimate m), colour (FC, CF, C) and shading (texture, vista, diffuse). Content records what was seen: human (H), animal (A), anatomy, nature, objects and so on. Scorers also code popular responses (P, answers common in the norm group) and form quality (how well the percept fits the blot's shape), and John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) merged the rival American scoring systems into one. Example: Meera says of Card V, 'A bat, the whole thing, because of its shape'; this is scored W for location, F for the form determinant, A for animal content and P for a popular response. Option b is right because no standard Rorschach system has a 'context' category: the test scores where on the blot, why that percept and what was seen, not the circumstances around the response. Exam trap: 'context' looks and sounds like 'content', and a quick reader treats them as the same word; content is a real category, context is not.", "topic": "Rorschach scoring categories", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Researchers have revealed that social desirability tendency is related to:", "options": {"a": "Need for social approval", "b": "Need for social conforming", "c": "Need for avoiding social criticism", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: research ties the social desirability tendency to the need for social approval, to social conformity and to avoiding social criticism. Social desirability is the tendency to answer test items in the way that makes oneself look good rather than in the way that is true. Allen Edwards (1957) showed that how desirable an item sounds strongly predicts how often people endorse it. Douglas Crowne and David Marlowe (1960) built the Marlowe-Crowne Social Desirability Scale from two kinds of items: behaviours that are approved but rarely true of anyone (never having disliked anyone) and behaviours that are disapproved but true of almost everyone. A high scorer claims too many virtues and denies too many common faults. In their later book, The Approval Motive (1964), they read the score as a need for approval. High scorers conformed more to group pressure, were more easily persuaded, and were more cautious and defensive in situations where they might be judged, which amounts to avoiding disapproval and criticism. Example: Arjun, a high scorer, ticks 'I never gossip' and, in a group task, quietly goes along with the others' wrong answer rather than risk their disapproval. Option d is right because a, b and c are three faces of the same approval motive that the research found. Exam trap: option a is the textbook label and looks like the single answer, but the stem asks what social desirability is related to, and conformity and fear of criticism are part of the same findings.", "topic": "Social desirability and approval", "fig": "Douglas Crowne and David Marlowe"}, {"q": "When the testee is found to respond in some systematic way by choosing any one type of the response options in each item, he/she is definitely showing", "options": {"a": "Deviation set", "b": "Acquiescence set", "c": "Social desirability bias", "d": "Social undesirability bias"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: choosing the same kind of response option on item after item, whatever the item says, is an acquiescence set. A response set is a habitual way of answering that shifts scores regardless of item content; Lee Cronbach (1946) drew attention to it. In acquiescence (yea-saying) the person tends to agree, or to answer 'true' or 'yes'; nay-saying is the mirror image. Because the bias attaches to the response format rather than to the content, a yea-sayer can score high on any scale keyed in the 'agree' direction without having the trait. Test builders control it with balanced keying: half the items are worded so that agreeing scores the trait and half so that disagreeing does, so a yea-sayer's agreements cancel out. Douglas Jackson and Samuel Messick (1958) argued that much of the variance on inventories reflects such styles rather than content. Example: Meera ticks 'agree' on all 40 items of a scale, including both 'I enjoy noisy parties' and 'I prefer to stay at home in the evening'; the contradiction exposes acquiescence. Option b is right because the stem's key words, 'systematic' and 'choosing any one type of the response options in each item', describe answering driven by the format, not by the content. Exam trap: social desirability also distorts answers, but it depends on content (the person picks what looks good), whereas acquiescence ignores content entirely.", "topic": "Acquiescence response set", "fig": ""}, {"q": "All cause non sampling errors except:", "options": {"a": "Faulty tools of measurement", "b": "Defect in data collection", "c": "Non response", "d": "Inadequate sample"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: an inadequate sample produces sampling error, so it is the one item that is not a cause of non-sampling error. Survey error comes in two families. Sampling error is the chance difference between a sample statistic and the population parameter that arises simply because only part of the population was studied. It shrinks as the sample grows (the standard error of the mean is the SD divided by the square root of n), is zero in a complete census, and can be calculated for probability samples. Non-sampling error is everything else that goes wrong while collecting and handling data, and it can occur even in a census: faulty measuring tools (badly worded questions, unreliable scales), defects in data collection (interviewer bias, wrong procedures, careless recording), non-response (people who do not reply differ from those who do), coverage error and processing error (coding and data entry mistakes). A bigger sample does not cure non-sampling error. Example: Kabir surveys 2,000 students with a confusing questionnaire and gets only a 30 percent reply rate; his large sample keeps sampling error small, but the poor tool and the non-response still bias his results. Option d is right because the size and representativeness of the sample concern sampling itself, so an inadequate sample is a source of sampling error, the exception here. Exam trap: 'inadequate sample' sounds like a mistake, so it tempts students to count it as non-sampling error; ask whether the error would vanish if the whole population were studied. If it would, it is sampling error.", "topic": "Sampling versus non-sampling error", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A researcher divides the population into certain groups and fixes the size of the sample from each group. It is called", "options": {"a": "Stratified sample", "b": "Quota sample", "c": "Cluster sample", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: dividing the population into groups and fixing how many people will be taken from each group is quota sampling. Quota sampling is a non-probability method. The researcher first decides which characteristics matter (sex, age, locality, occupation) and how many people of each kind are needed, usually in proportion to the population, say 60 women and 40 men. Interviewers then fill each quota with whoever they can find, so the choice within each group is left to the interviewer, not to chance. It is quick, cheap and needs no sampling frame, which is why market researchers and opinion pollsters use it, but bias enters at the point of choice and sampling error cannot be calculated. It is often described as a non-random version of stratified sampling. Stratified random sampling also divides the population into strata, but then selects people randomly within each stratum. Example: Riya needs 50 college students and 50 working adults for a survey on screen time; she stands outside a mall and stops passers-by until each quota of 50 is full. Option b is right because fixing the number to be drawn from each group is the defining feature of quota sampling. Exam trap: stratified sampling also involves groups and a sample size per group, so look for the word 'random'; without random selection within groups, the answer is quota. Key note: as far as it goes, the stem also fits stratified sampling, since a stratified sample also fixes a size for each stratum; in the research methods texts this wording comes from, fixing the number to be taken from each group is the defining feature of quota sampling, so b stands.", "topic": "Quota sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The cardinal DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation is based primarily on an IQ score:", "options": {"a": "One standard deviation below the mean", "b": "Two standard deviation below the mean", "c": "Three standard deviation below the mean", "d": "Four standard deviation below the mean"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the DSM-IV-TR intellectual criterion for mental retardation was an IQ about two standard deviations below the mean. Intelligence tests such as David Wechsler's scales (from 1939) are standardised to a mean of 100 and a standard deviation (SD) of 15, so two SD below the mean is 100 minus 30, an IQ of 70. DSM-IV-TR (2000) required significantly subaverage intellectual functioning, an IQ of about 70 or below on an individually given test, together with deficits in adaptive functioning (communication, self-care, social and practical skills) and onset before age 18. Allowing for measurement error, scores up to about 75 could qualify when adaptive deficits were clear. On the normal curve only about 2.3 percent of people score below 70. Severity was graded by IQ: mild 50 to 55 up to about 70 (about 85 percent of cases), moderate 35 to 40 up to 50 to 55, severe 20 to 25 up to 35 to 40, and profound below 20 to 25. DSM-5 later renamed the condition intellectual disability and graded severity by adaptive functioning instead. Example: Kabir, aged 9, scores 68 on the WISC and needs help with dressing and handling money; he meets both the IQ and the adaptive criteria. Option b is right because 70 lies exactly two SD below the mean of 100. Exam trap: one SD below the mean (85) marks the upper edge of borderline intellectual functioning (71 to 84), not the cut-off for mental retardation.", "topic": "Intellectual disability IQ criterion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Validity of a research can be improved by:", "options": {"a": "Taking the true representative samples of the population", "b": "Eliminating extraneous factors", "c": "Both of the above measures", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the validity of a research study improves both by taking a truly representative sample and by eliminating extraneous factors. Validity here belongs to a study, not to a test. Donald Campbell (1957) first set internal validity against external validity, and Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) listed the threats to each. Internal validity asks whether the independent variable really caused the change in the dependent variable. It is threatened by extraneous variables such as history, maturation, testing effects, selection differences between groups and experimenter bias, and it improves when these are removed or controlled through random assignment, constant conditions, blinding and control groups. External validity asks whether the findings generalise to other people, settings and times. It improves when the sample truly represents the population, ideally through random selection, and when the setting resembles real life. A study needs both: a tightly controlled study on an odd sample says little about anyone else, and a representative sample in an uncontrolled study cannot show cause. Example: Meera tests a new memory training programme; random assignment and a quiet, standard testing room protect internal validity, while drawing students at random from several colleges protects external validity. Option c is right because a raises external validity and b raises internal validity, so both measures improve the study's validity. Exam trap: random selection (who enters the study) serves external validity, while random assignment (who gets which condition) serves internal validity; examiners often swap the two.", "topic": "Internal and external validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following are not Factor Analytically derived personality inventories? 1. 16 PF 2. Comrey Personality Scales 3. Jenkins Activity Survey 4. Edwards Personal Preference Schedule", "options": {"a": "1 and 2 only", "b": "3 and 4 only", "c": "1, 2 and 3 only", "d": "2, 3 and 4 only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Jenkins Activity Survey (3) and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (4) are not factor-analytically derived. Personality inventories are built in four main ways. The logical-content strategy writes items whose content obviously fits the trait; the theoretical strategy writes items for the constructs of a theory; the empirical criterion-group strategy keeps whatever items separate a criterion group from controls; and the factor-analytic strategy correlates many items, keeps those that load on each factor and names the factors afterwards. Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (1949) came from factor analyses of trait terms, and Andrew Comrey's Comrey Personality Scales (1970; 180 items, eight factors such as trust and orderliness) were built the same way, so 1 and 2 are factor-analytic. The Jenkins Activity Survey of C. David Jenkins, Stephen Zyzanski and Ray Rosenman measures the Type A behaviour pattern; its Type A scale items were chosen because they agreed with Structured Interview classifications, a criterion approach. Allen Edwards's Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (revised edition, 1959) is theory-based: it measures 15 needs from Henry Murray's (1938) list, using forced-choice pairs matched for social desirability. Example: Arjun gives 300 new items to 1,000 students and keeps only the clusters that emerge from factor analysis; that is Cattell's and Comrey's route, not Edwards's. Option b is right because 3 and 4 are the only two not built by factor analysis. Exam trap: the EPPS controls social desirability so carefully that it looks statistical, but its items were written for Murray's need theory. Key note: the Jenkins Activity Survey also reports subscales (speed and impatience, job involvement, hard-driving competitiveness) found by factor analysis, but its main Type A scale was built to match interview classifications, so it is not classed as a factor-analytic inventory; b stands.", "topic": "Inventory construction strategies", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Task) with List-II (Type of memory): a. Free recall and recognition, b. Sentence verification task, c. Priming, d. Scanning task. 1. Semantic memory, 2. Sensory memory, 3. Episodic memory, 4. Implicit memory", "options": {"a": "a-4, b-2, c-1, d-3", "b": "a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4", "c": "a-1, b-4, c-2, d-3", "d": "a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: free recall and recognition test episodic memory (3), sentence verification tests semantic memory (1), priming tests implicit memory (4) and the scanning task, by elimination, sensory memory (2). Endel Tulving (1972) split long-term memory into episodic memory, personal events tied to a time and place, and semantic memory, general knowledge of facts and concepts. Free recall (write down every word from the list you just heard) and recognition (which of these words were on the list?) ask about one specific study episode, so they are episodic tasks. In a sentence verification task the person judges sentences such as 'A canary is a bird' as true or false as fast as possible; Allan Collins and Ross Quillian (1969) used the reaction times to map the network of stored facts, so the task taps semantic memory. Priming is an influence of earlier exposure without conscious recollection: having seen BUTTER, a person completes BUT___ as BUTTER more often, even without remembering the word; Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) called this implicit memory. Scanning a briefly flashed display, as in George Sperling's (1960) partial report, taps the brief iconic store of sensory memory. Example: Riya recognises yesterday's list words (episodic), confirms that a sparrow is a bird (semantic) and fills BUT___ with BUTTER without knowing why (implicit). Option d is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: recall and recognition feel like knowledge tests, so students send them to semantic memory; they test memory for an episode. Key note: Saul Sternberg's (1966) memory scanning task is really a short-term memory task, while scanning a briefly flashed display, as in Sperling's partial report, taps sensory (iconic) memory; with the other three pairs fixed, d stands.", "topic": "Memory tasks and memory systems", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): AutoShaping involves S-S learning. Reason (R): Some behaviours are innate responses to a stimulus and because of temporal contiguity between them, they acquire stimulus related properties.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and R explains A, because the lit key comes to stand for food. Assertion: Paul Brown and Herbert Jenkins (1968) lit a response key for a few seconds before giving hungry pigeons food, and the food came whether or not the bird did anything. Yet the pigeons soon began pecking the lit key, as if the response had been shaped by hand, hence 'autoshaping'. David Williams and Harriet Williams (1969) then used omission training: a peck at the lit key cancelled the food. The pigeons kept pecking on many trials and so lost food they could have had. A response that persists when it costs the reward is not operant; it is driven by the pairing of key light and food, which is Pavlovian stimulus-stimulus (S-S) learning. So A is true. Reason: pecking is the pigeon's innate, unconditioned response to food. Because the lit key repeatedly comes just before food (temporal contiguity), the light takes on some of food's properties and draws the same response, the idea of stimulus substitution. Herbert Jenkins and Bruce Moore (1973) showed this neatly: pigeons pecked a key signalling food with an open-beaked, eating-like peck and a key signalling water with a slower, drinking-like peck. So R is true. Example: Kabir's pet pigeon starts pecking the small light on its feeder that glows just before seeds drop, though the seeds drop anyway. Option a is right because R supplies the mechanism of the S-S learning that A names. Exam trap: a key peck looks like a skeletal, voluntary operant response, so students mark A false; the omission result shows it is classically conditioned.", "topic": "Autoshaping as S-S learning", "fig": "Paul Brown and Herbert Jenkins"}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?", "options": {"a": "The items on the test consistently relates to each other", "b": "That two tests are done at the same time", "c": "Two or more clinicians agree on the outcome", "d": "The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: concurrent validity means that scores on a test correlate highly with scores on an accepted measure of the same attribute taken at about the same time. This item repeats Q24 of this paper with the options reordered. Criterion-related validity is the correlation between a test and an independent, external measure of the attribute, the criterion, such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis, an established gold-standard scale, school marks or job ratings. Its two types differ only in timing. In concurrent validity, test and criterion are measured at about the same time, so the test is checked against present status. In predictive validity, the criterion is gathered later, as when entrance scores are correlated with first-year marks. The classic concurrent design gives a new test together with an established one: a new 20-item depression questionnaire that correlates 0.85 with a gold-standard scale completed the same day shows concurrent validity. The correlation is called the validity coefficient. Example: Riya builds a 10-minute reading screener and gives it to 200 children on the same day as a full, well-established reading battery; a high correlation between the two shows concurrent validity. Option d is right because it is the only option that contains the essential idea, a high correlation with an accepted measure of the same attribute. Exam trap: the word 'concurrent' tempts students to pick 'two tests are done at the same time', but timing alone is not validity. Key note: strictly, correlating with tests of the same attribute blends concurrent and convergent validity, and option b is a half-truth (the timing) with nothing about correlation; d is the best of the four and stands.", "topic": "Concurrent validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Trait theorists use: 1. 16 PF - NEO-PI - MBTI 2. MMPI - EPQ - Rorschach test 3. TAT - MBTI - 16 PF 4. EPQ - MMPI - NEO-PI", "options": {"a": "1 and 4 only are correct", "b": "1, 2 and 4 only are correct", "c": "1 and 3 only are correct", "d": "2 and 4 only are correct"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: sets 1 (16PF, NEO-PI, MBTI) and 4 (EPQ, MMPI, NEO-PI) are the tools trait theorists use. Trait theorists describe personality as stable, measurable dimensions on which everyone can be placed, and they measure them with structured self-report inventories scored against norms. Raymond Cattell's 16PF (1949) measures 16 source traits; Paul Costa and Robert McCrae's NEO Personality Inventory (1985) measures the Big Five; Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck's Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975) measures psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism. The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator of Isabel Briggs Myers and Katharine Briggs, based on Carl Jung's (1921) types, sorts people into types but rests on self-report scales underneath; the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943) is an empirically keyed clinical inventory that yields scale scores. Sets 2 and 3, by contrast, contain projective techniques: the Rorschach inkblots (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935), which rest on psychodynamic assumptions and are scored by interpretation; trait researchers such as Eysenck dismissed them as unreliable. Example: Kabir, studying stress at work from a trait view, gives his staff the NEO-PI and the EPQ, not inkblots. Option a is right because 1 and 4 are the only sets made up entirely of self-report inventories. Exam trap: one projective test spoils a set, so check each set for the Rorschach or TAT first. Key note: sets 1 and 4 each carry one non-trait tool (MBTI, MMPI), but both are self-report inventories with trait-style scores, while 2 and 3 include projective tests that trait theorists reject; a stands.", "topic": "Trait measures versus projectives", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Topographical organization: 1. Applies to sensory cortical areas 2. High degree of topographical organisation shows in the auditory pathway 3. Fibres in this pathway originate from the ganglion cells of the retina 4. Fibres then synapse at the lateral geniculate body of the hypothalamus", "options": {"a": "1, 3 and 4 are correct", "b": "2 and 3 are correct", "c": "1 and 3 are correct", "d": "1 and 4 are correct"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 3 are correct. Topographical (topographic) organisation means that neighbouring points on a sensory surface are represented by neighbouring points in the brain, so each sensory cortex carries an orderly map: retinotopic in vision (the primary visual cortex maps the retina, with the small fovea given a disproportionately large share), tonotopic in hearing (each sound frequency has its own place in auditory cortex) and somatotopic in touch (the body map, or homunculus, of the postcentral gyrus). So statement 1, that it applies to sensory cortical areas, is true. Statement 3 describes the visual pathway: the fibres of the optic nerve are the axons of the retina's ganglion cells, which carry the signal out of the eye, so it is true. Statement 4 is false: optic tract fibres synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus (body) of the thalamus, not of the hypothalamus, before the optic radiations carry the signal to the striate cortex (area 17). The hypothalamus receives only a small retinal input, to its suprachiasmatic nucleus, for setting circadian rhythms. Example: a small stroke in one spot of Arjun's left primary visual cortex blinds one matching patch of his right visual field, because the map keeps retinal neighbours together. Option c is right because 1 and 3 are true and 4 is false. Exam trap: the lateral geniculate body sounds hypothalamic because both lie in the diencephalon, but the geniculate bodies are thalamic relays (lateral for vision, medial for hearing). Key note: statement 2 names the auditory pathway, which is tonotopic in reality, but statement 3 then says 'this pathway' starts in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot both describe the same pathway; the paired statements describe the visual pathway and c (1 and 3) is the only fully correct set; c stands.", "topic": "Topographic maps, visual pathway", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Book authored) with List-II (Psychologist): a. The Meaning of Anxiety, b. Man's Search for Meaning, c. On Becoming a Person, d. A Theory of Human Motivation. 1. Abraham Maslow, 2. Rollo May, 3. Victor Frankl, 4. Carl Rogers", "options": {"a": "a-2, b-3, c-4, d-1", "b": "a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1", "c": "a-1, b-3, c-4, d-2", "d": "a-4, b-2, c-3, d-1"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: The Meaning of Anxiety is by Rollo May (2), Man's Search for Meaning by Viktor Frankl (3), On Becoming a Person by Carl Rogers (4) and 'A Theory of Human Motivation' by Abraham Maslow (1). Rollo May (1950), in The Meaning of Anxiety, separated normal anxiety, which fits the real threat and can be faced constructively, from neurotic anxiety, which is out of proportion to the threat and leads to repression; May did much to bring European existential thought into American psychology. Viktor Frankl wrote Man's Search for Meaning (first published in German in 1946) after surviving the Nazi concentration camps; it argues that the will to meaning is the primary human drive and sets out his logotherapy. Carl Rogers's On Becoming a Person (1961) collects his essays on the fully functioning person and on the conditions for growth in client-centred therapy: empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence. Abraham Maslow's 'A Theory of Human Motivation' (Psychological Review, 1943) is the paper that introduced the hierarchy of needs, rising from physiological needs through safety, love and esteem to self-actualisation. Example: Meera remembers the set by theme: May for anxiety, Frankl for meaning, Rogers for becoming, Maslow for motivation. Option a is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: 'meaning' appears in two titles, so May and Frankl are easily swapped; anxiety is May's subject, while meaning found even in suffering is Frankl's.", "topic": "Humanistic and existential books", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Put the parts of the brain in sequence, going from higher to lower level of brain: 1. Metencephalon 2. Mesencephalon 3. Diancephalon 4. Telencephalon 5. Myelencephalon", "options": {"a": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "b": "2, 4, 1, 3, 5", "c": "4, 3, 2, 1, 5", "d": "3, 2, 1, 5, 4"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: from higher to lower the order is telencephalon (4), diencephalon (3), mesencephalon (2), metencephalon (1) and myelencephalon (5). The names come from embryology. Early in development the front end of the neural tube forms three swellings: the forebrain (prosencephalon), the midbrain (mesencephalon) and the hindbrain (rhombencephalon). Before birth the forebrain divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon, and the hindbrain into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon, giving five divisions stacked from the top of the brain down to the spinal cord. The telencephalon becomes the cerebral hemispheres: cortex, basal ganglia, limbic structures and corpus callosum. The diencephalon ('between-brain') becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain, with its tectum (the superior and inferior colliculi) and tegmentum. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon ('marrow-brain') becomes the medulla oblongata, which joins the spinal cord. Example: Arjun remembers the order with a simple rule: telencephalon on top, then the other four in alphabetical order (di-, mes-, met-, myel-). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that runs from the cerebral hemispheres down to the medulla. Exam trap: metencephalon and mesencephalon differ by one letter; mes- is the midbrain, and met- is the pons and cerebellum below it. Option d keeps the middle of the order but drops the telencephalon to the bottom.", "topic": "Five divisions of the brain", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Working memory can hold large amount of information even though it can retain only seven to nine items. Reason (R): Items in working memory contain several separate bits of information grouped together called chunks.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and chunking (R) explains how a store of about seven items can hold a large amount of information (A). Assertion: George Miller (1956), in 'The magical number seven, plus or minus two', showed that the span of immediate memory is about seven units, give or take two. The surprise was that the limit is set in units, not in amounts of information: people hold about seven digits, or seven letters, or seven words, though seven words carry far more information than seven digits. So a small store can hold a lot. A is true. Reason: the units are chunks, separate bits bound into one meaningful group by knowledge already in long-term memory. I N D I A I I T I S R O is twelve letters but only three chunks (INDIA, IIT, ISRO). Recoding into bigger chunks is how chess masters remember whole board positions at a glance. R is true. R explains A: because capacity is counted in chunks and each chunk can pack many bits, the amount held can be large. Example: Riya cannot repeat ten random digits heard once, but she holds her friend's mobile number easily as three chunks, 984 621 7305, while she dials. Option a is right because R gives the very mechanism that makes A possible. Exam trap: 'seven to nine items' and 'large amount of information' seem to clash, so students mark A false; the clash disappears once items are understood as chunks. Key note: the span is usually quoted as 5 to 9 (seven plus or minus two), later estimates such as Nelson Cowan's (2001) put it nearer 4 chunks, and Miller wrote of short-term (immediate) memory rather than working memory; the A-R logic holds, so a stands.", "topic": "Chunking and memory span", "fig": "George Miller"}, {"q": "The F-test:", "options": {"a": "Is essentially a two-tailed test", "b": "Is essentially a one-tailed test", "c": "Can be one-tailed as well as two-tailed depending on the hypothesis", "d": "Can never be one-tailed test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the F-test can be one-tailed or two-tailed, depending on the hypothesis being tested. F is a ratio of two variance estimates; it grew out of Ronald Fisher's analysis of variance work in the 1920s and was named F in his honour by George Snedecor (1934). Because it is a ratio of variances, the F distribution is never negative and is positively skewed, its shape set by two degrees of freedom (numerator and denominator). Which tails matter depends on the alternative hypothesis (H1). When the variances of two groups are compared, H0 says they are equal. If H1 says only that they differ, a ratio far above 1 or far below 1 counts against H0, so the test is two-tailed; in practice the larger variance is put on top and the critical value for alpha divided by 2 is used. If H1 says that one named variance is larger, only large ratios count, so the test is one-tailed. In ANOVA, F is the mean square between groups over the mean square within groups, and only a large F counts against H0, so the ANOVA F-test is right-tailed only. Example: Kabir asks whether two teaching methods differ in how spread out exam marks are, without predicting which; his F-test of the two variances is two-tailed. Option c is right because the number of tails follows from H1, not from the statistic. Exam trap: ANOVA is so often taught as the only use of F that students carry its right-tailed test over to every F-test. Key note: many psychology texts call the ANOVA F 'essentially one-tailed', which tempts b; the question asks about the F-test in general, and the variance-ratio test can be one- or two-tailed depending on the hypothesis, so c stands.", "topic": "Tails of the F-test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which option is not associated with a quantitative approach?", "options": {"a": "Rigid", "b": "Structured", "c": "Predetermined", "d": "Narrative"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'narrative' is not associated with the quantitative approach; it belongs to qualitative research. Quantitative research measures variables as numbers and tests hypotheses statistically. It rests on positivism and postpositivism, reasons deductively from theory to hypothesis, and fixes its design before any data are collected: the sample, the instruments, the procedure and the analysis plan are all set in advance and then followed exactly. That is why it is described as structured (standardised tools and procedures), predetermined (questions and hypotheses fixed beforehand) and rigid or fixed (the design is not changed once data collection starts). Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and process through words, images and observation. Its designs are flexible and emergent, the researcher is the instrument, and its data are often stories. Narrative research, one of the five qualitative approaches John Creswell (2007) set beside phenomenology, grounded theory, ethnography and case study, collects and interprets the life stories people tell. Example: Meera's quantitative study gives a fixed 20-item anxiety scale to 300 nurses and runs a t-test; her colleague's narrative study asks six nurses to tell the story of their first night shift and analyses those stories. Option d is right because narrative data and narrative inquiry are qualitative, neither numeric nor fixed in advance. Exam trap: 'rigid' sounds like a criticism, so students suspect it is the odd one out, but textbooks use it plainly to describe quantitative designs.", "topic": "Quantitative versus qualitative features", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is one type of mixed method design?", "options": {"a": "Exploring", "b": "Convergent", "c": "Explicit", "d": "Connecting"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the convergent design is one of the core mixed methods designs. Mixed methods research collects and analyses both quantitative and qualitative data in one study and integrates them, on the pragmatist view that the research question should choose the methods. John Creswell and Vicki Plano Clark wrote Designing and Conducting Mixed Methods Research (2007; later editions 2011 and 2018), and their 2018 edition names three core designs. In the convergent (parallel) design, both kinds of data are collected at about the same time, analysed separately and then merged to see whether they agree; their first edition called it the triangulation design. In the explanatory sequential design, a quantitative phase comes first and a qualitative phase follows to explain its results. In the exploratory sequential design, a qualitative phase comes first to explore a topic, and a quantitative phase follows, for example to build and test an instrument. Their earlier editions also listed embedded, transformative and multiphase designs, which build on these three. Example: Kabir surveys 400 hostel students on loneliness and, in the same month, interviews 15 of them; he then sets the survey statistics beside the interview themes to see whether they converge. Option b is right because convergent is the name of an actual mixed methods design. Exam trap: 'exploring' and 'connecting' sound like design names because they echo real terms (the exploratory sequential design, and connecting as one way of integrating data), but neither is itself a design. Key note: LBS's first key for this paper (29 Nov 2021) marked b, but its revised key (6 Dec 2021) changed it to a, exploring. The exploratory sequential design is real, so a can be argued, but 'convergent' is the exact name of one of Creswell and Plano Clark's three core designs and 'exploring' is not, so b stays.", "topic": "Mixed methods designs", "fig": "John Creswell and Vicki Plano Clark"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a non-parametric test?", "options": {"a": "Analysis of covariance", "b": "Analysis of variance", "c": "Median test", "d": "Critical ratio"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the median test is a non-parametric test. A parametric test estimates population parameters such as means and variances and assumes that scores come from a normally distributed population, that group variances are roughly equal, that observations are independent and that the dependent variable is on an interval or ratio scale. A non-parametric (distribution-free) test makes few assumptions about the population's shape and works on ranks, signs or frequency counts; Sidney Siegel (1956) made such tests popular in psychology. The median test, usually credited to Alexander Mood (1950), finds the combined median of all scores from two or more independent groups, counts how many scores in each group fall above and below it, and tests the resulting table with chi-square. It needs only ordinal data and is unaffected by extreme scores. Example: Riya compares the time taken to fall asleep by students in three hostels; a few students take hours, so the data are badly skewed, and she uses the median test instead of comparing means. Option c is right because it is the only test listed that works on counts above and below a median rather than on means and variances. Exam trap: the critical ratio sounds unfamiliar and so seems non-parametric, but it is the large-sample z-test of a difference between means, which is parametric. Other non-parametric tests often listed beside the median test are the Mann-Whitney U, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Kruskal-Wallis and Friedman tests.", "topic": "Parametric and non-parametric tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Kruskal Wallis test is the non parametric alternative to the:", "options": {"a": "Factorial design", "b": "One-way ANOVA", "c": "t-test", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric alternative to the one-way (independent) ANOVA. William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952) designed it for three or more independent groups measured on one dependent variable, when the data are ordinal or clearly break ANOVA's assumptions of normality and equal variances. All the scores are pooled and ranked from lowest to highest, and the ranks are then added up within each group. If the groups do not differ, their mean ranks should be similar; the statistic H measures how far the mean ranks spread apart and is read against chi-square with k minus 1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of groups. That is why it is called the one-way ANOVA by ranks. Like ANOVA, a significant H shows only that at least one group differs, so follow-up comparisons are needed to find which. Example: Arjun compares pain ratings on a 0 to 10 scale from patients in three wards; the samples are small and skewed, so he ranks all the ratings together and runs a Kruskal-Wallis test. Option b is right because both tests compare three or more independent groups on one factor. Exam trap: the t-test also has rank-based partners, but they are different tests: the Mann-Whitney U for two independent groups and the Wilcoxon signed-rank test for two related samples. For three or more related conditions, the rank test is Friedman's.", "topic": "Kruskal-Wallis test", "fig": "William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis"}, {"q": "A researcher measured the same group of people's physiological reactions while watching horror films and compared them to when watching erotic films and a documentary about wild life. The resulting data were skewed. What test should be used to analyse the data?", "options": {"a": "Independent analysis of variance", "b": "Repeated-measures analysis of variance", "c": "Friedman's ANOVA", "d": "Kruskal-Wallis test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the same people were measured under three conditions and the data were skewed, so Friedman's ANOVA is the right test. Choosing a test starts with two questions: how many conditions are compared, and are they independent groups or repeated measures? Here one group watched horror films, erotic films and a wildlife documentary, so there are three related conditions, a repeated-measures design. If the scores were normal, a repeated-measures ANOVA would fit, but it assumes normally distributed scores (and sphericity), and these data are skewed. The non-parametric answer is the Friedman test, devised by the economist Milton Friedman (1937). It ranks each person's scores across the conditions (1, 2, 3), then sums the ranks for each condition; if the conditions do not differ, the rank sums should be similar. The statistic is read against chi-square with k minus 1 degrees of freedom, which is why it is also called the two-way ANOVA by ranks. Example: Meera records heart rate in 12 students during each type of film; one student's heart rate soars during horror, skewing the data, so she ranks the three readings within each student and runs Friedman's ANOVA. Option c is right because it is the rank-based test for three or more related conditions. Exam trap: Kruskal-Wallis is the best-known non-parametric 'ANOVA', but it is for independent groups; the deciding phrase in the stem is 'the same group of people'.", "topic": "Friedman's ANOVA", "fig": "Milton Friedman"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"imhans-2021": [{"q": "Genogram was originated by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the genogram grew out of the family systems work of Murray Bowen. A genogram is a family diagram that maps at least three generations: squares for males, circles for females, horizontal lines for marriages, lines down to children, and special lines for closeness, conflict and emotional cutoff. Murray Bowen (1978), an American psychiatrist, built family systems theory in the 1950s after admitting whole families of patients with schizophrenia to a research ward. He argued that anxiety and ways of relating pass down the generations (multigenerational transmission), so the therapist must look at the wider family, not only the person with the symptom. His main ideas include differentiation of self (thinking clearly while staying emotionally connected), triangles (a tense pair pulls in a third person, often a child) and emotional cutoff. The family diagram was his tool for tracking these patterns. Example: Riya's therapist draws her genogram and notices that in three generations the eldest daughter took on the caretaking role and then cut off from her mother; the pattern, not Riya alone, becomes the focus. Option b is right because the genogram is the signature assessment tool of Bowen's transgenerational school. Exam trap: genograms are now used everywhere (case histories, social work, structural and strategic family therapy), so examiners offer other famous names from therapy; only Bowen is credited with its origin. Key note: the standard symbols and format were set out later by Monica McGoldrick and Randy Gerson in Genograms in Family Assessment (1985), so some books link the genogram with them; its origin is credited to Bowen, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Franz Alexander was a psychoanalyst who founded psychosomatic medicine and coined the 'corrective emotional experience'; he treated individuals and devised no family diagram.", "c": "William Masters and Virginia Johnson studied the human sexual response (Human Sexual Response, 1966) and built couple-based sex therapy with sensate focus; the genogram is not theirs.", "d": "James Mann devised time-limited psychotherapy (1973), a 12-session brief dynamic therapy built around one central issue; it is an individual therapy with no family-mapping tool."}}, {"q": "The Vygosky's stage which is similar to Piaget's concentration and conservation:", "concept": "The answer is c: the potential concept stage is the stage of Lev Vygotsky (1934) usually set beside the centration and conservation of Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980). In Thinking and Speech (1934), Vygotsky studied concept formation with a block-sorting task refined by his colleague Leonid Sakharov. Blocks of different colours, shapes, heights and sizes carry nonsense words on their undersides, and the child must work out which blocks belong together. Vygotsky described a sequence. First come vague syncretic images, or heaps: objects lumped together by chance impression or position. Next is thinking in complexes: groups built on concrete, shifting links (a red block, then a red triangle, then any triangle), ending in the pseudo-concept, which looks like a true concept but rests on visible likeness. Then come potential concepts: the child abstracts one attribute (all the tall ones) and groups on it, but can hold only one attribute at a time. Last, in adolescence, mature (true) concepts combine abstracted attributes into a rule-based system tied to a word. Piaget's preoperational child shows centration, fixing on one striking dimension (the height of the water) and so failing conservation. Example: Arjun sorts the blocks by height alone and ignores width even when width also matters. Option c is right because grouping on a single attribute at a time is the Vygotskian parallel to centring on a single dimension. Exam trap: the complex stage contains the pseudo-concept, which looks advanced and tempts. Key note: the booklet's 'concentration' is read as Piaget's centration; the potential concept stage, where only one attribute is held at a time, is the stage usually set beside centration and the conservation tasks that expose it, though some guides pair the mature concept stage with decentration; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The mature (true) concept stage comes last, in adolescence, when several abstracted attributes are combined into a rule-based system; if anything it matches decentration, not centration.", "b": "Vague syncretic images (heaps) are the earliest stage, where objects are lumped together by chance or nearness; no attribute is singled out, so there is nothing like centration on one dimension.", "d": "Thinking in complexes groups objects by concrete, changing links, and its top form, the pseudo-concept, only looks like a concept; it is not the one-attribute abstraction that parallels centration."}}, {"q": "The first attempt for standardization of mental tests in India was made by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the first attempt to standardise a mental test in India was made by C. Herbert Rice (1922). Standardisation means giving a test to a large, representative sample under uniform conditions, so that norms can be built and any one score can be read against them. Intelligence testing began with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) in Paris, and Binet-type scales soon spread across the world. In India, C. Herbert Rice, working in Lahore, began adapting the Binet scale for Indian children in 1922, work that produced the Hindustani Binet Performance Point Scale. As its name says, the scale put Binet-type items into Hindustani, added performance tasks and scored the whole by points, and it was tried out on Indian school children to give local norms. That makes it the first Indian adaptation and standardisation of a Binet-type test. Later Indian work built on it. V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test), and S. M. Mohsin and Sohan Lal developed Hindi group tests of general mental ability. Example: if Meera's teacher wants to know whether Meera's score is high, she needs norms from Indian children like Meera, and Rice's scale was the first to offer them. Option c is right because Rice's work, begun in 1922, came before the work of Kamat, Mohsin and Sohan Lal. Exam trap: the Binet-Kamat test is the best known Indian Binet, so it tempts as 'the first'; it was a later revision, not the first attempt.", "traps": {"a": "S. M. Mohsin built a Hindi group test of general intelligence; it is an important Indian test but came well after Rice's 1922 scale.", "b": "V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test); it is the best known Indian Binet revision, but it followed Rice's work.", "d": "Sohan Lal developed a Hindi group test of general mental ability later; it is a standard Indian group test, not the first attempt at standardisation."}}, {"q": "According to psychodynamic theory, individuals are sometimes motivated to respond to the world through the perspectives they have learnt from important other people in their developmental past. This is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: this is object relations theory. In psychodynamic language an 'object' is not a thing but a person, or part of a person, towards whom feelings and wishes are directed, usually the mother or another early caregiver. Object relations theory holds that the infant takes in (internalises) mental pictures of the self and of important others from early relationships, along with the feelings that link them. These internal objects then act as templates: later in life the person sees and responds to partners, bosses and therapists through the perspectives learnt from those early figures. The school grew in Britain after Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). Melanie Klein (1882 to 1960) described splitting of the world into good and bad objects and the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions; W. R. D. Fairbairn (1952) argued that the infant is object-seeking rather than pleasure-seeking; Donald Winnicott (1953) described the transitional object and the good-enough mother; Otto Kernberg (1975) linked split internal object relations to borderline personality organisation. Example: Kabir grew up with a harsh, critical father, and as an adult he hears criticism in every neutral remark from his manager, reacting to the internal father image rather than to the manager. Option c is right because the stem describes exactly this internalised, relationship-based template learnt from important others. Exam trap: 'object permanence' shares the word 'object' but belongs to Jean Piaget's (1896 to 1980) sensorimotor stage, where the infant learns that things go on existing when out of sight.", "traps": {"a": "Object permanence is Jean Piaget's sensorimotor achievement (knowing that things exist when unseen); it is a cognitive milestone, not a psychodynamic theory of relating to others.", "b": "'Distorted perspective theory' is not a recognised psychodynamic theory; it is a made-up distractor built from the stem's word 'perspectives'.", "d": "Ego defence refers to defence mechanisms such as repression and projection, set out by Anna Freud (1936); they protect the ego from anxiety rather than carry learnt templates of important others."}}, {"q": "Which of the following test will you use to find out whether children's motivation to succeed at school is related to the size, ethnicity, and socio-economic status of their families?", "concept": "The answer is c: multiple regression is the test to use. Multiple regression predicts one continuous outcome (the criterion) from two or more predictors at once. It produces an equation of the form Y = a + b1X1 + b2X2 + b3X3, where each b (a regression coefficient) shows how much the outcome changes with that predictor while the others are held constant. R squared shows how much of the variance in the outcome all the predictors explain together, and a test on each coefficient shows which predictors matter. Predictors can be continuous (family size, income) or categorical once dummy-coded (ethnic groups entered as 0 and 1 codes). Here the outcome is children's motivation to succeed at school, a scale score, and there are three family predictors: size (a count), ethnicity (categorical) and socio-economic status (often a continuous index). The question asks whether motivation is related to all three, which is exactly what one regression model tests. Example: Riya collects motivation scores from 300 pupils in Kochi and enters family size, ethnicity codes and SES together; she finds that SES predicts motivation even with family size held constant. Option c is right because it is the only listed technique that relates one continuous outcome to several predictors of mixed type at once. Exam trap: the words 'related to' tempt chi-square, but chi-square works on frequencies in categories, not on a motivation score. Key note: two-way ANOVA, the option ticked on the copy, handles exactly two categorical factors; with three predictors, some of them continuous, multiple regression is the standard answer, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Chi-square (Karl Pearson, 1900) tests association between categorical variables using frequency counts; motivation is a score, and family size and SES are not simple categories.", "b": "The Kruskal-Wallis test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952) compares ranks across three or more independent groups on one factor; it cannot weigh three predictors at once.", "d": "Two-way ANOVA tests exactly two categorical factors and their interaction on a continuous outcome; here there are three predictors, and family size and SES are continuous."}}, {"q": "The ------ can be defined as the sequence of identical occurrence of the element (numbers or symbols), preceded or followed by different occurrence of the elements or by no elements at all.", "concept": "The answer is b: the definition describes a run. A run is an unbroken sequence of identical symbols, preceded and followed by a different symbol or by nothing at all (the start or end of the series). In the coin series H H T T T H there are three runs: HH, TTT and H. Counting runs is the basis of the runs test, a non-parametric test of randomness. If a sequence has too few runs, like symbols cluster together (H H H H T T T T); if it has too many, they alternate too regularly (H T H T H T); either way the order does not look random. The one-sample runs test checks whether a series of events (right and wrong answers, men and women arriving at a clinic) occurs in random order. Abraham Wald and Jacob Wolfowitz (1940) turned runs into a two-sample test: pool the scores of two groups, put them in order, label each score by its group and count the runs; few runs mean the two groups differ. Example: Kabir notes whether each patient entering the OPD is a man (M) or a woman (W): M M W W W M W W M M. Each change of letter starts a new run, so he counts 5 runs and compares that number with the table for chance. Option b is right because the stem gives the standard definition of a run, the unit the runs test counts. Exam trap: the other three options are named test statistics (H, U and t), so a student who half-remembers 'non-parametric test' may grab one; a run is a pattern in a sequence, not a test statistic.", "traps": {"a": "Kruskal-Wallis H (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952) is a rank-based test comparing three or more independent groups; it is a test statistic, not a sequence of identical symbols.", "c": "Mann-Whitney U (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares the ranks of two independent groups by counting how often scores in one group exceed those in the other, not by counting runs.", "d": "Student's t (William Sealy Gosset, 1908) is the ratio of a mean difference to its standard error; it has nothing to do with sequences of symbols."}}, {"q": "An analogue observation enables:", "concept": "The answer is a: analogue observation enables surreptitious observation of the client. In behavioural assessment the clinician watches behaviour directly rather than relying only on what the client reports. Naturalistic observation happens in the real setting (home, classroom, ward). Analogue observation happens in a contrived, controlled setting built to resemble the real one: a clinic playroom where a parent and child carry out a set task, or a role-played social situation in which a client must refuse a request or start a conversation. Because the setting is arranged in advance, the clinician can watch from behind a one-way mirror or through a video link, unseen and unobtrusive, which is what 'surreptitious' means. This reduces reactivity (people behaving differently because they know they are being watched) and lets behaviour be coded in a standard way. The cost is generalisation: behaviour in a clinic room may not match behaviour at home. Example: Meera's parents say she refuses every instruction, so the psychologist asks her mother to have Meera tidy toys in a playroom and records Meera's compliance through a one-way mirror. Option a is right because the controlled analogue setting is what makes hidden, unobtrusive observation of the client possible. Exam trap: the word 'analogue' also appears in analogue research, where non-clinical people stand in for patients; that is a research strategy, not an observation method. Key note: the booklet prints 'Superstitious', plainly a slip for 'surreptitious' (unseen, unobtrusive), and the option is shown corrected; b describes analogue research, a different use of the word, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Using non-clinical people as stand-ins for a clinical group (snake-fearful students for phobic patients) is analogue research, a research design, not what analogue observation of a client enables.", "c": "Watching several clients at once belongs to group settings such as group therapy or classroom observation; it is not the defining advantage of an analogue setting.", "d": "Analogue observation needs only one observer behind a mirror or a video record; requiring three or more clinicians is not part of the method."}}, {"q": "An IQ test that does not provide which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is d: an IQ test does not provide a good prediction of everyday behaviour. Modern intelligence tests such as the Wechsler scales (David Wechsler, 1939 onwards) and the Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916) are among the best built tools in psychology. Their full-scale IQs show high test-retest reliability, usually .90 or more, so scores stay stable over weeks and years. They show high internal consistency, because their items and subtests cohere in measuring general ability. They also have good validity for what they were built to predict: school grades and educational attainment, and to a fair degree job performance. What they do poorly is predict how a person will actually behave in daily life: social conduct, emotional adjustment, everyday adaptive behaviour, or how someone copes in a crisis. Behaviour depends on motivation, personality, emotional and social skills, culture and the situation, none of which an IQ score captures. That is why a diagnosis of intellectual disability also needs a separate measure of adaptive behaviour, such as the Vineland scales. Example: Arjun scores 125 on the WAIS, yet he struggles to keep friends and cannot manage his money; his IQ did not forecast these everyday difficulties. Option d is right because reliability, internal consistency and validity are strengths of IQ tests, while broad prediction of behaviour is their known limit. Exam trap: 'good validity' tempts because IQ tests are often criticised, but the criticism is about cultural bias and narrowness, not poor validity for academic outcomes.", "traps": {"a": "IQ tests do have good validity, especially criterion validity for school achievement and educational attainment, so validity is something they provide.", "b": "Full-scale IQ is highly stable over time, with test-retest coefficients around .90 and above, so high test-retest reliability is a strength, not what is missing.", "c": "The items and subtests of IQ tests cohere strongly in measuring general ability, giving high internal consistency (split-half and alpha coefficients often above .90)."}}, {"q": "If two variables, X and Y, have a very strong linear relationship, then:", "concept": "The answer is c: even a very strong linear relationship between X and Y might involve no causal relationship at all. Correlation measures how closely two variables move together. Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment r runs from minus 1 to plus 1, and a value near either end means the points lie close to a straight line. But r says nothing about why the variables move together. Two problems block a causal reading. The directionality problem: X may cause Y, or Y may cause X, and the correlation is the same either way. The third-variable problem: an unmeasured factor Z may drive both, so X and Y rise together with no direct link. Ice cream sales and drowning deaths correlate strongly because hot weather raises both. Some strong correlations are pure coincidence, especially when many variables are searched. Only a true experiment, in which the researcher manipulates X, controls other variables and assigns people at random, licenses a cause-and-effect conclusion. Example: Riya finds r = .85 between daily screen time and anxiety scores in college students; anxiety might drive screen use, screen use might raise anxiety, or poor sleep might cause both. Option c is right because 'there might not be any causal relationship' is the honest conclusion from correlational data. Exam trap: options a and b each pick one direction, and a strong r makes them sound convincing; the strength of an association is not evidence of causation in either direction.", "traps": {"a": "Claiming that X causes Y from a correlation ignores both the directionality problem (Y could cause X) and the third-variable problem (Z could cause both).", "b": "This only reverses option a; a correlation is symmetrical and cannot show that Y causes X any more than that X causes Y.", "d": "Option c is a correct statement about correlational data, so 'none of these alternatives is correct' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "ANCOVA is:", "concept": "The answer is d: ANCOVA is an extension of ANOVA that partials out the effect of other measured variables. Analysis of covariance, developed by Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962), combines analysis of variance with regression. Alongside the grouping variable (the IV), the researcher measures one or more continuous covariates known to affect the outcome, such as pre-test score, age or IQ. ANCOVA uses regression to remove the part of the outcome that the covariate predicts, then compares the groups on adjusted means, as if every group had the same covariate score. This does two things. It reduces error variance, so the test gains power, and it statistically adjusts for groups that started out unequal on the covariate. It cannot replace random assignment, and it adjusts only for variables that were actually measured and entered. Example: Kabir compares three teaching methods on final maths scores, but the three classes began with different maths ability; with the pre-test score as the covariate, ANCOVA compares the methods on adjusted final scores. Option d is right because partialling out the effect of a measured covariate is the defining feature of ANCOVA. Exam trap: option a sounds almost the same but says 'unmeasured'; ANCOVA can remove only the influence of variables entered as covariates, and unmeasured confounds are exactly what it cannot fix.", "traps": {"a": "ANCOVA corrects only for measured covariates entered into the analysis; unmeasured variables stay uncontrolled, which is why random assignment is still needed.", "b": "Breaking down a significant ANOVA to find which groups differ describes post hoc tests (such as John Tukey's HSD) or planned contrasts, not ANCOVA.", "c": "A robust version of ANOVA, one that resists violated assumptions, is something like Welch's F or ANOVA on trimmed means; ANCOVA keeps ANOVA's assumptions and adds more."}}, {"q": "The assumption which does ANCOVA have and ANOVA does not have:", "concept": "The answer is d: homogeneity of regression slopes is the assumption ANCOVA has and ANOVA does not. ANCOVA, developed by Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962), adjusts group means for a covariate by fitting a regression of the outcome on the covariate and using one pooled slope for all groups. Pooling only makes sense if the covariate relates to the outcome in the same way in every group, so the groups' regression lines must be parallel. If the slopes differ, there is a group by covariate interaction: the effect of the treatment depends on the level of the covariate, and a single adjustment cannot fit every group. Researchers check this by adding the group x covariate interaction term to the model; a non-significant interaction supports parallel slopes. ANCOVA also keeps all of ANOVA's assumptions (normality, independence, homogeneity of variance) and adds a linear covariate-outcome relationship and a reliably measured covariate, ideally recorded before treatment. Example: Meera uses ANCOVA, with baseline anxiety as the covariate, to compare yoga, CBT and a waitlist. If baseline anxiety predicted final anxiety strongly in the CBT group but only weakly in the yoga group, the slopes would be unequal and the adjusted means misleading. Option d is right because only ANCOVA involves regression slopes at all, so this assumption cannot belong to plain ANOVA. Exam trap: homogeneity of variance is the best known ANOVA assumption and tempts, but ANCOVA shares it rather than adding it.", "traps": {"a": "Homogeneity of variance (equal error variances across groups, checked with Howard Levene's test) is an assumption of ANOVA as well, so it is shared, not unique to ANCOVA.", "b": "Homoscedasticity is the regression name for equal spread of residuals; ANOVA assumes the same thing as homogeneity of variance, so it is not ANCOVA's extra assumption.", "c": "'Homogeneity of sample' is not a formal assumption of either analysis; observations are assumed independent and randomly sampled, not homogeneous."}}, {"q": "A Latin Square in which the treatments in the first row and in the first column are arranged in alphabetical order or numerical order.", "concept": "The answer is b: a Latin square whose first row and first column are in natural (alphabetical or numerical) order is a standard Latin square. A Latin square is an n x n arrangement of n treatments in which each treatment appears exactly once in every row and exactly once in every column. Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962) brought it into agricultural field trials, where rows and columns controlled two directions of soil variation. In psychology the rows are often participants or groups and the columns the order of testing, so the square controls two nuisance variables at once and balances order effects. Many different squares of a given size exist. To list and choose among them, statisticians use the standard form (also called reduced or normalised), in which both the first row and the first column read A B C D, or 1 2 3 4. Any Latin square can be turned into a standard one by reordering its rows and columns, and in practice the chosen square is then randomised by shuffling rows, columns and treatment labels. A 4 x 4 standard square: row 1 A B C D, row 2 B C D A, row 3 C D A B, row 4 D A B C; the first row and the first column are both in order. Example: Arjun tests four memory strategies on four participants across four sessions and starts from this standard square before randomising it. Option b is right because 'standard' is the technical name for exactly this ordered form. Exam trap: 'simple' sounds like a plain, basic square, but it is not the technical term for the ordered form.", "traps": {"a": "'Simple Latin square' is not the technical term for the ordered form; any square is just a Latin square until its first row and first column are put in order.", "c": "A partial Latin square is a grid that is only partly filled in (no symbol repeated in a row or column); it does not describe the ordered first row and column.", "d": "A double or Graeco-Latin square superimposes two orthogonal Latin squares (say Latin and Greek letters) so each pair occurs once; it controls an extra variable and has nothing to do with ordering."}}, {"q": "In LSD the number of treatment, rows and columns are:", "concept": "The answer is c: in a Latin square design (LSD) the number of treatments, rows and columns are equal. A Latin square is an n x n grid: n rows, n columns and n treatments, with each treatment appearing exactly once in each row and once in each column. This balance is what lets the design control two nuisance variables at the same time. Ronald Fisher (1890 to 1962) developed it for crop trials in which the rows and columns of a field differed in fertility. In psychology the rows usually stand for participants or groups and the columns for the order of testing; every condition then appears once in each position, which counterbalances practice and fatigue with only n orders instead of every possible order (4 conditions need 4 orders, not 24). If the numbers were unequal, some treatment would have to appear twice in a row or be missing from a column, and the design would no longer be a Latin square. Example: Kabir compares three relaxation methods (A, B, C) with three participants over three sessions; the square A B C, B C A, C A B has 3 treatments, 3 rows and 3 columns. Option c is right because equal numbers of treatments, rows and columns are part of the definition, not a habit. Exam trap: 'usually equal' sounds careful and scholarly, but there is no 'usually' about it; a layout with unequal numbers is a different design, such as a Youden square or an incomplete block design.", "traps": {"a": "'Seldom equal' reverses the definition; a Latin square requires the number of treatments, rows and columns to be the same.", "b": "'Usually equal' implies exceptions, but an n x n Latin square has none; layouts with unequal numbers have other names.", "d": "If treatments, rows and columns were unequal, each treatment could not appear exactly once in every row and every column, so the layout would not be a Latin square."}}, {"q": "------ research is based on naturalism.", "concept": "The answer is a: field research is based on naturalism. Naturalism is the principle that behaviour should be studied in its natural setting, as it ordinarily happens, with as little interference from the researcher as possible. Field research takes the researcher out of the laboratory into homes, schools, factories, wards and streets. Its forms range from naturalistic observation (watching children in a playground) and ethnography (living with a community to understand it from the inside) to field studies and field experiments. Research methods texts separate field studies, which manipulate nothing and study relationships as they exist in real social settings, from field experiments, which manipulate a variable in a real setting. The strength of this naturalism is ecological and external validity: the findings reflect real life. The cost is weaker control over extraneous variables, so causal conclusions are harder to draw. Example: Meera wants to understand how patients spend their day in a rehabilitation centre in Kozhikode; instead of handing out a questionnaire in her office, she spends weeks on the ward, observing and taking notes without changing any routine. Option a is right because studying people in their natural environment, without artificial manipulation, is what defines field research. Exam trap: descriptive research also describes behaviour as it is, so it tempts; but 'descriptive' is defined by its aim (describing what exists) in any setting, while naturalism is the setting principle of field research.", "traps": {"b": "Descriptive research aims to describe the characteristics of a population or phenomenon (surveys, case studies) and can be done in any setting; it is defined by its purpose, not by naturalism.", "c": "Basic (pure) research aims to build theory and knowledge for its own sake, often in a laboratory; its defining feature is its goal, not a natural setting.", "d": "Applied research aims to solve a practical problem, such as improving a school programme; it may use any setting, so it is not defined by naturalism."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements are true? 1. Classical conditioning regulates reflexive, involuntary responses exclusively. 2. Classical conditioning can increase sexual arousal. 3. Operant conditioning regulates voluntary responses exclusively. 4. The distinction between the two types of conditioning is not absolute.", "concept": "The answer is c: only statements 2 and 4 are true. Classical conditioning, studied by Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936), pairs a neutral stimulus with one that already triggers a response, until the neutral stimulus triggers it too. Operant conditioning, studied by Edward Thorndike (1898) and B. F. Skinner (1938), changes behaviour through its consequences. Standard accounts say classical conditioning mainly governs reflexive, involuntary responses (salivation, fear, heart rate) and operant conditioning mainly governs voluntary ones (pressing a lever, studying). Statements 1 and 3 fail on the word 'exclusively'. In autoshaping, a pigeon's key-peck, a skeletal response, is produced by classical pairing of a key light with food. In biofeedback, autonomic responses such as heart rate and skin temperature are changed by operant reinforcement, following Neal Miller's (1969) claim that visceral responses can be operantly conditioned. So statement 4 is true: the distinction is not absolute, and the two processes interact in everyday learning. Statement 2 is true as well: sexual arousal can be classically conditioned. Stanley Rachman (1966) conditioned mild sexual arousal in men to a picture of boots by pairing it with erotic slides, and Michael Domjan's work in the 1980s showed conditioned sexual responses in male quail to a cue that signalled a female. Example: Arjun learns to feel his heart race (classical) when he sees his strict teacher, and then starts avoiding her corridor (operant) because avoiding her brings relief. Option c is right because it keeps the two true statements and drops the two 'exclusively' claims. Exam trap: 'exclusively' turns a broadly true generalisation into a false statement, so read absolute words carefully. Key note: LBS's first key for this paper (29 Nov 2021) marked c, but its revised key (6 Dec 2021) changed it to a, statements 1, 2 and 3. The revised key is wrong: statements 1 and 3 say 'exclusively', which autoshaping and biofeedback disprove, and a leaves out statement 4, the textbook line that the distinction is not absolute, so c stays.", "traps": {"a": "This includes statements 1 and 3, both false because of 'exclusively' (autoshaping and biofeedback break each rule), and it leaves out the true statement 4.", "b": "Statement 4 is true, but statement 3 is false because operant conditioning can shape autonomic responses, as biofeedback shows; b also omits the true statement 2.", "d": "Statements 1 and 3 are false 'exclusively' claims, and d leaves out statement 2, which is true because sexual arousal can be classically conditioned."}}, {"q": "The region of the brain which is not implicated in OCD?", "concept": "The answer is d: the pons is not implicated in OCD. Obsessive-compulsive disorder is linked to the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) loop. In this circuit the orbitofrontal cortex and the anterior cingulate cortex raise a sense that something is wrong or unfinished; the signal passes to the striatum, chiefly the caudate nucleus, which is part of the basal ganglia and normally filters such signals; the thalamus then sends it back to the cortex. In OCD the loop seems to run too hot, so worry signals keep cycling and the person feels driven to check or wash. Evidence came from PET studies in the late 1980s by Lewis Baxter and colleagues, which showed overactivity in the orbitofrontal cortex and caudate of OCD patients, and later work by the same group found that caudate activity fell after successful treatment with either medication or behaviour therapy. Surgery for severe, treatment-resistant cases (cingulotomy, capsulotomy, deep brain stimulation) targets parts of the same circuit. The pons, by contrast, sits in the brainstem between the midbrain and the medulla. It helps control breathing, triggers REM sleep and relays signals from the cortex to the cerebellum; it is not part of the OCD loop. Example: Kabir checks the gas knob twenty times before leaving home; a scan of a patient like him would show the frontal and caudate parts of the loop working overtime, not the pons. Option d is right because the pons is the only listed structure outside the CSTC circuit. Exam trap: 'caudate nuclei' and 'basal ganglia' look like two separate answers, but the caudate is part of the basal ganglia, so both are implicated.", "traps": {"a": "The caudate nuclei, part of the striatum, are the filtering hub of the OCD circuit, and PET studies found them overactive, so they are implicated.", "b": "The orbitofrontal cortex generates the 'something is wrong' signal in the CSTC loop and is overactive in OCD, so it is implicated.", "c": "The basal ganglia include the caudate nucleus at the centre of the OCD loop, and childhood OCD after streptococcal infection (PANDAS) is thought to involve them, so they are implicated."}}, {"q": "In a classroom, students are self-motivated to do homework where the teacher stopped nagging students about late homework, is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is c: this is an example of negative reinforcement. In the operant conditioning of B. F. Skinner (1938), reinforcement always increases a behaviour and punishment always decreases it. 'Positive' means something is added and 'negative' means something is taken away. So negative reinforcement is the removal of an unpleasant (aversive) stimulus when a behaviour occurs, which makes that behaviour more likely in future. In the stem the teacher's nagging is the aversive stimulus. When students hand in homework on time, the nagging stops; because doing homework removes the nagging, homework becomes more frequent. This is escape and avoidance learning, the same mechanism that makes a driver fasten the seat belt to stop the car's beeping. Example: Kabir's mother scolds him every evening until he tidies his room; once he tidies it the scolding ends, so he starts tidying sooner each day. Option c is right because the behaviour increases and the consequence is the removal of an aversive event. Exam trap: students often read 'negative' as 'bad' and treat negative reinforcement as punishment, but punishment weakens behaviour while negative reinforcement strengthens it. Key note: 'self-motivated' in the stem may tempt intrinsic or extrinsic motivation, but the operant mechanism described (behaviour strengthened because the nagging stops) is negative reinforcement, which is not punishment; b is a broad label and every reinforcement contingency is extrinsic, so c stands as the specific answer.", "traps": {"a": "Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus (praise, a gold star) after the behaviour; here nothing is added, and an unpleasant stimulus, the nagging, is removed.", "b": "Extrinsic motivation is the broad idea of acting for outside rewards or pressures (Edward Deci and Richard Ryan's self-determination theory); it names no specific contingency, while the stem describes one.", "d": "Blocking (Leon Kamin, 1969) is a classical conditioning effect in which an already learned cue stops a newly added cue from being learned; it has nothing to do with removing nagging."}}, {"q": "When the client is repeatedly tapping their foot on the ground, the therapist would get them to exaggerate the movement and talk about the feelings that come up. This therapeutic technique is most likely related to:", "concept": "The answer is a: exaggerating a movement and talking about the feelings it brings up is a Gestalt therapy technique. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970), Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; its founding book, Gestalt Therapy (1951), was written by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman. It holds that people get stuck when they lose awareness of parts of their own experience, and that growth comes from full awareness of what one is feeling and doing in the here and now. Body language is treated as a message: a tapping foot, a clenched fist or a soft voice may carry a feeling the client is not admitting in words. In the exaggeration exercise the therapist asks the client to repeat and enlarge the gesture until its meaning becomes clear, then to put the feeling into words ('I am impatient, I want to leave'). Other Gestalt methods include the empty chair (speaking to an absent person or a part of oneself), the dialogue between 'top dog' and 'underdog', and 'I take responsibility' statements. Example: Riya keeps tapping her foot while describing her mother; asked to tap harder and harder, she suddenly says, 'I'm furious and I want to run away.' Option a is right because amplifying a bodily cue to bring a hidden feeling into present awareness is a signature Gestalt exercise. Exam trap: mindfulness also stresses present-moment awareness, but it observes sensations without changing them, whereas Gestalt deliberately amplifies them to provoke an emotional discovery.", "traps": {"b": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) relies on empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness, and avoids directive exercises such as asking the client to exaggerate a gesture.", "c": "Mindfulness-based approaches, such as Jon Kabat-Zinn's mindfulness-based stress reduction (1979), ask clients to notice sensations without judging or changing them, not to amplify them.", "d": "Dialectical behaviour therapy (Marsha Linehan, 1993) teaches mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness skills for borderline personality disorder; exaggerating a gesture is not one of its methods."}}, {"q": "Amiya is having stage fear in schools, her therapist teaches her to minimize emotional disturbances and self-defeating behaviors by acquiring a more realistic, workable, and compassionate philosophy of life. On this journey her therapist asks her to ask silly questions to the teacher in classroom. What kind of therapy the therapist would have most likely used here?", "concept": "The answer is c: the therapist is most likely using rational emotive therapy. Albert Ellis (1913 to 2007) founded rational therapy in 1955; it later became rational emotive therapy, and in 1993 rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). Ellis held that emotional disturbance comes not from events but from irrational beliefs about them, summed up in the ABC model: an Activating event, the Belief about it, and the emotional and behavioural Consequence. The most damaging beliefs are rigid 'musts' ('I must never look foolish'), awfulising and low frustration tolerance. Therapy disputes these beliefs and replaces them with flexible preferences, and Ellis stated its goal in almost the words of the stem: to help people minimise emotional disturbance and self-defeating behaviour by acquiring a more realistic, workable philosophy of life. REBT also sets behavioural homework, and its best known task is the shame-attacking exercise: the client deliberately does something mildly 'shameful' in public (asking a silly question, calling out the stops on a bus) and discovers that the feared disaster does not happen and that others' disapproval is bearable. Amiya's stage fear rests on a belief such as 'I must not be laughed at', so asking silly questions in class attacks that belief directly. Option c is right because the 'philosophy of life' aim and the shame-attacking assignment are both Ellis's signatures. Exam trap: REBT is one of the cognitive-behavioural therapies, so CBT tempts, but when a stem gives a technique specific to one school, choose that school.", "traps": {"a": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) is non-directive and offers empathy and unconditional positive regard; it neither disputes beliefs nor sets shame-attacking homework.", "b": "Existential therapy (Rollo May; Irvin Yalom, 1980) explores freedom, death, isolation and meaning; it does not assign behavioural exercises such as asking silly questions in class.", "d": "CBT in Aaron Beck's tradition tests automatic thoughts with thought records and behavioural experiments; REBT is one of the CBTs, but the 'philosophy of life' aim and shame attacking are Ellis's own signatures, so the specific answer beats the umbrella."}}, {"q": "Three mountain task was used by Piaget to study ------ in children.", "concept": "The answer is b: the three mountain task was used to study egocentrism. Jean Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1948 in French; The Child's Conception of Space, 1956 in English) seated a child before a model of three mountains of different sizes, one capped with snow, one with a house and one with a red cross. A doll was placed at different points around the model, and the child had to choose, from a set of pictures, the view the doll would see. Children of about four usually picked their own view, and even six-year-olds often failed; only at about seven to eight did children reliably choose the doll's view. Piaget took this as evidence of egocentrism in the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years): not selfishness, but an inability to take another person's point of view, here literally a visual perspective. Later work by Martin Hughes (1975), in which a child hid a boy doll from policeman dolls, showed that four-year-olds could take another's view when the task made sense to them, so Piaget probably underestimated young children. Example: Kabir, aged four, covers his own eyes during hide-and-seek and says, 'You can't see me now', assuming that what he sees is what everyone sees. Option b is right because the three mountain task was built to test perspective taking, the core of egocentrism. Exam trap: centration, conservation and reversibility are also preoperational topics, but they are studied with conservation tasks (pouring water, rolling clay), not with the mountain model.", "traps": {"a": "Centration is focusing on one striking feature of a problem (the height of the water) while ignoring others; it shows up in conservation tasks, not in the three mountain task.", "c": "Conservation, knowing that quantity stays the same despite changes in appearance, is tested with Piaget's liquid, number and clay tasks, not with the mountain model.", "d": "Reversibility, mentally undoing an action (pouring the water back), is a concrete operational ability probed within conservation tasks, not a perspective-taking skill."}}, {"q": "Gilligan found that women base their moral decision on culture of caring other human beings. This would place them at the stage ------ of Kohlberg's moral development theory", "concept": "The answer is c: the scoring system of Lawrence Kohlberg (1958 onwards) tended to place a morality of caring for others at Stage 3. Kohlberg studied moral reasoning with dilemmas, the best known being Heinz, who must decide whether to steal a drug to save his dying wife. He scored the reasons, not the choice, into three levels of two stages each. The preconventional level has Stage 1 (punishment and obedience) and Stage 2 (instrumental exchange, 'you scratch my back, I'll scratch yours'). The conventional level has Stage 3 (interpersonal concordance, the 'good boy, good girl' orientation, where being good means caring about others, being kind and keeping their approval and trust) and Stage 4 (law and order, keeping the social system going). The postconventional level has Stage 5 (social contract) and Stage 6 (universal ethical principles). Carol Gilligan (1982), who had worked with Kohlberg at Harvard, argued in In a Different Voice that women's reasoning, built on relationships, responsibility and not hurting anyone, was often scored at Stage 3, which made women look less mature than men. She held that Kohlberg's scale reflects a male ethic of justice (rights, rules, fairness) and misses an equally mature ethic of care. Example: Meera says Heinz should not steal but should keep talking to the druggist so that nobody is hurt and the family stays together; a Kohlberg scorer would rate this caring reasoning at Stage 3. Option c is right because care for others and their approval define Stage 3. Exam trap: Stage 4 is also conventional and tempts, but it centres on duty to laws and society, which Gilligan linked to the justice orientation, not to care.", "traps": {"a": "Stage 1 (punishment and obedience) judges right and wrong by what gets punished; it has nothing to do with caring for others.", "b": "Stage 2 (instrumental exchange) treats morality as fair deals that serve self-interest ('I'll help you if you help me'), not as care for relationships.", "d": "Stage 4 (law and order) bases morality on duty, rules and keeping the social system working, which belongs to the justice orientation Gilligan contrasted with care."}}, {"q": "Arrange the phase of CBT in correct sequence: 1. Assessment or psychological assessment 2. Skill acquisition 3. Reconceptualization 4. Skill consolidation and application training 5. Maintenance post treatment assessment follow-up", "concept": "The answer is a: the correct sequence is 1-3-2-4-5, that is assessment, reconceptualisation, skills acquisition, skills consolidation and application training, then maintenance with post-treatment assessment and follow-up. This phase model of cognitive-behavioural treatment was set out by Dennis Turk, Donald Meichenbaum and Myles Genest in Pain and Behavioral Medicine (1983), and it closely follows Meichenbaum's stress inoculation training. Assessment comes first: therapist and client gather information on the problem, its triggers, thoughts and behaviours, using interviews, diaries and questionnaires. Reconceptualisation comes next: the therapist helps the client form a new, more hopeful understanding of the problem (for pain, that thoughts, feelings and behaviour change how pain is felt), which gives the rationale for treatment and builds motivation. Only then does skills acquisition begin, teaching coping skills such as relaxation, attention control and cognitive restructuring. Skills consolidation and application training follows, with rehearsal, role play, imagery and homework in real situations. The last phase covers maintenance and generalisation, with relapse prevention, post-treatment assessment and follow-up. Example: Arjun, who has chronic back pain, first keeps a pain diary (assessment), then learns that worry and inactivity make his pain worse (reconceptualisation), learns relaxation and pacing (acquisition), practises them at work (application) and returns for follow-up sessions (maintenance). Option a is right because reconceptualisation must come before skills training: a client first needs a reason for learning the skills. Exam trap: option b looks tidy because it follows the printed 1 to 5 order, but the list deliberately puts reconceptualisation third.", "traps": {"b": "This simply follows the printed numbers and puts skills acquisition before reconceptualisation; skills are taught only after the client has the new understanding that justifies them.", "c": "This places reconceptualisation after skills consolidation, so the client would practise skills before understanding why; the model puts it straight after assessment.", "d": "This starts consolidation before any skills are acquired and puts reconceptualisation fourth, after both skills phases; skills must be learnt before they can be consolidated, and the rationale must come first."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true of test-retest reliability?", "concept": "The answer is b: test-retest reliability means the test will produce consistent results over time. Reliability is the consistency of measurement. In classical test theory every observed score is a true score plus random error, and a reliability coefficient estimates how much of the score variance is true variance. Test-retest reliability, also called the coefficient of stability, is found by giving the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the two sets of scores with Karl Pearson's (1896) r. A high coefficient (say .85 or more) means people keep roughly the same rank order across occasions, so the test is stable. The interval matters: too short and people remember their answers, which inflates the estimate; too long and real change (growth, therapy, learning) lowers it. The method suits stable traits such as intelligence better than changeable states such as mood. Example: Kabir takes a trait anxiety inventory in June and again in July; a correlation of .88 between the two sets of scores shows high test-retest reliability. Option b is right because consistency of results across occasions is exactly what test-retest reliability measures. Exam trap: option a defines validity (measuring what the test claims to measure); a test can be perfectly consistent and still measure the wrong thing, so reliability and validity must not be confused.", "traps": {"a": "Measuring what a test claims to measure is the definition of validity, not reliability; a reliable test can still be invalid.", "c": "Doing better the second time is a practice (carry-over) effect, a threat that can distort test-retest estimates, not what the coefficient means.", "d": "Because a and c are wrong, 'all of the above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "Choose the statement which refers to concurrent validity:", "concept": "The answer is d: concurrent validity is the notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. Criterion-related validity checks a test against an outside standard, the criterion, and has two forms that differ only in timing. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later (an entrance test against first-year marks). In concurrent validity the criterion is measured at about the same time, and often it is an established test of the same attribute, a clinician's diagnosis or membership of a known group. The evidence is a validity coefficient: the correlation between the new test and the criterion. Concurrent validation is common when a short or cheap test is meant to replace a longer one. Example: Riya builds a 10-item Malayalam depression screen and gives it, in the same week, with Aaron Beck's (1961) Beck Depression Inventory to 150 clinic patients; a correlation of .80 supports concurrent validity. Option d is right because it describes a high correlation with an existing measure of the same attribute, which is how concurrent validity is shown. Exam trap: option a reads 'concurrent' literally (two tests at the same time) and leaves out the correlation with a criterion, which is the whole point. Key note: strictly, d blends concurrent validity with convergent validity, the construct validity idea of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959); a is a half-truth about timing with nothing about correlation, so d is the best of the four and stands. The same item reappears as Q64 with the options reordered.", "traps": {"a": "Giving two tests at the same time is only the timing half of 'concurrent'; without a correlation with an established criterion it says nothing about validity.", "b": "Agreement between two or more clinicians on the outcome is inter-rater (inter-scorer) reliability, a form of reliability, not validity.", "c": "Items consistently relating to each other is internal consistency reliability (split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach's alpha), not validity."}}, {"q": "------ is assessed by Cronbach's alpha.", "concept": "The answer is d: coefficient alpha, devised by Lee Cronbach (1951), assesses internal consistency. Cronbach introduced it in Psychometrika as a general index of how well the items of a test hang together. Internal consistency asks whether all the items measure the same thing: if they do, a person who scores high on some items should tend to score high on the others. Alpha is calculated from the number of items and the ratio of the summed item variances to the variance of total scores, so in effect it reflects the average inter-item correlation and the length of the test. It has a maximum of 1, and values of about .70 or more are usually treated as acceptable for research, with higher values needed for clinical decisions about individuals. It needs only one administration of one test, unlike test-retest or parallel forms. Alpha generalises Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), which works only for items scored right or wrong, to items with several response points, such as Likert items, and Cronbach showed it equals the mean of all possible split-half estimates. Example: Meera writes a 20-item self-esteem scale rated on five points; an alpha of .86 tells her the items measure a common attribute fairly consistently. Option d is right because alpha is computed from the interrelations of items within a single administration, which is what internal consistency means. Exam trap: inter-rater reliability also rests on agreement, but agreement between scorers (Jacob Cohen's kappa, 1960), not between items.", "traps": {"a": "Concurrent validity is the correlation of a test with a criterion measured at the same time, a validity question; alpha says nothing about agreement with an outside criterion.", "b": "Inter-rater reliability is agreement between scorers, measured by correlating raters' scores or by Jacob Cohen's kappa (1960), not by alpha across items.", "c": "Test-retest reliability correlates scores from two administrations separated in time; alpha needs only one administration."}}, {"q": "The term 'mental test' was invented by:", "concept": "The answer is b: James McKeen Cattell coined the term \"mental test\". Cattell (1860 to 1944) was an American who took his doctorate under Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig (1886) and then worked with Francis Galton in England. He used the phrase in his paper \"Mental Tests and Measurements\", published in the journal Mind in 1890. There he proposed a standard battery of simple sensory and motor tasks, such as grip strength, rate of movement, two-point touch sensitivity, reaction time to sound and speed of naming colours, to be given to students. The idea, taken from Galton, was that a quick, keen nervous system marks an able mind. It failed: Clark Wissler (1901) found that the scores of Columbia students barely correlated with their college grades. The breakthrough came instead from Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), who tested judgement and reasoning directly. So the term is Cattell's even though his kind of test did not last. Option b is right because the phrase \"mental test\" first appears in Cattell's 1890 paper. Exam trap: Galton is called the father of mental testing and of psychometrics, and he ran the Anthropometric Laboratory in London from 1884, so his name pulls students; but fathering the movement is not the same as coining the term. A second trap is the name itself: James McKeen Cattell (mental test, reaction time, individual differences) is unrelated to Raymond Cattell, who built the 16PF and proposed fluid and crystallised intelligence.", "traps": {"a": "Francis Galton (1822 to 1911) founded the study of individual differences and ran the Anthropometric Laboratory in London from 1884, earning the title father of mental testing, but the term \"mental test\" comes from Cattell's 1890 paper.", "c": "Florence Goodenough devised the Draw-a-Man test (Measurement of Intelligence by Drawings, 1926), a non-verbal intelligence test for children; she worked decades after the term was in use.", "d": "Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) turned Galton's idea of correlation into the product-moment correlation coefficient; he was a statistician, not the person who named the mental test."}}, {"q": "The major purpose of including negative and positive items in the test is to:", "concept": "The answer is c: positively and negatively worded items are mixed in a test mainly to check, and offset, the acquiescence response set. A response set is a habitual way of answering that shifts scores regardless of what the items say; Lee Cronbach (1946) drew attention to it. Acquiescence, or yea-saying, is the tendency to agree, or to answer \"true\" or \"yes\", whatever the content; its mirror image is nay-saying. If every item on an anxiety scale is keyed so that \"agree\" means anxious, a yea-sayer earns a high anxiety score without being anxious at all. Balanced keying solves this. About half the items are worded so that agreeing shows the trait (\"I often feel tense\") and half so that disagreeing shows it (\"I usually feel calm\"), and the second set is reverse-scored. Example: Kabir, a habitual yea-sayer, agrees with both of those items. On a balanced scale his agreements cancel out, and a run of agreements with contradictory statements flags his answer sheet for a closer look. Option c is right because the reversed items exist precisely to expose and neutralise a content-free tendency to agree. Other response sets have their own controls: forced-choice pairs matched for desirability guard against social desirability (Allen Edwards, 1957), and validity scales such as the MMPI's L, F and K catch faking. Exam trap: \"maintain balance\" sounds right because the method is literally called balanced keying, but balance is the means, not the purpose; the purpose is controlling acquiescence. Do not confuse acquiescence with social desirability, which depends on item content (choosing what looks good).", "traps": {"a": "\"Maintain balance\" echoes the name balanced keying, but balance is the technique, not its aim; the stem asks why the balance is built in, and the reason is to control acquiescence.", "b": "Practicality (usability) concerns cost, time and ease of giving and scoring a test; reverse-keyed items add scoring work rather than making the test more practical.", "d": "Reversed items are not added to raise reliability: a consistent response style can actually inflate reliability while lowering validity, and negatively worded items can slightly lower internal consistency when some people misread them."}}, {"q": "An item of the test is best defined as a single task that:", "concept": "The answer is c: a test item is best defined as a single task or question that cannot be broken down into any smaller units. The item is the basic building block of a test, its smallest scorable unit: one question or task with its own scoring rule, such as one multiple-choice question, one true-false statement or one block design puzzle. Total scores are built by adding item scores, and item analysis (difficulty, discrimination, distractor analysis) works item by item. That only makes sense if each item is one indivisible unit. If a \"question\" can be split into parts that are scored separately, each part is really an item, and the original is a cluster of items (a section or testlet), not a single item. This is also why item-writing rules warn against double-barrelled items such as \"I enjoy parties and meeting strangers\": a person who likes one but not the other cannot answer honestly, because the item hides two tasks. Example: Meera writes a maths question for Class 4: \"What is 12 + 7, and is the answer odd or even?\" It contains two tasks, so a child who adds correctly but confuses odd and even gets a muddled score; she splits it into two items. Good items also measure only one thing, are unambiguous, give no clues to the answer, suit the difficulty level and discriminate between high and low scorers. Option c is right because indivisibility is what makes an item the unit of scoring and analysis. Exam trap: options a, b and d all describe something that can be broken down, which is the mark of a subtest, section or compound question, not an item; words like \"partially\", \"wholly\" and \"stretched\" are filler that makes the wrong options sound technical.", "traps": {"a": "A task that can be partially broken down is a compound or double-barrelled question, which item-writing rules tell you to split, so it does not define a single item.", "b": "Something that can be wholly broken down into smaller units is a subtest, scale or section made of many items, not an item.", "d": "\"Stretched and broken down into two smaller units\" is not a psychometric idea; a task that splits into two parts is two items, not one."}}, {"q": "The steepness or slope of the item characteristic curve gives information about:", "concept": "The answer is b: the steepness, or slope, of the item characteristic curve tells you the item's discriminating power. Item response theory (IRT), also called latent trait theory, models each item separately. It assumes an unobservable trait, theta, and plots the probability of a correct (or keyed) response against the person's level on that trait. The graph is the item characteristic curve (ICC), an S-shaped ogive, and three of its features carry three parameters. Its location on the trait axis, the point halfway up the curve (a probability of 0.50 when there is no guessing), is the difficulty, the b parameter: a curve shifted to the right belongs to a harder item. Its slope at that point is the discrimination, the a parameter: a steep curve means the chance of success jumps sharply as ability rises past b, so the item separates people just below that level from people just above it. A flat curve means high and low scorers pass at nearly the same rate, so the item tells you little. Its lower asymptote is the pseudo-guessing parameter, c, the chance that a very low-ability examinee still gets it right. The one-parameter model of Georg Rasch (1960) fixes the slope and estimates only difficulty; the work of Frederic Lord from 1952 led to the two- and three-parameter models. Example: Arjun's algebra item has a steep curve centred on average ability, so it cleanly divides average students from below-average ones. Option b is right because the slope is the IRT counterpart of the classical discrimination index. Exam trap: difficulty is read from where the curve sits (its left-right position), not from how steep it is; examiners count on students linking a \"steep\" curve with a \"hard\" item.", "traps": {"a": "Difficulty (the b parameter) is the curve's position along the ability axis, read at the point halfway up the curve (probability 0.50 when there is no guessing); two items can share the same slope yet differ greatly in difficulty.", "c": "Distractor effectiveness is judged in classical item analysis by counting how many upper- and lower-group examinees choose each wrong option; it is not read from the ICC's slope (the lower asymptote reflects guessing, not distractor quality).", "d": "The slope does carry information, namely discrimination, so \"none of the above\" fails."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Psychologist) with List-II (Concept): a. Adler, b. Bandura, c. Jung, d. Horney. 1. Basic anxiety, 2. Triadic Reciprocal causation, 3. Fictional Finalism, 4. Principle of Equivalence", "concept": "The answer is c: Adler goes with fictional finalism (a-3), Bandura with triadic reciprocal causation (b-2), Jung with the principle of equivalence (c-4) and Horney with basic anxiety (d-1). Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), founder of individual psychology, borrowed from Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of \"As If\" (1911) the idea that people live by useful fictions. Fictional finalism is the imagined final goal, never fully real, that pulls a person's behaviour forward, such as \"I must be the best\". Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021) described reciprocal determinism (1978), later called triadic reciprocal causation (1986): person factors (beliefs, expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another in both directions. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) treated libido as general psychic energy governed by principles borrowed from physics. By the principle of equivalence, energy withdrawn from one interest reappears in another of equal value; by the principle of entropy, energy flows toward a balance that is never reached. Karen Horney (The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, 1937) traced neurosis to basic anxiety, the child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, which drives the strategies of moving toward, against or away from people. Example: when Kabir retires from cricket and pours the same passion into coaching, Jung would call it the principle of equivalence. Option c is right because it is the only set in which all four pairs are correct. Exam trap: option d gets Adler and Horney right but swaps Bandura and Jung; \"reciprocal\" and \"equivalence\" both sound like a balance of forces, so students trade them. Bandura is the social-cognitive theorist of three interacting factors; Jung is the energy theorist.", "traps": {"a": "a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1 gives Adler triadic reciprocal causation (Bandura's), Bandura the principle of equivalence (Jung's) and Jung fictional finalism (Adler's); only Horney with basic anxiety is right.", "b": "a-1, b-3, c-2, d-4 has every pair wrong: basic anxiety is Horney's, fictional finalism is Adler's, triadic reciprocal causation is Bandura's and the principle of equivalence is Jung's.", "d": "a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1 is right for Adler and Horney but swaps the middle two: the principle of equivalence is Jung's law of psychic energy, and triadic reciprocal causation is Bandura's."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): The usual educational evaluation is aimed at maximizing examiner variance. Reason (R): Examiner variance is reduced with higher inter-scorer reliability.", "concept": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false as printed, while the Reason is true. Start with what \"variance\" means here. The spread of marks in an examination comes partly from true differences among examinees and partly from error. One error source is the examiner, or scorer: if a mark depends on who corrects the paper, part of the spread is examiner (scorer) variance. Assertion: educational evaluation aims to maximise examiner variance. False. A fair examination wants marks to reflect what students know, so it tries to maximise true differences among examinees and to minimise examiner variance. That is why boards use model answers, marking schemes, objective items and moderation of markers. Reason: examiner variance falls as inter-scorer reliability rises. True. Inter-scorer (scorer) reliability, discussed by Anne Anastasi in Psychological Testing (first edition 1954), is the agreement between two or more scorers who mark the same responses independently. High agreement means the score hardly depends on who marks it, so the share of variance due to scorers is small. Example: Riya's history essay gets 14 from one teacher and 6 from another; that gap is examiner variance. A clear marking scheme that brings both teachers to 11 raises inter-scorer reliability and shrinks it. Option d is right because a false Assertion paired with a true Reason is exactly what d states. Exam trap: the Reason is a correct textbook statement, which tempts students to call both true; judge each statement on its own before asking whether one explains the other. Key note: read as printed, A is false and d stands. If \"examiner\" was a slip for \"examinee\", A would be true (tests aim to spread examinees out), but R would still not explain it, which would give b; as printed, d stands.", "traps": {"a": "a fails because A is false: evaluation tries to reduce, not increase, examiner variance; R in fact cuts against A, since raising scorer agreement is a way of lowering examiner variance.", "b": "b would fit only if the Assertion read \"examinee variance\" (true, but not explained by scorer agreement); as printed A is false, so \"both true\" fails.", "c": "c has the verdicts the wrong way round: R is the true statement (higher inter-scorer reliability does mean less scorer variance) and A is the false one."}}, {"q": "Which of the following variable(s) can be labelled as organismic variable(s)? 1. Task complexity 2. Intelligence 3. Errors made while learning task 4. Heart rate", "concept": "The answer is b: intelligence and heart rate are organismic variables, so 2 and 4 only. Robert Woodworth (1869 to 1962), whose Dynamic Psychology (1918) put the organism and its drives at the centre, expanded the behaviourist stimulus-response (S-R) formula into S-O-R, placing the organism between stimulus and response, and experimental psychology still sorts variables this way. Stimulus or task variables are features of the situation or task the experimenter presents: task complexity, list length, noise, instructions. Organismic (O, or subject) variables are characteristics or states that the participant brings to the study: age, sex, intelligence, personality, motivation, fatigue, and physiological states such as heart rate or blood sugar. Response variables are the behaviour measured as the outcome, the dependent variable: errors, reaction time, trials to criterion. Organismic variables usually cannot be manipulated, so they are measured, selected or controlled (by matching or random assignment); left uncontrolled, they act as extraneous variables. Example: Arjun learns a finger maze. The number of turns in the maze is a task variable, his IQ and resting heart rate are organismic variables, and the errors he makes are the response variable. Option b is right because only intelligence (a trait) and heart rate (a physiological state) belong to the organism. Exam trap: errors made while learning come from the person, so they feel organismic, but they are the response being measured (the DV); task complexity is a property of the task. Key note: heart rate is recorded as a response measure in some experiments, for example in psychophysiology, but as a state of the organism it is an O variable here; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "\"4 only\" leaves out intelligence, which is the textbook example of an organismic (subject) variable, a stable characteristic the participant brings to the study.", "c": "c adds task complexity, which is a stimulus or task variable set by the experimenter, not a characteristic of the organism.", "d": "d adds errors made while learning, which is the response variable (the dependent measure), not an organismic variable."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): A large body of research indicates that violence and aggression is learned through observation. Reason (R): Learning through observation takes place due to exposure to similar situation and experienced reinforcement.", "concept": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false. Assertion: a large body of research shows that aggression is learned through observation. True. In the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch, kick and hammer an inflatable doll later copied the model's exact acts and words far more than children who saw a calm model or none, without any reward. Their 1963 follow-up found filmed and cartoon models worked too, and decades of media-violence research point the same way. Reason: observational learning happens because of exposure to the same situation and experienced reinforcement. False. Observational learning needs neither. The observer learns by watching someone else, often in a situation they have never been in, and need not be reinforced. Bandura's four processes are attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation; the first two govern learning (acquisition) and the last two govern performance. In Bandura's 1965 study, children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet when offered a reward for showing what the model had done, the differences between the groups all but disappeared: they had learned without reinforcement. Seeing a model rewarded or punished (vicarious reinforcement) changes whether an act is performed, not whether it is learned. Example: Kabir has never been in a fight, but after watching a film hero slap a rival he tries the same move on a classmate. Option c is right because A is well supported while R describes direct, operant learning rather than observational learning. Exam trap: R uses the language of reinforcement, which fits the earlier imitation theory of John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941), who held that imitation must itself be reinforced; Bandura showed it need not be.", "traps": {"a": "a fails because R is false, and a false statement cannot explain a true one; observational learning works without direct reinforcement or prior exposure to the same situation, as the Bobo doll children showed.", "b": "b needs both statements to be true, but R wrongly makes experienced reinforcement a condition of observational learning, the very claim Bandura's 1965 study disproved.", "d": "\"Both false\" fails because A is true: the Bobo doll studies and the wider media-violence literature support aggression being learned by watching models."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Recent research has shown positive effect of cognitive remediation training on the neuro-cognitive functions as well as the overall functioning of patients with schizophrenia. Reason (R): Pharmacotherapy alone does not help patients with schizophrenia to function at the highest possible level of their ability.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but the Reason does not explain the Assertion. Cognitive remediation is a training approach for the cognitive deficits of schizophrenia, such as poor attention, memory, processing speed and planning. The patient does graded, repeated exercises on a computer or with paper and pencil, learns strategies (chunking, self-instruction, breaking a task into steps) and practises carrying them into daily tasks. Assertion: research shows it improves neurocognition and overall functioning. True. A 2011 meta-analysis by Til Wykes and colleagues found durable, moderate gains in cognition and gains in everyday functioning, and the functional gains were larger when cognitive remediation was combined with psychiatric rehabilitation such as supported employment. Reason: medication alone does not help patients function at their highest level. Also true. Antipsychotics mainly reduce positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations) but do little for the cognitive deficits and negative symptoms (flat affect, avolition) that best predict work and social outcome. Does R explain A? R explains why add-on psychosocial treatments are needed; it does not explain why cognitive remediation works. Remediation works because practice and strategy training improve cognitive skills that then carry over to daily life. Example: Arjun is stable on medication but cannot hold a job; after three sessions a week of attention and memory training plus vocational support, his work performance improves. Option b is right because each statement is true on its own, but R states a need, not the mechanism behind A's finding. Exam trap: when R reads like a sensible justification, students jump to a; always ask whether R tells you why A is true. Key note: some answer sheets read R as the rationale and mark a; strictly R gives the need, not the reason A is true; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "a needs R to give the reason A is true; R only says medication leaves a gap in functioning, which justifies trying add-on treatments but does not explain how remediation improves cognition.", "c": "c fails because R is true: antipsychotics reduce positive symptoms but leave cognitive deficits and negative symptoms largely untouched, so medication alone rarely restores full functioning.", "d": "d fails because A is true: meta-analyses, notably that of Til Wykes and colleagues (2011), show gains in cognition and in daily functioning after cognitive remediation."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Concept) with List-II (Explanation): a. Primack Principle, b. Maintenance Rehearsal, c. Pygmalion Effect, d. Activation spreading. 1. Exceptional performance by a student as a result of high teacher expectations. 2. Retrieval of pieces of information based on their relatedness to one another. 3. Keeping information in working memory by repeating it to you. 4. More preferred activity conserve as a reinforcer for a less preferred activity.", "concept": "The answer is a: Premack principle 4, maintenance rehearsal 3, Pygmalion effect 1 and spreading activation 2. The Premack principle (David Premack, 1959) says a more probable, preferred activity can reinforce a less probable one: \"finish your homework, then you can play cricket\". (The stem's \"conserve\" should read \"serve\".) Maintenance rehearsal, a term tied to the levels of processing framework of Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972), is simple rote repetition that keeps information active in working (short-term) memory, as when you repeat a phone number until you dial it; it contrasts with elaborative rehearsal, which links the item to meaning and lasts longer. The Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, Pygmalion in the Classroom, 1968) is a self-fulfilling prophecy: teachers told that certain randomly chosen pupils were about to bloom expected more of them, and those pupils showed larger IQ gains. Spreading activation (Allan Collins and Elizabeth Loftus, 1975) treats concepts in semantic memory as linked nodes; activating one spreads activation to related ones, so retrieval follows relatedness (\"doctor\" makes \"nurse\" easier to retrieve). Example: Meera's mother lets her watch television only after she has practised the veena, a Premack arrangement. Option a is right because every pair matches its definition. Exam trap: options b and d hand the Pygmalion definition to the Premack principle, because both describe performance rising after something else happens; remember that Premack is about one activity reinforcing another, and Pygmalion is about other people's expectations.", "traps": {"b": "b swaps Premack and Pygmalion: it gives the Premack principle the teacher-expectation definition and the Pygmalion effect the preferred-activity definition (maintenance rehearsal 3 and spreading activation 2 are right).", "c": "c is wrong on every pair: it links Premack with retrieval by relatedness, maintenance rehearsal with teacher expectations, Pygmalion with rote repetition and spreading activation with reinforcement by a preferred activity.", "d": "d keeps maintenance rehearsal 3 but gives Premack the Pygmalion definition, Pygmalion the spreading activation definition and spreading activation the Premack definition."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Surgery is one of the methods to understand brain behaviour relations in humans. Reason (R): Lesion method destroys part of the brain.", "concept": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this item, in both the 2019 and the 2021 paper, marks (A) false and (R) true. Reason: the lesion method destroys part of the brain. True. A lesion is damage to or removal of tissue, and the lesion (ablation) method infers a region's function from what is lost, as in the ablation studies of Pierre Flourens (1820s) and the cortical lesions Karl Lashley made in rats (Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence, 1929). Assertion: surgery is one of the methods to understand brain-behaviour relations in humans. On the key's reading, false: as a research method, surgery means experimental lesioning, deliberately destroying or removing tissue to see what changes, and that is done only in animals. No one may operate on a human brain to find out what it does. In humans, brain-behaviour relations are studied through naturally occurring damage (strokes, tumours, head injuries), neuropsychological testing, electrical recording (EEG, event-related potentials), neuroimaging (CT, MRI, fMRI, PET) and non-invasive stimulation (transcranial magnetic stimulation). Patients who need surgery for epilepsy or a tumour can be tested before and after, and some of the best known findings came that way: Henry Molaison (H.M.), whose medial temporal lobes William Scoville removed in 1953 and whom Brenda Milner tested, and the split-brain patients of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1960s). But there the surgery is treatment, and the research method is the clinical study of a lesion. Example: a researcher who wants to know what the hippocampus does can lesion it in rats, but in people has to find patients whose hippocampus was damaged by illness or by a necessary operation. Option d is right on the key's reading because R correctly defines the lesion method, while A wrongly lists surgery as a method that can be used on humans. Exam trap: H.M. and the split-brain cases make A look obviously true, so most students pick a. Key note: this mock used to mark a, because therapeutic surgery such as H.M.'s has taught a great deal about the human brain and is a lesion in all but name. LBS's official key marks d for this question in both 2019 and 2021, on the reading that surgery is never a research method in humans. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "traps": {"a": "a treats surgery as a human research method that the lesion method explains. On the key's reading, deliberate brain surgery for research is never done in people; what we learn from cases like H.M. comes from operations done as treatment, so A is false.", "b": "b also needs A to be true. R is true, but on the key's reading A is false, because surgery as a research method is used only in animals.", "c": "c has it the wrong way round: R is true, since destroying or removing tissue and watching what is lost is the definition of the lesion method; it is A that the key treats as false."}}, {"q": "Read the following statements carefully and give an appropriate response: 1. The association between IQ and high-status professional jobs shows that such jobs require more intelligence. 2. Women tend to excel on test items that require verbal ability, vocabulary and rote learning. 3. Only about 6 percent of the population score above 140 on IQ tests. 4. An IQ score below 90 indicates mental retardation.", "concept": "The answer is a: statements 1, 3 and 4 are false and statement 2 is true. Take them one at a time. Statement 1 claims that because IQ is associated with high-status jobs, those jobs require more intelligence. False: a correlation cannot show that. People with higher IQs get more years of education and the degrees such jobs demand, and family background affects both IQ and access to these careers, so selection, credentials and class can all produce the link. Statement 2 says women tend to do better on verbal, vocabulary and rote-learning items. True in standard texts: the reviews of Diane Halpern (Sex Differences in Cognitive Abilities, first edition 1986) report small female advantages in verbal fluency, spelling, reading and rote verbal memory, while overall IQ shows no sex difference. Statement 3 says about 6 percent score above 140. False: with mean 100 and standard deviation 15, 140 lies about 2.67 SD above the mean, so only about 0.4 percent score higher; even above 130 (2 SD) is only about 2 percent. Statement 4 says an IQ below 90 indicates mental retardation. False: the cut-off for intellectual disability is about 70 (two SD below the mean), together with deficits in adaptive functioning and onset in childhood; 80 to 89 is \"low average\" and 70 to 79 \"borderline\". Example: Riya scores 86; she is in the low average range, not intellectually disabled. Option a is right because it is the only pattern with statement 2 alone true. Exam trap: the 6 percent figure is chosen to look plausible; work it from the normal curve, where only about 2 percent lie above 130. Key note: statement 2 is a broad generalisation (vocabulary differences are tiny), but with 1, 3 and 4 clearly false, a is the only option that fits; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "b calls statement 2 false and 4 true, but 70, not 90, is the cut-off for intellectual disability, and the small female verbal advantage in statement 2 is the textbook position.", "c": "c calls statement 1 true, but a correlation between IQ and job status cannot show that the jobs require more intelligence; education, credentials and family background also link the two.", "d": "d also accepts statement 1, which mistakes correlation for causation, so it fails even though it rightly rejects statements 3 and 4."}}, {"q": "Which of the following does not assess the internal consistency reliability?", "concept": "The answer is a: test-retest reliability does not assess internal consistency. Reliability is the consistency of scores, and each method catches a different source of error. Test-retest reliability gives the same test to the same people twice, usually a fortnight to a few weeks apart, and correlates the two sets of scores. It yields a coefficient of stability and catches error from changes over time, such as mood, health and testing conditions. Internal consistency methods need only one administration and ask whether the parts of the test agree with each other. Split-half reliability scores two halves (often odd against even items), correlates them, and steps the half-test correlation up to full length with the Spearman-Brown formula, published independently by Charles Spearman and William Brown in 1910. The Kuder-Richardson formulas, KR-20 and KR-21 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), estimate consistency across all items for items scored right or wrong. Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) generalises KR-20 to items with several answer points, such as rating scales, and equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients. Example: Kabir gives his new exam-anxiety scale once to 200 students and computes alpha to check that the items hang together; a month later he retests them to check stability, which is test-retest reliability. Option a is right because test-retest needs two occasions and measures stability over time, not agreement among items. Exam trap: all four options are reliability methods, so the item turns on which one is about time; parallel (alternate) forms reliability is not internal consistency either, since it measures the equivalence of two forms.", "traps": {"b": "Split-half reliability is an internal consistency method: one test, one sitting, two halves correlated and then corrected to full length with the Spearman-Brown formula.", "c": "The Kuder-Richardson formulas (KR-20 and KR-21, 1937) measure inter-item consistency for dichotomously scored items, so they are internal consistency estimates.", "d": "Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) is the most widely used internal consistency coefficient, the average of all possible split-half coefficients."}}, {"q": "When the reliability coefficient of a test is 1.00, the standard error of measurement will be:", "concept": "The answer is b: when the reliability coefficient is 1.00, the standard error of measurement (SEM) is 0. A reliability coefficient describes how consistent a group's scores are; the SEM turns that into the likely error in one person's score. It is the standard deviation of the scores a person would obtain over many imaginary retestings, spread around their true score. The formula is SEM = SD x square root of (1 minus r), where SD is the standard deviation of the test scores and r is the reliability coefficient. With r = 1, 1 minus r is 0, its square root is 0, and the SEM is 0: every observed score equals the true score, with no measurement error at all. At the other extreme, with r = 0, the SEM equals the SD, because the score is pure error. In between, the SEM shrinks as reliability rises. Example: on an IQ test with SD 15 and reliability 0.91, SEM = 15 x 0.30 = 4.5, so if Meera scores 110, her true score lies between 105.5 and 114.5 about 68 percent of the time. Option b is right because perfect reliability leaves no error to estimate. Exam trap: students see 1.00 in the stem and pick 1.00, or assume SEM and reliability move together; they move in opposite directions.", "traps": {"a": "An SEM of 1.00 would mean some error remains; with r = 1 the formula SD x square root of (1 minus 1) gives 0, not 1.", "c": "0.50 does not follow from perfect reliability; it would need a reliability below 1 (for example r = 0.75 with an SD of 1).", "d": "\"None of these\" fails because 0 is listed and is the exact answer."}}, {"q": "If a psychological test yields the same results consistently but does not measure what it intends to measure, it is:", "concept": "The answer is b: a test that gives the same results consistently but does not measure what it is meant to measure is reliable but not valid. Reliability is consistency: the same person gets about the same score on retesting, on another form or from another scorer. Validity is accuracy of meaning: the test measures the construct it claims to measure and supports the decisions made from it. The two are linked in one direction only. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. An unreliable test cannot be valid, because random error swamps any true signal, but a reliable test can still be consistently measuring the wrong thing. In numbers, a test's validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of its reliability coefficient. Example: Arjun's bathroom scale always reads 2 kg too high. Weigh him five times and it shows 72 kg every time, so it is perfectly reliable, but it is not valid, because his true weight is 70 kg. In testing, a \"creativity\" test that really measures vocabulary can give stable scores for the wrong construct. Option b is right because consistency without accuracy is exactly what \"reliable but not valid\" means. Exam trap: option a reverses the pair, and a test cannot be valid without being reliable; tie \"consistent\" to reliability and \"measures what it intends\" to validity.", "traps": {"a": "\"Valid but not reliable\" reverses the terms and is impossible, because inconsistent scores cannot measure anything accurately; reliability is a precondition of validity.", "c": "c denies reliability, but the stem says the test yields the same results consistently, which is what reliability means.", "d": "d claims validity, but the stem says the test does not measure what it intends to, which is the absence of validity."}}, {"q": "The largest percentage of people taking an IQ test will score in range from:", "concept": "The answer is c: the largest percentage of people taking an IQ test score in the 90 to 109 range. Modern intelligence tests use the deviation IQ: within each age group the mean is 100 and the standard deviation 15, and scores fall roughly on a normal curve, so most people cluster near the middle and fewer appear the further out you go. David Wechsler's classification, used since the Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939), labels bands of this curve. On the Wechsler scales, 90 to 109 is \"Average\" and holds about 50 percent of people, the largest single band. 80 to 89 (low average) and 110 to 119 (high average) hold about 16 percent each; 70 to 79 (borderline) and 120 to 129 (superior) about 7 percent each; 130 and above, and 69 and below, about 2 percent each. Example: if 100 children in Kabir's school are tested, about half will score between 90 and 109, so Kabir's 104 is perfectly ordinary. Option c is right because the average band sits at the peak of the curve and is the classification band with the biggest share. Exam trap: option a is a wider range and looks generous, but it covers only the half of the curve above 100, and even 100 to 140 holds slightly less than half of all people; option b (80 to 100) holds roughly 40 percent and option d (60 to 90) about a quarter. Key note: on pure normal-curve arithmetic, the 40-point span 100 to 140 also holds just under half (about 49.6 percent), but it is not a classification band; the question tests the classic fact that the average band 90 to 109 holds the most people, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "100 to 140 covers only the upper half of the curve, from the mean upward; it holds just under half of people (about 49.6 percent) and is not a classification band, so it does not beat the average band.", "b": "80 to 100 straddles the low average band and the lower part of the average band and holds roughly 40 percent, less than the band centred on the mean.", "d": "60 to 90 lies entirely below average, from the extremely low range up to low average, and holds only about a quarter of people."}}, {"q": "Cattel's 16 PF inventory is based upon:", "concept": "The answer is a: Raymond Cattell's 16PF is built on the factor-analytic strategy. Personality inventories are built in four main ways. The logical-content (rational) strategy writes items whose content plainly reflects the trait, as in Robert Woodworth's Personal Data Sheet (1917), the first personality inventory. The theoretical strategy writes items for the constructs of a theory, as in the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule, based on the needs of Henry Murray (Explorations in Personality, 1938). The criterion-group (empirical keying) strategy keeps whichever items separate a diagnosed group from controls, whatever they say, as in the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943). The factor-analytic strategy gives many items to many people, correlates them, and lets factor analysis find clusters of items that go together; each cluster becomes a scale. Raymond Cattell (1905 to 1998) started from the lexical hypothesis, that important traits are coded in everyday words. He reduced the list of trait words compiled by Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) to clusters, factor-analysed ratings and questionnaire data, and arrived at 16 source traits such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability and dominance. The Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire appeared in 1949. Example: Meera's 16PF profile shows high warmth and low tension, each a factor-derived scale. Option a is right because the 16 scales are the factors that emerged from the analysis, not categories decided in advance. Exam trap: do not confuse Raymond Cattell (16PF, fluid and crystallised intelligence) with James McKeen Cattell, who coined \"mental test\"; and remember that the MMPI, not the 16PF, is the criterion-keyed inventory.", "traps": {"b": "The logical-content (rational) strategy relies on items whose content obviously fits the trait, as in Robert Woodworth's Personal Data Sheet (1917); Cattell's scales came from factor analysis instead.", "c": "The criterion-group strategy keeps items that separate a diagnosed group from controls regardless of content; that is how Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley built the MMPI (1943), not the 16PF.", "d": "A mixed (construct) strategy combines theory, empirical and statistical steps, as in Douglas Jackson's Personality Research Form (1967); the 16PF rests on factor analysis."}}, {"q": "------ is not measured by General Aptitude Test Battery (GATB).", "concept": "The answer is d: abstract reasoning is not measured by the General Aptitude Test Battery (GATB). The GATB, released by the United States Employment Service in 1947 to match job seekers to occupations, is a multiple aptitude battery: a set of separately scored tests that gives a profile of aptitudes instead of a single IQ. It measures nine aptitudes: G, general learning ability; V, verbal aptitude; N, numerical aptitude; S, spatial aptitude; P, form perception; Q, clerical perception; K, motor coordination; F, finger dexterity; and M, manual dexterity. The dexterity aptitudes are measured with apparatus such as pegboards rather than paper and pencil. Abstract reasoning belongs to a different battery, the Differential Aptitude Tests (DAT) of George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman (1947), built for school guidance. Its eight subtests are verbal reasoning, numerical ability, abstract reasoning, clerical speed and accuracy, mechanical reasoning, space relations, spelling and language usage. Example: Arjun, applying for work assembling watch parts, takes the GATB; his high finger and manual dexterity scores suit precision work, but the battery gives him no abstract reasoning score. Option d is right because abstract reasoning is a DAT subtest and is not one of the GATB's nine aptitudes. Exam trap: both batteries date from the 1940s and both include numerical and clerical measures, so students merge their lists; the GATB's signature is its motor and dexterity aptitudes, the DAT's is abstract and mechanical reasoning.", "traps": {"a": "Numerical aptitude (N) is one of the GATB's nine aptitudes, measured by computation and arithmetic reasoning tests.", "b": "Clerical perception (Q), the speed of spotting differences in names and numbers, is one of the GATB's nine aptitudes.", "c": "Finger dexterity (F) is one of the GATB's nine aptitudes, measured with a board on which small rivets and washers are assembled and taken apart."}}, {"q": "If 10 years old has a mental age of 14 years, his IQ would be:", "concept": "The answer is a: a 10-year-old with a mental age of 14 has a ratio IQ of 140. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon built the first intelligence scale in 1905, and their 1908 revision expressed ability as mental age (MA), the age level of the items a child passes. William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age (CA) to get the intelligence quotient, and Lewis Terman (Stanford-Binet, 1916) multiplied it by 100 to remove the decimal. So ratio IQ = MA / CA x 100. Here MA is 14 and CA is 10: 14 / 10 = 1.4, and 1.4 x 100 = 140. A child whose mental age equals chronological age scores exactly 100; a mental age above chronological age gives an IQ above 100, and one below gives an IQ below 100. The ratio IQ breaks down for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15 within each age group). Option a is right because 14 divided by 10, times 100, is 140. Exam trap: option b (71.4) comes from inverting the ratio, CA / MA x 100 = 10 / 14 x 100; a quick check is that a child who is ahead of their age must score above 100.", "traps": {"b": "71.4 is CA / MA x 100 (10 / 14 x 100), the ratio turned upside down; a child ahead of age cannot score below 100.", "c": "100 would mean mental age equals chronological age, but this child is four years ahead.", "d": "120 would need a mental age of 12 at age 10 (12 / 10 x 100); with a mental age of 14 the quotient is 140."}}, {"q": "Researches have shown that antisocial and schizoid personality often score high on ------ of EPQ.", "concept": "The answer is d: research shows that antisocial and schizoid personalities often score high on the P scale only. The Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ) of Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1975) measures three superfactors plus a Lie scale: E (extraversion versus introversion), N (neuroticism versus emotional stability) and P (psychoticism versus impulse control), with L detecting faking good. High P scorers are described as cold, tough-minded, egocentric, impersonal, impulsive, aggressive and lacking in empathy, and sometimes creative; despite its name, P is closer to psychopathy than to psychosis. Both personality types share P features. The antisocial person is callous, impulsive and aggressive; the schizoid person is cold, detached and emotionally flat. On E, however, they part ways: schizoid people are typically introverted (low E), while antisocial people tend to be extraverted and sensation seeking (high E). N, the anxious and moody dimension, is not the shared core of these two cold, callous types. So the one scale on which both score high is P. Example: Kabir, a detached loner with no close friends, and Arjun, a thrill-seeking youth with a record of fights, would both score high on P, but Kabir low and Arjun high on E. Option d is right because P is the only dimension the two profiles share. Exam trap: option a looks right if you remember that antisocial people are extraverted, but schizoid people are not, so \"P and E\" fails for the pair; option b adds N, which describes anxious emotional reactivity, not cold detachment.", "traps": {"a": "\"P and E\" fits the antisocial, sensation-seeking profile but not the schizoid one, since schizoid people are typically introverted (low E).", "b": "\"P and N\" adds neuroticism, Eysenck's dimension of anxiety, moodiness and emotional reactivity; it is not the shared feature of these two cold, detached or callous types.", "c": "\"N only\" misses the point entirely: the coldness, impulsivity and lack of empathy common to both types are the content of the P scale, not of N."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Phenomenon) with List-II (Psychologist): a. Two factor theory, b. Multifactor Theory of Intelligence, c. Structure of Intellect Model, d. Level Theory of Intelligence. 1. Thurston, 2. Jensen, 3. Spearman, 4. Guilford", "concept": "The answer is d: two-factor theory goes with Spearman (a-3), multifactor theory with Thurstone (b-1), the Structure of Intellect model with Guilford (c-4) and level theory with Jensen (d-2). Charles Spearman (1904) noticed that all mental tests correlate positively and proposed two factors: g, a general ability shared by every test, and s, a specific ability unique to each test. Louis Thurstone (Primary Mental Abilities, 1938) factor-analysed a large set of tests given to students and found about seven primary mental abilities (verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning): several independent abilities rather than one dominant g, hence a multiple-factor theory. J. P. Guilford (1967) proposed the Structure of Intellect cube, operations x contents x products, giving 120 abilities at first and later 150 and 180. Arthur Jensen (1969) proposed a two-level theory: Level I, associative ability (rote memory, digit span), and Level II, conceptual ability (reasoning and abstraction, close to g). Example: Meera recalls a long string of phone numbers easily (Level I) but struggles with matrix puzzles (Level II). Option d is right because it is the only set in which all four pairs match. Exam trap: the word \"two\" misleads, since Spearman has two factors and Jensen two levels, so option a hands two-factor theory to Jensen; tie \"two-factor\" to g and s, which are Spearman's alone. Key note: Indian texts often call Edward Thorndike's theory (1920s) \"multifactor\" and Thurstone's \"group factor\"; with Thorndike absent, Thurstone is the only fit for b; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "a-2, b-3, c-1, d-4 gives two-factor theory to Jensen, multifactor theory to Spearman, the Structure of Intellect to Thurstone and level theory to Guilford; every pair is wrong.", "b": "a-1, b-2, c-4, d-3 gets Guilford right but gives two-factor theory to Thurstone, multifactor theory to Jensen and level theory to Spearman.", "c": "a-4, b-3, c-2, d-1 is wrong throughout: Guilford built the Structure of Intellect, not two-factor theory; Spearman proposed g and s; Jensen the two levels; Thurstone the primary abilities."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Route) with List-II (Processing): a. Central route to persuasion, b. Peripheral route to persuasion, c. Distinct routes to persuasion, d. Attitude behaviour discrepancies. 1. Heuristic processing, 2. Elaboration likelihood processing, 3. Cognitive Dissonance, 4. Systematic processing", "concept": "The answer is a: central route 4 (systematic processing), peripheral route 1 (heuristic processing), distinct routes 2 (elaboration likelihood) and attitude-behaviour discrepancy 3 (cognitive dissonance). The elaboration likelihood model (ELM) of Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1981, 1986) says there are two distinct routes to persuasion. Elaboration means thinking carefully about a message's arguments, and its likelihood depends on motivation (personal relevance, need for cognition) and ability (time, knowledge, freedom from distraction). With both, people take the central route: they scrutinise the quality of the arguments, and the resulting attitude change lasts, resists attack and predicts behaviour. Without them, people take the peripheral route, relying on cues such as an attractive or expert source, the number of arguments or a good mood, and the change is shallow and short-lived. The heuristic-systematic model of Shelly Chaiken (1980) makes a parallel split, and its terms map onto the ELM: systematic processing is the central route and heuristic processing (\"experts are usually right\") the peripheral route. When people act against their attitudes, Leon Festinger's cognitive dissonance theory (1957) applies: the discrepancy creates discomfort, which people often reduce by changing the attitude, as in the one-dollar study of Festinger and James Carlsmith (1959). Example: Riya compares battery life and camera specifications before buying a phone (central, systematic), while her brother buys the one a cricket star advertises (peripheral, heuristic). Option a is right because every pair fits. Exam trap: option c gives the central route heuristic processing and option d calls the peripheral route systematic; \"central\" should pull you to careful, systematic thought and \"peripheral\" to mental shortcuts.", "traps": {"b": "a-3, b-2, c-4, d-1 gives the central route to cognitive dissonance, the peripheral route to elaboration likelihood and attitude-behaviour discrepancy to heuristics; dissonance is Leon Festinger's account of attitude-behaviour discrepancy, not a route to persuasion.", "c": "a-1, b-2, c-4, d-3 gives the central route heuristic processing, the peripheral route elaboration likelihood and \"distinct routes\" systematic processing; only the dissonance pairing is right.", "d": "a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1 is wrong throughout: it makes the peripheral route systematic, gives \"distinct routes\" to dissonance and treats attitude-behaviour discrepancy as heuristic processing."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Encoding specificity states that a retrieval cue is successful only if it is present at the time of encoding. Reason (R): When participants are given category names as retrieval it enables the participant of the experiment to remember which were previously presented to them to remember.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but the Reason does not explain the Assertion. Assertion: encoding specificity states that a retrieval cue succeeds only if it was present at encoding. True. Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson (1973) showed that a cue helps recall only to the extent that it was encoded together with the item. In their experiments, a target word studied beside a weakly related word was recalled better when that same weak partner was the cue than when a strong associate of the target, never seen at study, was the cue. Context-dependent memory fits the same principle: Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley (1975) found that divers recalled words best in the setting, underwater or on land, where they had learned them. Reason: category names given as retrieval cues help people recall the words presented to them. Also true. Endel Tulving and Zena Pearlstone (1966) had people learn word lists grouped by category; at recall, those given the category names (\"animals\", \"fruits\") recalled far more than those without cues, showing that many words were available in memory but not accessible without a cue. Does R explain A? No. R demonstrates that cues aid retrieval; it does not say why a cue must match what was encoded, which is the heart of encoding specificity. Example: Kabir studies in a quiet library and later recalls more in a quiet hall than in a noisy canteen. Option b is right because R illustrates cued recall rather than stating the reason for the principle. Exam trap: A and R share the word \"cue\", which tempts a. Key note: in that study the category names were present at study, so R is consistent with A and some sheets mark a; an illustration is not the explanation of a principle, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "a needs R to state why A holds; R reports that category cues improve recall, a fact about cued recall, but it does not say that a cue must match encoding, so it illustrates rather than explains.", "c": "c fails because R is true: Endel Tulving and Zena Pearlstone (1966) found that category-name cues sharply raised recall of studied words.", "d": "d fails because A is true: the encoding specificity principle of Tulving and Thomson (1973) says a cue is effective only to the extent that it was encoded with the item."}}, {"q": "Anitha is a 23-year-old girl who is unstable in relationships, self-destructive, and having recurrent thoughts of suicide and impulsive. Which is the most suitable therapeutic method for this condition?", "concept": "The answer is c: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) is the most suitable method for Anitha. Her picture, unstable relationships, self-destructive acts, recurrent suicidal thoughts and impulsivity, matches borderline personality disorder (BPD): a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotion, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood. Marsha Linehan (Cognitive-Behavioral Treatment of Borderline Personality Disorder, 1993) developed DBT for chronically suicidal women with BPD. Standard CBT's constant push for change felt invalidating to them, while acceptance alone left the self-harm untouched, so DBT joins acceptance (drawn from Zen practice) with change (drawn from behaviour therapy). Her biosocial theory sees BPD as emotion dysregulation arising when an emotionally sensitive child meets an invalidating environment. Standard DBT combines weekly individual therapy, a skills group, telephone coaching in crises and a therapist consultation team. Its four skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, and treatment targets life-threatening behaviour first. Randomised trials, beginning with Linehan's own in 1991, show fewer suicide attempts, less self-harm and fewer days in hospital than usual treatment. Option c is right because DBT was built for exactly this cluster and has the strongest trial evidence for reducing suicidal and self-harming behaviour. Exam trap: \"most suitable\" asks for the evidence-based first-line treatment, not for any therapy that might be tried. Key note: expressive psychotherapy, which Otto Kernberg described for borderline patients (Borderline Conditions and Pathological Narcissism, 1975) and later developed into transference-focused psychotherapy, is also used for BPD with some evidence, but DBT is the first-line answer for recurrent suicidality and impulsivity; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) offers empathy and unconditional positive regard but no structured skills training or suicide-risk protocol, and it lacks trial evidence for BPD.", "b": "Expressive psychotherapy, as in Otto Kernberg's transference-focused psychotherapy, works on split images of self and others through the transference; it has some evidence for BPD but is not the first-line choice for active suicidality and impulsivity.", "d": "Classical psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud), with free association and little structure, can deepen regression in BPD and has no strong evidence for reducing self-harm."}}, {"q": "18-year-old Navaneeth believes that he is unique and life difficulties may not affect him, and he involves in rash driving. This phenomenon is known as", "concept": "The answer is b: Navaneeth's belief that he is unique and that life's difficulties will not touch him is the personal fable. David Elkind (1967) argued that when adolescents reach the formal operational stage of Jean Piaget (described with Barbel Inhelder in 1955; English translation 1958) they can think about thinking, including other people's thoughts, but they fail to separate what others are thinking about from their own preoccupations. He called this adolescent egocentrism and described two mental constructions. The personal fable is the belief that one's experiences and feelings are unique (\"no one has ever felt what I feel\"), together with a sense of invulnerability (\"it will not happen to me\"). It helps explain why teenagers take risks such as rash driving, unprotected sex or drug use while knowing the dangers. The imaginary audience is the belief that everyone is watching and judging, which makes teenagers intensely self-conscious about a pimple or a bad haircut. Both are strongest in early adolescence and fade in the late teens. Navaneeth shows both strands of the fable: uniqueness (\"I am different\") and invulnerability, acted out in rash driving. Option b is right because uniqueness plus invulnerability leading to risk-taking is the textbook description of the personal fable. Exam trap: the imaginary audience is its twin, also from Elkind; tell them apart by focus: the audience is about being watched (self-consciousness), the fable is about being special and safe (risk-taking).", "traps": {"a": "The imaginary audience, Elkind's other form of adolescent egocentrism, is the belief that everyone is watching and judging one; it explains self-consciousness, not a sense of being unique and invulnerable.", "c": "Self-concept is a person's organised picture of who they are (central to Carl Rogers's theory); it is a general self-description, not a specific belief that dangers will not apply to oneself.", "d": "Self-esteem is the evaluative side of the self-concept, how much one values oneself (Morris Rosenberg's scale, 1965); high self-esteem does not imply believing oneself immune to harm."}}, {"q": "Infants understand much longer sentence from their parents and others, but they may be able to produce one or two words only. This phenomenon is known as", "concept": "The answer is b: the gap between understanding long sentences and producing only one or two words is the receptive-productive lag. Receptive (comprehension) language is what a child understands; productive (expressive) language is what the child can say. In every normally developing child, comprehension runs ahead of production. Around the first birthday an infant may say only a word or two yet respond correctly to dozens of words and to whole requests such as 'bring me your shoes'; children understand about 50 words several months before they can say 50. The lag exists because understanding a word only needs the child to match a heard sound pattern to a meaning, while saying it needs the child to retrieve the word and to plan and control the muscles of speech. The gap never fully closes: adults, too, understand far more words than they use. Example: one-year-old Riya cannot yet say 'ball', but when her grandmother asks 'where is the ball?' she crawls across the room and points to it. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly this mismatch: long sentences understood, one or two words produced. Exam trap: the other three options are real terms from the same chapter on language development, so they feel familiar. Telegraphic speech describes the form of what the child says (two-word utterances), child-directed speech describes what adults say to the child, and the language acquisition device is a theory of why language develops at all; none names the gap between understanding and saying.", "traps": {"a": "Child-directed speech (motherese) is the slow, simplified, high-pitched, exaggerated speech adults use with infants; it describes the parents' input, not the child's gap between comprehension and production.", "c": "Telegraphic speech is Roger Brown's term for the two-word stage at about 18 to 24 months ('more milk'), with content words and no function words; it describes the form of production, not the lag behind comprehension.", "d": "The language acquisition device is Noam Chomsky's (1965) proposed innate mechanism holding a universal grammar; it explains how language is acquired and is not a name for the understanding-speaking gap."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Analysis) with List-II (Explanation): a) Structural analysis, b) TA, c) Script analysis, d) Game analysis. 1) describing what between two or more people, 2) understanding transaction that lead to bad feeling, 3) understanding what is happening within individual, 4) understanding individual's life plan", "concept": "The answer is d: structural analysis studies what is happening within the individual (3), transactional analysis proper studies what passes between two or more people (1), script analysis studies the person's life plan (4) and game analysis studies transactions that end in bad feeling (2). All four are levels of Eric Berne's transactional analysis (TA), set out in Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy (1961) and Games People Play (1964). Structural analysis maps the three ego states inside one person: Parent (attitudes copied from parents), Adult (objective processing of present reality) and Child (feelings and responses kept from childhood). Transactional analysis proper then studies exchanges between people, each a stimulus from one ego state and a response from another: complementary transactions keep talk going, crossed ones stop it and ulterior ones carry a hidden message. Game analysis studies repeated series of ulterior transactions with a predictable payoff of bad feelings, such as 'Why Don't You, Yes But', in which every suggestion is rejected. Script analysis uncovers the life script, an unconscious life plan decided in childhood under parental injunctions ('Don't be close') and resting on a life position such as 'I'm not OK, you're OK'. Example: Kabir keeps asking friends for advice, rejects every idea and then feels misunderstood (a game); his therapist traces it to a childhood decision that nobody can really help him (his script). Option d is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: the label TA names both the whole theory and its second level, so students give 'within the individual' to TA; that belongs to structural analysis, because ego states live inside one person.", "traps": {"a": "a-3 is right, but b-2 gives transactional analysis proper the bad-feeling exchanges that belong to game analysis, c-1 gives script analysis the between-people exchanges, and d-4 gives game analysis the life plan that belongs to script analysis.", "b": "a-1 and b-3 swap the first two levels: structural analysis concerns ego states within one person, while transactional analysis proper concerns exchanges between people; c-4 and d-2 are right.", "c": "a-3 is right, but b-4, c-2 and d-1 rotate the rest: the life plan is script analysis, bad-feeling transactions are game analysis, and between-people exchanges are transactional analysis proper."}}, {"q": "When the person answers your question in a disorganized or rambled way and doesn't reach an answer it is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: a reply that wanders away from the question and never reaches the answer is tangentiality. Tangentiality and its look-alikes are disorders of the form of thought, judged from how speech is organised rather than from what the person believes. In tangentiality the person starts near the question, drifts off along a loosely related path and never returns to the goal. Nancy Andreasen's (1979) Scale for the Assessment of Thought, Language and Communication defines it as replying to a question in an oblique or even irrelevant way, which matches this stem's 'answers your question'. It is seen in schizophrenia and mania. In circumstantiality the person also takes needless detours and piles up detail, but finally does reach the point; it is typical of epilepsy, obsessional traits and organic states. Example: asked 'Where do you live, Arjun?', a tangential reply runs 'Near the temple; the temple bells are loud; loud music hurts my ears; my ears have hurt since school...', and the place is never named. A circumstantial reply wanders through the bus route and the neighbours, then ends 'so, behind Palayam market'. Option a is right because the stem's deciding phrase is 'doesn't reach an answer'. Exam trap: tangentiality and circumstantiality both sound rambling, so examiners pair them; the test is whether the goal is reached. Goal never reached: tangentiality. Goal reached late, after detours: circumstantiality.", "traps": {"b": "Word salad is an incoherent jumble of words with no meaningful connection, the most severe formal thought disorder, seen in schizophrenia; the stem describes a rambling but connected answer, not a jumble.", "c": "Circumstantiality (printed 'Circumtiality') is also a rambling, over-detailed answer, but the person eventually reaches the point; the stem says the answer is never reached.", "d": "Flight of ideas is a rapid stream of speech jumping from topic to topic by chance associations, rhymes or puns, typical of mania; the stem describes a slow drift away from one question, not rapid jumps."}}, {"q": "Which of the following cannot be included in the categories of classification of responses of Rorschach test?", "concept": "The answer is b: 'context' is not one of the scoring categories of the Rorschach test. Hermann Rorschach (1921), in Psychodiagnostik, presented ten symmetrical inkblots and asked 'What might this be?'. Every response is then coded on fixed categories. Location records which part of the blot was used: W the whole blot, D a common detail, Dd an unusual detail and S the white space. Determinants record what feature of the blot shaped the percept: form (F), movement (human M, animal FM, inanimate m), colour (FC, CF, C) and shading (texture, vista, diffuse). Content records what was seen: human (H), animal (A), anatomy, nature, objects and so on. Scorers also code popular responses (P, answers common in the norm group) and form quality (how well the percept fits the blot's shape), and John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) merged the rival American scoring systems into one. Example: Meera says of Card V, 'A bat, the whole thing, because of its shape'; this is scored W for location, F for the form determinant, A for animal content and P for a popular response. Option b is right because no standard Rorschach system has a 'context' category: the test scores where on the blot, why that percept and what was seen, not the circumstances around the response. Exam trap: 'context' looks and sounds like 'content', and a quick reader treats them as the same word; content is a real category, context is not.", "traps": {"a": "Location (W, D, Dd, S) is the first scoring category, recording which part of the blot the response uses, so it is included.", "c": "Content (human, animal, anatomy, nature and so on) records what the person saw and is a standard scoring category, so it is included.", "d": "Determinants (form, movement, colour, shading) record which feature of the blot shaped the percept; they are the heart of Rorschach scoring, so they are included."}}, {"q": "Researchers have revealed that social desirability tendency is related to:", "concept": "The answer is d: research ties the social desirability tendency to the need for social approval, to social conformity and to avoiding social criticism. Social desirability is the tendency to answer test items in the way that makes oneself look good rather than in the way that is true. Allen Edwards (1957) showed that how desirable an item sounds strongly predicts how often people endorse it. Douglas Crowne and David Marlowe (1960) built the Marlowe-Crowne Social Desirability Scale from two kinds of items: behaviours that are approved but rarely true of anyone (never having disliked anyone) and behaviours that are disapproved but true of almost everyone. A high scorer claims too many virtues and denies too many common faults. In their later book, The Approval Motive (1964), they read the score as a need for approval. High scorers conformed more to group pressure, were more easily persuaded, and were more cautious and defensive in situations where they might be judged, which amounts to avoiding disapproval and criticism. Example: Arjun, a high scorer, ticks 'I never gossip' and, in a group task, quietly goes along with the others' wrong answer rather than risk their disapproval. Option d is right because a, b and c are three faces of the same approval motive that the research found. Exam trap: option a is the textbook label and looks like the single answer, but the stem asks what social desirability is related to, and conformity and fear of criticism are part of the same findings.", "traps": {"a": "Need for social approval is Crowne and Marlowe's own reading of their scale and is correct, but it is only one of the three linked findings, so choosing it alone misses the fuller answer.", "b": "Conformity is also linked to high social desirability scores (high scorers go along with group judgements more), so b is true but incomplete on its own.", "c": "Avoiding social criticism or disapproval is the defensive side of the approval motive and is also supported, so c is true but incomplete on its own."}}, {"q": "When the testee is found to respond in some systematic way by choosing any one type of the response options in each item, he/she is definitely showing", "concept": "The answer is b: choosing the same kind of response option on item after item, whatever the item says, is an acquiescence set. A response set is a habitual way of answering that shifts scores regardless of item content; Lee Cronbach (1946) drew attention to it. In acquiescence (yea-saying) the person tends to agree, or to answer 'true' or 'yes'; nay-saying is the mirror image. Because the bias attaches to the response format rather than to the content, a yea-sayer can score high on any scale keyed in the 'agree' direction without having the trait. Test builders control it with balanced keying: half the items are worded so that agreeing scores the trait and half so that disagreeing does, so a yea-sayer's agreements cancel out. Douglas Jackson and Samuel Messick (1958) argued that much of the variance on inventories reflects such styles rather than content. Example: Meera ticks 'agree' on all 40 items of a scale, including both 'I enjoy noisy parties' and 'I prefer to stay at home in the evening'; the contradiction exposes acquiescence. Option b is right because the stem's key words, 'systematic' and 'choosing any one type of the response options in each item', describe answering driven by the format, not by the content. Exam trap: social desirability also distorts answers, but it depends on content (the person picks what looks good), whereas acquiescence ignores content entirely.", "traps": {"a": "The deviation set comes from Irwin Berg's deviation hypothesis: a tendency to give unusual, atypical answers, which Berg held to be general and linked with deviant behaviour; it is not a tendency to pick the same option every time.", "c": "Social desirability bias is choosing the answer that presents oneself favourably (Allen Edwards, 1957); it depends on what each item says, not on one fixed type of response option.", "d": "'Social undesirability bias' is not a standard response set; the nearest real idea is faking bad (exaggerating problems, flagged by the MMPI's F scale), which again depends on item content."}}, {"q": "All cause non sampling errors except:", "concept": "The answer is d: an inadequate sample produces sampling error, so it is the one item that is not a cause of non-sampling error. Survey error comes in two families. Sampling error is the chance difference between a sample statistic and the population parameter that arises simply because only part of the population was studied. It shrinks as the sample grows (the standard error of the mean is the SD divided by the square root of n), is zero in a complete census, and can be calculated for probability samples. Non-sampling error is everything else that goes wrong while collecting and handling data, and it can occur even in a census: faulty measuring tools (badly worded questions, unreliable scales), defects in data collection (interviewer bias, wrong procedures, careless recording), non-response (people who do not reply differ from those who do), coverage error and processing error (coding and data entry mistakes). A bigger sample does not cure non-sampling error. Example: Kabir surveys 2,000 students with a confusing questionnaire and gets only a 30 percent reply rate; his large sample keeps sampling error small, but the poor tool and the non-response still bias his results. Option d is right because the size and representativeness of the sample concern sampling itself, so an inadequate sample is a source of sampling error, the exception here. Exam trap: 'inadequate sample' sounds like a mistake, so it tempts students to count it as non-sampling error; ask whether the error would vanish if the whole population were studied. If it would, it is sampling error.", "traps": {"a": "Faulty measurement tools (poorly worded items, unreliable scales) give wrong data whatever the sample size, and would do so in a census, so they are a classic non-sampling error.", "b": "Defects in data collection (interviewer bias, wrong instructions, careless recording) arise in the field, not from studying only part of the population, so they are non-sampling errors.", "c": "Non-response is a non-sampling error: those who do not reply differ from those who do, and enlarging the sample does not remove the bias, as the 1936 Literary Digest poll showed."}}, {"q": "A researcher divides the population into certain groups and fixes the size of the sample from each group. It is called", "concept": "The answer is b: dividing the population into groups and fixing how many people will be taken from each group is quota sampling. Quota sampling is a non-probability method. The researcher first decides which characteristics matter (sex, age, locality, occupation) and how many people of each kind are needed, usually in proportion to the population, say 60 women and 40 men. Interviewers then fill each quota with whoever they can find, so the choice within each group is left to the interviewer, not to chance. It is quick, cheap and needs no sampling frame, which is why market researchers and opinion pollsters use it, but bias enters at the point of choice and sampling error cannot be calculated. It is often described as a non-random version of stratified sampling. Stratified random sampling also divides the population into strata, but then selects people randomly within each stratum. Example: Riya needs 50 college students and 50 working adults for a survey on screen time; she stands outside a mall and stops passers-by until each quota of 50 is full. Option b is right because fixing the number to be drawn from each group is the defining feature of quota sampling. Exam trap: stratified sampling also involves groups and a sample size per group, so look for the word 'random'; without random selection within groups, the answer is quota. Key note: as far as it goes, the stem also fits stratified sampling, since a stratified sample also fixes a size for each stratum; in the research methods texts this wording comes from, fixing the number to be taken from each group is the defining feature of quota sampling, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Stratified random sampling also divides the population into strata, but its defining step is random selection within each stratum, which the stem does not mention.", "c": "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole natural groups (schools, villages) and studies all or some units inside them; it does not fix a sample size for every group of the population.", "d": "The three methods differ in how people are chosen (non-randomly within quotas, randomly within strata, whole groups at random), so they cannot all be the single procedure the stem describes."}}, {"q": "The cardinal DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation is based primarily on an IQ score:", "concept": "The answer is b: the DSM-IV-TR intellectual criterion for mental retardation was an IQ about two standard deviations below the mean. Intelligence tests such as David Wechsler's scales (from 1939) are standardised to a mean of 100 and a standard deviation (SD) of 15, so two SD below the mean is 100 minus 30, an IQ of 70. DSM-IV-TR (2000) required significantly subaverage intellectual functioning, an IQ of about 70 or below on an individually given test, together with deficits in adaptive functioning (communication, self-care, social and practical skills) and onset before age 18. Allowing for measurement error, scores up to about 75 could qualify when adaptive deficits were clear. On the normal curve only about 2.3 percent of people score below 70. Severity was graded by IQ: mild 50 to 55 up to about 70 (about 85 percent of cases), moderate 35 to 40 up to 50 to 55, severe 20 to 25 up to 35 to 40, and profound below 20 to 25. DSM-5 later renamed the condition intellectual disability and graded severity by adaptive functioning instead. Example: Kabir, aged 9, scores 68 on the WISC and needs help with dressing and handling money; he meets both the IQ and the adaptive criteria. Option b is right because 70 lies exactly two SD below the mean of 100. Exam trap: one SD below the mean (85) marks the upper edge of borderline intellectual functioning (71 to 84), not the cut-off for mental retardation.", "traps": {"a": "One SD below the mean is an IQ of 85, which lies in the low-average range; about 16 percent of people score below it, far too many for a diagnosis meant for about 2 percent.", "c": "Three SD below the mean is an IQ of 55, which lies around the boundary between mild and moderate retardation, not at the overall diagnostic cut-off.", "d": "Four SD below the mean is an IQ of 40, which falls in the moderate to severe range; as a cut-off it would exclude every mild case, about 85 percent of all cases."}}, {"q": "Validity of a research can be improved by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the validity of a research study improves both by taking a truly representative sample and by eliminating extraneous factors. Validity here belongs to a study, not to a test. Donald Campbell (1957) first set internal validity against external validity, and Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) listed the threats to each. Internal validity asks whether the independent variable really caused the change in the dependent variable. It is threatened by extraneous variables such as history, maturation, testing effects, selection differences between groups and experimenter bias, and it improves when these are removed or controlled through random assignment, constant conditions, blinding and control groups. External validity asks whether the findings generalise to other people, settings and times. It improves when the sample truly represents the population, ideally through random selection, and when the setting resembles real life. A study needs both: a tightly controlled study on an odd sample says little about anyone else, and a representative sample in an uncontrolled study cannot show cause. Example: Meera tests a new memory training programme; random assignment and a quiet, standard testing room protect internal validity, while drawing students at random from several colleges protects external validity. Option c is right because a raises external validity and b raises internal validity, so both measures improve the study's validity. Exam trap: random selection (who enters the study) serves external validity, while random assignment (who gets which condition) serves internal validity; examiners often swap the two.", "traps": {"a": "A truly representative sample raises external validity (generalisation), but it does nothing about extraneous variables that threaten internal validity, so it is only half the answer.", "b": "Eliminating extraneous factors raises internal validity (confidence that the independent variable caused the change), but it does not make findings generalise, so it is only half the answer.", "d": "Both listed measures are standard ways of strengthening a study's validity, so 'none of these' is wrong."}}, {"q": "Which of the following are not Factor Analytically derived personality inventories? 1. 16 PF 2. Comrey Personality Scales 3. Jenkins Activity Survey 4. Edwards Personal Preference Schedule", "concept": "The answer is b: the Jenkins Activity Survey (3) and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (4) are not factor-analytically derived. Personality inventories are built in four main ways. The logical-content strategy writes items whose content obviously fits the trait; the theoretical strategy writes items for the constructs of a theory; the empirical criterion-group strategy keeps whatever items separate a criterion group from controls; and the factor-analytic strategy correlates many items, keeps those that load on each factor and names the factors afterwards. Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (1949) came from factor analyses of trait terms, and Andrew Comrey's Comrey Personality Scales (1970; 180 items, eight factors such as trust and orderliness) were built the same way, so 1 and 2 are factor-analytic. The Jenkins Activity Survey of C. David Jenkins, Stephen Zyzanski and Ray Rosenman measures the Type A behaviour pattern; its Type A scale items were chosen because they agreed with Structured Interview classifications, a criterion approach. Allen Edwards's Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (revised edition, 1959) is theory-based: it measures 15 needs from Henry Murray's (1938) list, using forced-choice pairs matched for social desirability. Example: Arjun gives 300 new items to 1,000 students and keeps only the clusters that emerge from factor analysis; that is Cattell's and Comrey's route, not Edwards's. Option b is right because 3 and 4 are the only two not built by factor analysis. Exam trap: the EPPS controls social desirability so carefully that it looks statistical, but its items were written for Murray's need theory. Key note: the Jenkins Activity Survey also reports subscales (speed and impatience, job involvement, hard-driving competitiveness) found by factor analysis, but its main Type A scale was built to match interview classifications, so it is not classed as a factor-analytic inventory; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "1 and 2 are exactly the two inventories that are factor-analytic (Cattell's 16PF and Comrey's scales), so this option names the opposite of what the stem asks.", "c": "1 and 2 are factor-analytic, so they cannot be on a 'not factor-analytic' list; only 3 in this set is correct, and 4 is left out.", "d": "2, the Comrey Personality Scales (Andrew Comrey, 1970), was derived by factor analysis, so it does not belong; 3 and 4 are correct."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Task) with List-II (Type of memory): a. Free recall and recognition, b. Sentence verification task, c. Priming, d. Scanning task. 1. Semantic memory, 2. Sensory memory, 3. Episodic memory, 4. Implicit memory", "concept": "The answer is d: free recall and recognition test episodic memory (3), sentence verification tests semantic memory (1), priming tests implicit memory (4) and the scanning task, by elimination, sensory memory (2). Endel Tulving (1972) split long-term memory into episodic memory, personal events tied to a time and place, and semantic memory, general knowledge of facts and concepts. Free recall (write down every word from the list you just heard) and recognition (which of these words were on the list?) ask about one specific study episode, so they are episodic tasks. In a sentence verification task the person judges sentences such as 'A canary is a bird' as true or false as fast as possible; Allan Collins and Ross Quillian (1969) used the reaction times to map the network of stored facts, so the task taps semantic memory. Priming is an influence of earlier exposure without conscious recollection: having seen BUTTER, a person completes BUT___ as BUTTER more often, even without remembering the word; Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) called this implicit memory. Scanning a briefly flashed display, as in George Sperling's (1960) partial report, taps the brief iconic store of sensory memory. Example: Riya recognises yesterday's list words (episodic), confirms that a sparrow is a bird (semantic) and fills BUT___ with BUTTER without knowing why (implicit). Option d is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: recall and recognition feel like knowledge tests, so students send them to semantic memory; they test memory for an episode. Key note: Saul Sternberg's (1966) memory scanning task is really a short-term memory task, while scanning a briefly flashed display, as in Sperling's partial report, taps sensory (iconic) memory; with the other three pairs fixed, d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Every pairing is wrong: a-4 sends recall and recognition to implicit memory though both need conscious remembering, b-2 makes sentence verification sensory, c-1 makes priming semantic and d-3 makes scanning episodic.", "b": "b-1 (sentence verification as semantic) is right, but a-2 calls recall and recognition sensory, c-3 makes priming episodic though it works without recollection, and d-4 makes scanning implicit.", "c": "Every pairing is wrong: recall and recognition are episodic (not semantic), sentence verification is semantic (not implicit), priming is implicit (not sensory) and scanning is sensory (not episodic)."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): AutoShaping involves S-S learning. Reason (R): Some behaviours are innate responses to a stimulus and because of temporal contiguity between them, they acquire stimulus related properties.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and R explains A, because the lit key comes to stand for food. Assertion: Paul Brown and Herbert Jenkins (1968) lit a response key for a few seconds before giving hungry pigeons food, and the food came whether or not the bird did anything. Yet the pigeons soon began pecking the lit key, as if the response had been shaped by hand, hence 'autoshaping'. David Williams and Harriet Williams (1969) then used omission training: a peck at the lit key cancelled the food. The pigeons kept pecking on many trials and so lost food they could have had. A response that persists when it costs the reward is not operant; it is driven by the pairing of key light and food, which is Pavlovian stimulus-stimulus (S-S) learning. So A is true. Reason: pecking is the pigeon's innate, unconditioned response to food. Because the lit key repeatedly comes just before food (temporal contiguity), the light takes on some of food's properties and draws the same response, the idea of stimulus substitution. Herbert Jenkins and Bruce Moore (1973) showed this neatly: pigeons pecked a key signalling food with an open-beaked, eating-like peck and a key signalling water with a slower, drinking-like peck. So R is true. Example: Kabir's pet pigeon starts pecking the small light on its feeder that glows just before seeds drop, though the seeds drop anyway. Option a is right because R supplies the mechanism of the S-S learning that A names. Exam trap: a key peck looks like a skeletal, voluntary operant response, so students mark A false; the omission result shows it is classically conditioned.", "traps": {"b": "Both statements are true, but R is not a side fact: the contiguity between key light and food, which lets the light take on food-like properties, is exactly what makes autoshaping S-S learning, so R does explain A.", "c": "R is true: pecking is the pigeon's innate response to food, and the lit key acquires food-like properties through pairing, as the eating-like and drinking-like pecks in Jenkins and Moore's (1973) study show.", "d": "A is true: omission training shows that pigeons keep pecking even when pecking cancels the food, so the response is driven by the light-food (S-S) pairing, not by its consequences."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?", "concept": "The answer is d: concurrent validity means that scores on a test correlate highly with scores on an accepted measure of the same attribute taken at about the same time. This item repeats Q24 of this paper with the options reordered. Criterion-related validity is the correlation between a test and an independent, external measure of the attribute, the criterion, such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis, an established gold-standard scale, school marks or job ratings. Its two types differ only in timing. In concurrent validity, test and criterion are measured at about the same time, so the test is checked against present status. In predictive validity, the criterion is gathered later, as when entrance scores are correlated with first-year marks. The classic concurrent design gives a new test together with an established one: a new 20-item depression questionnaire that correlates 0.85 with a gold-standard scale completed the same day shows concurrent validity. The correlation is called the validity coefficient. Example: Riya builds a 10-minute reading screener and gives it to 200 children on the same day as a full, well-established reading battery; a high correlation between the two shows concurrent validity. Option d is right because it is the only option that contains the essential idea, a high correlation with an accepted measure of the same attribute. Exam trap: the word 'concurrent' tempts students to pick 'two tests are done at the same time', but timing alone is not validity. Key note: strictly, correlating with tests of the same attribute blends concurrent and convergent validity, and option b is a half-truth (the timing) with nothing about correlation; d is the best of the four and stands.", "traps": {"a": "Items consistently relating to each other is internal consistency reliability (split-half, KR-20, Cronbach's alpha), a property of the test's own items, not validity against a criterion.", "b": "Giving two tests at the same time is only the timing half of concurrent validity; without a high correlation between the test and an accepted criterion it shows nothing about validity.", "c": "Two or more clinicians agreeing on the outcome is inter-rater (inter-scorer) reliability, measured by agreement indices such as kappa, not concurrent validity."}}, {"q": "Trait theorists use: 1. 16 PF - NEO-PI - MBTI 2. MMPI - EPQ - Rorschach test 3. TAT - MBTI - 16 PF 4. EPQ - MMPI - NEO-PI", "concept": "The answer is a: sets 1 (16PF, NEO-PI, MBTI) and 4 (EPQ, MMPI, NEO-PI) are the tools trait theorists use. Trait theorists describe personality as stable, measurable dimensions on which everyone can be placed, and they measure them with structured self-report inventories scored against norms. Raymond Cattell's 16PF (1949) measures 16 source traits; Paul Costa and Robert McCrae's NEO Personality Inventory (1985) measures the Big Five; Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck's Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975) measures psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism. The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator of Isabel Briggs Myers and Katharine Briggs, based on Carl Jung's (1921) types, sorts people into types but rests on self-report scales underneath; the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943) is an empirically keyed clinical inventory that yields scale scores. Sets 2 and 3, by contrast, contain projective techniques: the Rorschach inkblots (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935), which rest on psychodynamic assumptions and are scored by interpretation; trait researchers such as Eysenck dismissed them as unreliable. Example: Kabir, studying stress at work from a trait view, gives his staff the NEO-PI and the EPQ, not inkblots. Option a is right because 1 and 4 are the only sets made up entirely of self-report inventories. Exam trap: one projective test spoils a set, so check each set for the Rorschach or TAT first. Key note: sets 1 and 4 each carry one non-trait tool (MBTI, MMPI), but both are self-report inventories with trait-style scores, while 2 and 3 include projective tests that trait theorists reject; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Set 2 includes the Rorschach, a projective technique scored by interpretation, which trait theorists do not use; only 1 and 4 are correct.", "c": "Set 3 includes the TAT (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935), a projective storytelling test, so 3 is wrong even though set 1 is right.", "d": "Set 2 includes the Rorschach inkblot test, a projective method, so 2 cannot be correct; only set 4 in this option is right."}}, {"q": "Topographical organization: 1. Applies to sensory cortical areas 2. High degree of topographical organisation shows in the auditory pathway 3. Fibres in this pathway originate from the ganglion cells of the retina 4. Fibres then synapse at the lateral geniculate body of the hypothalamus", "concept": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 3 are correct. Topographical (topographic) organisation means that neighbouring points on a sensory surface are represented by neighbouring points in the brain, so each sensory cortex carries an orderly map: retinotopic in vision (the primary visual cortex maps the retina, with the small fovea given a disproportionately large share), tonotopic in hearing (each sound frequency has its own place in auditory cortex) and somatotopic in touch (the body map, or homunculus, of the postcentral gyrus). So statement 1, that it applies to sensory cortical areas, is true. Statement 3 describes the visual pathway: the fibres of the optic nerve are the axons of the retina's ganglion cells, which carry the signal out of the eye, so it is true. Statement 4 is false: optic tract fibres synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus (body) of the thalamus, not of the hypothalamus, before the optic radiations carry the signal to the striate cortex (area 17). The hypothalamus receives only a small retinal input, to its suprachiasmatic nucleus, for setting circadian rhythms. Example: a small stroke in one spot of Arjun's left primary visual cortex blinds one matching patch of his right visual field, because the map keeps retinal neighbours together. Option c is right because 1 and 3 are true and 4 is false. Exam trap: the lateral geniculate body sounds hypothalamic because both lie in the diencephalon, but the geniculate bodies are thalamic relays (lateral for vision, medial for hearing). Key note: statement 2 names the auditory pathway, which is tonotopic in reality, but statement 3 then says 'this pathway' starts in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot both describe the same pathway; the paired statements describe the visual pathway and c (1 and 3) is the only fully correct set; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "1 and 3 are right, but 4 is false: the lateral geniculate body belongs to the thalamus, not the hypothalamus.", "b": "2 places the pathway in hearing, yet 3 says 'this pathway' begins in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot describe the same pathway (hearing relays through the medial geniculate nucleus); this option also leaves out the true statement 1.", "d": "1 is true, but 4 is false because the lateral geniculate body is a thalamic relay nucleus, not part of the hypothalamus."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Book authored) with List-II (Psychologist): a. The Meaning of Anxiety, b. Man's Search for Meaning, c. On Becoming a Person, d. A Theory of Human Motivation. 1. Abraham Maslow, 2. Rollo May, 3. Victor Frankl, 4. Carl Rogers", "concept": "The answer is a: The Meaning of Anxiety is by Rollo May (2), Man's Search for Meaning by Viktor Frankl (3), On Becoming a Person by Carl Rogers (4) and 'A Theory of Human Motivation' by Abraham Maslow (1). Rollo May (1950), in The Meaning of Anxiety, separated normal anxiety, which fits the real threat and can be faced constructively, from neurotic anxiety, which is out of proportion to the threat and leads to repression; May did much to bring European existential thought into American psychology. Viktor Frankl wrote Man's Search for Meaning (first published in German in 1946) after surviving the Nazi concentration camps; it argues that the will to meaning is the primary human drive and sets out his logotherapy. Carl Rogers's On Becoming a Person (1961) collects his essays on the fully functioning person and on the conditions for growth in client-centred therapy: empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence. Abraham Maslow's 'A Theory of Human Motivation' (Psychological Review, 1943) is the paper that introduced the hierarchy of needs, rising from physiological needs through safety, love and esteem to self-actualisation. Example: Meera remembers the set by theme: May for anxiety, Frankl for meaning, Rogers for becoming, Maslow for motivation. Option a is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: 'meaning' appears in two titles, so May and Frankl are easily swapped; anxiety is May's subject, while meaning found even in suffering is Frankl's.", "traps": {"b": "a-3 gives The Meaning of Anxiety to Frankl, b-4 gives Man's Search for Meaning to Rogers and c-2 gives On Becoming a Person to May; only d-1 (Maslow) is right.", "c": "a-1 gives The Meaning of Anxiety to Maslow and d-2 gives Maslow's 1943 motivation paper to May; b-3 and c-4 are right.", "d": "a-4 gives The Meaning of Anxiety to Rogers, b-2 gives Frankl's book to May and c-3 gives On Becoming a Person to Frankl; only d-1 is right."}}, {"q": "Put the parts of the brain in sequence, going from higher to lower level of brain: 1. Metencephalon 2. Mesencephalon 3. Diancephalon 4. Telencephalon 5. Myelencephalon", "concept": "The answer is c: from higher to lower the order is telencephalon (4), diencephalon (3), mesencephalon (2), metencephalon (1) and myelencephalon (5). The names come from embryology. Early in development the front end of the neural tube forms three swellings: the forebrain (prosencephalon), the midbrain (mesencephalon) and the hindbrain (rhombencephalon). Before birth the forebrain divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon, and the hindbrain into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon, giving five divisions stacked from the top of the brain down to the spinal cord. The telencephalon becomes the cerebral hemispheres: cortex, basal ganglia, limbic structures and corpus callosum. The diencephalon ('between-brain') becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain, with its tectum (the superior and inferior colliculi) and tegmentum. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon ('marrow-brain') becomes the medulla oblongata, which joins the spinal cord. Example: Arjun remembers the order with a simple rule: telencephalon on top, then the other four in alphabetical order (di-, mes-, met-, myel-). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that runs from the cerebral hemispheres down to the medulla. Exam trap: metencephalon and mesencephalon differ by one letter; mes- is the midbrain, and met- is the pons and cerebellum below it. Option d keeps the middle of the order but drops the telencephalon to the bottom.", "traps": {"a": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5 simply follows the list as printed: it starts with the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and puts the telencephalon fourth, which is not a top-to-bottom order.", "b": "2, 4, 1, 3, 5 starts with the midbrain, places the cerebral hemispheres second and puts the diencephalon below the pons, scrambling the order.", "d": "3, 2, 1, 5, 4 has the middle stretch right (diencephalon, midbrain, pons and cerebellum, medulla) but puts the telencephalon, the highest division, at the bottom."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Working memory can hold large amount of information even though it can retain only seven to nine items. Reason (R): Items in working memory contain several separate bits of information grouped together called chunks.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and chunking (R) explains how a store of about seven items can hold a large amount of information (A). Assertion: George Miller (1956), in 'The magical number seven, plus or minus two', showed that the span of immediate memory is about seven units, give or take two. The surprise was that the limit is set in units, not in amounts of information: people hold about seven digits, or seven letters, or seven words, though seven words carry far more information than seven digits. So a small store can hold a lot. A is true. Reason: the units are chunks, separate bits bound into one meaningful group by knowledge already in long-term memory. I N D I A I I T I S R O is twelve letters but only three chunks (INDIA, IIT, ISRO). Recoding into bigger chunks is how chess masters remember whole board positions at a glance. R is true. R explains A: because capacity is counted in chunks and each chunk can pack many bits, the amount held can be large. Example: Riya cannot repeat ten random digits heard once, but she holds her friend's mobile number easily as three chunks, 984 621 7305, while she dials. Option a is right because R gives the very mechanism that makes A possible. Exam trap: 'seven to nine items' and 'large amount of information' seem to clash, so students mark A false; the clash disappears once items are understood as chunks. Key note: the span is usually quoted as 5 to 9 (seven plus or minus two), later estimates such as Nelson Cowan's (2001) put it nearer 4 chunks, and Miller wrote of short-term (immediate) memory rather than working memory; the A-R logic holds, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Both are true, but R is not an unrelated fact: chunking is exactly why a store limited to about seven items can still hold a large amount of information, so R does explain A.", "c": "R is true: George Miller (1956) described the items held in short-term memory as chunks, each made of several bits grouped into one meaningful unit.", "d": "A is true: the limit is about seven units whatever their size, so by holding large chunks the store can carry a large amount of information."}}, {"q": "The F-test:", "concept": "The answer is c: the F-test can be one-tailed or two-tailed, depending on the hypothesis being tested. F is a ratio of two variance estimates; it grew out of Ronald Fisher's analysis of variance work in the 1920s and was named F in his honour by George Snedecor (1934). Because it is a ratio of variances, the F distribution is never negative and is positively skewed, its shape set by two degrees of freedom (numerator and denominator). Which tails matter depends on the alternative hypothesis (H1). When the variances of two groups are compared, H0 says they are equal. If H1 says only that they differ, a ratio far above 1 or far below 1 counts against H0, so the test is two-tailed; in practice the larger variance is put on top and the critical value for alpha divided by 2 is used. If H1 says that one named variance is larger, only large ratios count, so the test is one-tailed. In ANOVA, F is the mean square between groups over the mean square within groups, and only a large F counts against H0, so the ANOVA F-test is right-tailed only. Example: Kabir asks whether two teaching methods differ in how spread out exam marks are, without predicting which; his F-test of the two variances is two-tailed. Option c is right because the number of tails follows from H1, not from the statistic. Exam trap: ANOVA is so often taught as the only use of F that students carry its right-tailed test over to every F-test. Key note: many psychology texts call the ANOVA F 'essentially one-tailed', which tempts b; the question asks about the F-test in general, and the variance-ratio test can be one- or two-tailed depending on the hypothesis, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A two-tailed F-test fits only a non-directional comparison of two variances; a directional H1, and the ANOVA F, use one tail, so F is not essentially two-tailed.", "b": "The ANOVA F is right-tailed only, but the F-test comparing two variances is two-tailed when H1 simply says they differ, so 'essentially one-tailed' overgeneralises.", "d": "'Can never be one-tailed' is the reverse of the truth: the ANOVA F-test, the commonest F-test in psychology, uses only the upper tail."}}, {"q": "Which option is not associated with a quantitative approach?", "concept": "The answer is d: 'narrative' is not associated with the quantitative approach; it belongs to qualitative research. Quantitative research measures variables as numbers and tests hypotheses statistically. It rests on positivism and postpositivism, reasons deductively from theory to hypothesis, and fixes its design before any data are collected: the sample, the instruments, the procedure and the analysis plan are all set in advance and then followed exactly. That is why it is described as structured (standardised tools and procedures), predetermined (questions and hypotheses fixed beforehand) and rigid or fixed (the design is not changed once data collection starts). Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and process through words, images and observation. Its designs are flexible and emergent, the researcher is the instrument, and its data are often stories. Narrative research, one of the five qualitative approaches John Creswell (2007) set beside phenomenology, grounded theory, ethnography and case study, collects and interprets the life stories people tell. Example: Meera's quantitative study gives a fixed 20-item anxiety scale to 300 nurses and runs a t-test; her colleague's narrative study asks six nurses to tell the story of their first night shift and analyses those stories. Option d is right because narrative data and narrative inquiry are qualitative, neither numeric nor fixed in advance. Exam trap: 'rigid' sounds like a criticism, so students suspect it is the odd one out, but textbooks use it plainly to describe quantitative designs.", "traps": {"a": "Rigid (fixed) is a standard description of quantitative designs, which are set before data collection and not changed midway, so it is associated with the approach.", "b": "Structured describes quantitative research well: standardised instruments, fixed procedures and closed questions.", "c": "Predetermined fits quantitative research: hypotheses, variables, sample and analysis are decided in advance from theory."}}, {"q": "What is one type of mixed method design?", "concept": "The answer is b: the convergent design is one of the core mixed methods designs. Mixed methods research collects and analyses both quantitative and qualitative data in one study and integrates them, on the pragmatist view that the research question should choose the methods. John Creswell and Vicki Plano Clark wrote Designing and Conducting Mixed Methods Research (2007; later editions 2011 and 2018), and their 2018 edition names three core designs. In the convergent (parallel) design, both kinds of data are collected at about the same time, analysed separately and then merged to see whether they agree; their first edition called it the triangulation design. In the explanatory sequential design, a quantitative phase comes first and a qualitative phase follows to explain its results. In the exploratory sequential design, a qualitative phase comes first to explore a topic, and a quantitative phase follows, for example to build and test an instrument. Their earlier editions also listed embedded, transformative and multiphase designs, which build on these three. Example: Kabir surveys 400 hostel students on loneliness and, in the same month, interviews 15 of them; he then sets the survey statistics beside the interview themes to see whether they converge. Option b is right because convergent is the name of an actual mixed methods design. Exam trap: 'exploring' and 'connecting' sound like design names because they echo real terms (the exploratory sequential design, and connecting as one way of integrating data), but neither is itself a design. Key note: LBS's first key for this paper (29 Nov 2021) marked b, but its revised key (6 Dec 2021) changed it to a, exploring. The exploratory sequential design is real, so a can be argued, but 'convergent' is the exact name of one of Creswell and Plano Clark's three core designs and 'exploring' is not, so b stays.", "traps": {"a": "'Exploring' is a near-miss for the exploratory sequential design (qualitative first, then quantitative); the bare word is not the name of a design.", "c": "'Explicit' is not a mixed methods design in any standard scheme; it sounds technical but names nothing.", "d": "Connecting is one way of integrating the two strands (beside merging and embedding), where the results of one phase shape the next; it is an integration strategy, not a design."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a non-parametric test?", "concept": "The answer is c: the median test is a non-parametric test. A parametric test estimates population parameters such as means and variances and assumes that scores come from a normally distributed population, that group variances are roughly equal, that observations are independent and that the dependent variable is on an interval or ratio scale. A non-parametric (distribution-free) test makes few assumptions about the population's shape and works on ranks, signs or frequency counts; Sidney Siegel (1956) made such tests popular in psychology. The median test, usually credited to Alexander Mood (1950), finds the combined median of all scores from two or more independent groups, counts how many scores in each group fall above and below it, and tests the resulting table with chi-square. It needs only ordinal data and is unaffected by extreme scores. Example: Riya compares the time taken to fall asleep by students in three hostels; a few students take hours, so the data are badly skewed, and she uses the median test instead of comparing means. Option c is right because it is the only test listed that works on counts above and below a median rather than on means and variances. Exam trap: the critical ratio sounds unfamiliar and so seems non-parametric, but it is the large-sample z-test of a difference between means, which is parametric. Other non-parametric tests often listed beside the median test are the Mann-Whitney U, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Kruskal-Wallis and Friedman tests.", "traps": {"a": "Analysis of covariance (Ronald Fisher) is ANOVA plus a measured covariate; it compares adjusted means and assumes normality, equal variances and equal regression slopes, so it is parametric.", "b": "Analysis of variance (Ronald Fisher, 1925) compares group means with the F ratio and assumes normality and equal variances; its non-parametric partner is the Kruskal-Wallis test.", "d": "The critical ratio is the difference between two means divided by the standard error of that difference, read against the normal curve; it is a parametric z-test."}}, {"q": "The Kruskal Wallis test is the non parametric alternative to the:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric alternative to the one-way (independent) ANOVA. William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952) designed it for three or more independent groups measured on one dependent variable, when the data are ordinal or clearly break ANOVA's assumptions of normality and equal variances. All the scores are pooled and ranked from lowest to highest, and the ranks are then added up within each group. If the groups do not differ, their mean ranks should be similar; the statistic H measures how far the mean ranks spread apart and is read against chi-square with k minus 1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of groups. That is why it is called the one-way ANOVA by ranks. Like ANOVA, a significant H shows only that at least one group differs, so follow-up comparisons are needed to find which. Example: Arjun compares pain ratings on a 0 to 10 scale from patients in three wards; the samples are small and skewed, so he ranks all the ratings together and runs a Kruskal-Wallis test. Option b is right because both tests compare three or more independent groups on one factor. Exam trap: the t-test also has rank-based partners, but they are different tests: the Mann-Whitney U for two independent groups and the Wilcoxon signed-rank test for two related samples. For three or more related conditions, the rank test is Friedman's.", "traps": {"a": "Factorial designs have two or more independent variables at once; Kruskal-Wallis handles only one factor, so it is not their alternative.", "c": "The t-test compares only two groups; its non-parametric alternatives are the Mann-Whitney U test (independent groups) and the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (related samples).", "d": "There is a standard parametric partner, the one-way ANOVA, so 'none of the above' is wrong."}}, {"q": "A researcher measured the same group of people's physiological reactions while watching horror films and compared them to when watching erotic films and a documentary about wild life. The resulting data were skewed. What test should be used to analyse the data?", "concept": "The answer is c: the same people were measured under three conditions and the data were skewed, so Friedman's ANOVA is the right test. Choosing a test starts with two questions: how many conditions are compared, and are they independent groups or repeated measures? Here one group watched horror films, erotic films and a wildlife documentary, so there are three related conditions, a repeated-measures design. If the scores were normal, a repeated-measures ANOVA would fit, but it assumes normally distributed scores (and sphericity), and these data are skewed. The non-parametric answer is the Friedman test, devised by the economist Milton Friedman (1937). It ranks each person's scores across the conditions (1, 2, 3), then sums the ranks for each condition; if the conditions do not differ, the rank sums should be similar. The statistic is read against chi-square with k minus 1 degrees of freedom, which is why it is also called the two-way ANOVA by ranks. Example: Meera records heart rate in 12 students during each type of film; one student's heart rate soars during horror, skewing the data, so she ranks the three readings within each student and runs Friedman's ANOVA. Option c is right because it is the rank-based test for three or more related conditions. Exam trap: Kruskal-Wallis is the best-known non-parametric 'ANOVA', but it is for independent groups; the deciding phrase in the stem is 'the same group of people'.", "traps": {"a": "Independent ANOVA is for different people in each group and assumes normal data; here the same people were measured in every condition, and the data are skewed.", "b": "Repeated-measures ANOVA fits the design (same people, three conditions) but is parametric, assuming normal scores and sphericity, which skewed data violate.", "d": "Kruskal-Wallis is a rank test for three or more independent groups; it ignores the pairing of scores within each person, so it is the wrong design here."}}]});
